	<Folder>
		<name>A. Production</name>
		<Folder>
			<name>Oil Fields</name>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Bolívar Coastal Fields (Tía Juana–Lagunillas–Bachaquero)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational — deep legacy decline
Crude grade: Medium-heavy blends | API: ~9-22° (varies sharply by sub-field) | Sulfur: medium-high sour
Operator: PDVSA (nationalised 1976; originally developed by Standard Oil/Creole Petroleum, Shell and Gulf)
Role: THE largest oil field in South America and the largest outside the Middle East — 6,000-7,000 wells strung along 35 miles of Lake Maracaibo&apos;s eastern shore. Comprises seven named sub-fields: Tía Juana, Lagunillas, Bachaquero, Ceuta, Motatán, Barua and Ambrosio
History: Discovered 1914, production from 1922 — this single complex built modern Venezuela&apos;s oil industry and made it the world&apos;s top exporter through the mid-20th century
Extraction note: Tía Juana crude alone runs 9-11 API, so heavy that Cyclic Steam Stimulation and later Steam-Assisted Gravity Drainage (from 1987) were needed just to produce it economically
Export route: Gathered at the Ulé Tank Farm (Tía Juana) for the Ule-Amuay pipeline north to Paraguaná; separately, western lake crude and Boscán route via La Salina/Bajo Grande
Risks: A century of depletion; ageing lake-bed wells and platforms; the 1981 relocation of entire camp towns (Tía Juana, Lagunillas, Bachaquero) to drill beneath them shows how mature this basin already was decades ago</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>-71.55,10.2,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Orinoco Belt (Faja Petrolífera del Orinoco)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational — well below historical capacity
Crude grade: Raw extra-heavy crude (8-10 API, 4-6% sulfur) — becomes MEREY 16 once diluted with Mesa 30 light crude at Jose. Merey 16 is Venezuela&apos;s ONLY currently-traded Orinoco grade, and consistently the lowest-priced crude in the OPEC basket
Operator: PDVSA and joint-venture partners across four administrative districts (Boyacá, Junín, Ayacucho, Carabobo) — foreign partners have shifted heavily toward Russia and China as US and European majors scaled back under sanctions
Role: The world&apos;s largest single accumulation of hydrocarbons — ~600 km along the Orinoco River, an estimated 300+ billion barrels of certified recoverable reserves, roughly matching Saudi Arabia&apos;s
Why one entry: The four districts don&apos;t produce distinct traded grades of their own — the historical upgraded grades tied to them (Cerro Negro, Hamaca, Zuata Sweet, Morichal 16) have not shipped since 2017-2019, when sanctions and underinvestment shut the upgraders down. Merey, made by dilution rather than full upgrading, is the only stream that stayed in the market
Export route: Faja-Jose Crude &amp; Diluent Corridor (already on this map) north to the Jose complex, where it is blended into Merey 16, stored, and exported
Risks: Extreme viscosity requiring constant dilution; chronic underinvestment; production levels highly sensitive to US sanctions policy and OFAC licence status</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>-64.8,9,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Caño Limón Field (Arauca)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Caño Limón | API: 29.5° | Sulfur: low (light sweet)
Operator: SierraCol Energy (successor to Occidental Petroleum&apos;s Colombian assets), with Ecopetrol as partner under the original 20-year association contract
Role: A supergiant discovery (1983) in Arauca department on the Venezuelan border — historically credited with RESTORING Colombia&apos;s status as a net oil exporter. Developed with record speed: 40+ wells drilled to 2,740+ m depth within a few years
Field group: Part of the wider Caño Limón Area, including the Cravo Norte, Rondón, Chipirón and Cosecha contracts — three major production facilities, 430+ active wells, over 1.5 billion barrels produced cumulatively
Export route: The Caño Limón-Coveñas pipeline, entirely separate from the OCENSA/Cusiana system elsewhere on this map
Why it matters: Colombia&apos;s SECOND major export corridor, independent of the Casanare/Meta cluster — both systems happen to converge at the same terminal, Coveñas
Risks: One of the most heavily attacked pipelines in the world — hundreds of guerrilla/paramilitary attacks over four decades, with one 2015 shutdown lasting 67 consecutive days</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>-71.16794415090243,6.9324538085042,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Cusiana-Cupiagua Fields (Casanare)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational — mature, in steep decline
Crude grade: Cusiana/Cupiagua | API: ~34-42° | Sulfur: low (light sweet)
Operator: Ecopetrol (98%), with Equión Energía and Emerald Energy — Ecopetrol took full operatorship in 2016 after BP and successor Equión ran the fields for decades
Role: Colombia&apos;s historic flagship discovery, in the Llanos foothills near Tauramena and Aguazul — Cusiana was Colombia&apos;s LARGEST oilfield through the 1990s, peaking above 300,000 b/d in 1998, over a third of national output at the time. Cupiagua, next door, was developed jointly to share infrastructure
Why here: This is the ORIGIN of the OCENSA pipeline — OCENSA was built specifically to move Cusiana/Cupiagua crude to the coast, and the pipeline&apos;s own route starts at this exact location
Export route: OCENSA pipeline north via Vasconia to Coveñas
Risks: Steep multi-decade decline from 1990s peak; now a fraction of its former output, though still the anchor that gives OCENSA its name and reason for being</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>-72.71003282272821,5.002821002623989,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Lote 192 Field (formerly 1AB, Andoas)</name>
				<description>Status: Largely idle — reactivation in progress under new operator
Crude grade: Medium, light and heavy crude mix | precise assay varies by well cluster
Operator: Petroperu (took over 2023, 30-year licence), with Altamesa Energy (Canada) as strategic partner
Role: Peru&apos;s largest and historically most important oil block — the Pastaza, Corrientes, Marañón and Tigre river basins in Loreto, near the Ecuador border. First developed by Occidental Petroleum (OXY) in 1971; 737 million barrels produced cumulatively, ~127 million barrels of proven reserves remaining
Capacity: ~13,000 b/d when fully operating — far below its historical output after years of shutdowns
Why it matters: Also holds an unwanted record — more oil-contamination incidents than any other Peruvian block in the last 20 years, which is central to why local communities have repeatedly blocked its reactivation and demanded a prior-consultation process
Export route: Feeds the ONP via the Andoas gathering station
Risks: Chronic community opposition tied to its contamination history; reactivation dependent on Petroperu/Altamesa investment and continued indigenous consultation</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>-76.45688316701163,-2.805834176660972,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Lote 8 Field (Corrientes-Marañón, Trompeteros)</name>
				<description>Status: Idle — reactivation pending as of 2024
Crude grade: Medium crude (Yanayacu stream)
Operator: Upland Oil &amp; Gas (US), awarded a new 4-year contract in 2024; historically operated by Pluspetrol Norte
Role: A long-producing block in the Corrientes and Marañón river basins, Loreto — one of the two blocks (with Lote 192) whose shutdown left Lote 67 and Lote 95 as the country&apos;s only active jungle production for several years
Recent: Reactivation approved via prior consultation with 44 of the area&apos;s indigenous communities in 2024, though some resistance remained
Export route: Feeds the ONP via Station 1 at San José de Saramuro
Risks: Extended shutdown has eroded reservoir management and infrastructure condition; indigenous-consultation requirements for any restart</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>-75.05885236737453,-3.813252438244974,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Lote 67 Field (Loreto, near Ecuador border)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Medium crude
Operator: Perenco
Role: One of only two Peruvian jungle blocks that kept producing through the mid-2020s while Lotes 192 and 8 sat idle — restarted in 2021 after a year-long COVID/low-price shutdown, peaking around 17,500 b/d
Export route: Connects to the ONP via the Ramal Norte branch (Andoas–Station 5, 252 km)
Risks: Remote single-block dependence; the same pipeline-access constraints affecting the whole Loreto oil patch</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>-75.86530518042686,-2.313966403589508,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Lote 95 Field (Bretaña, PetroTal)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational — actively expanding
Crude grade: Medium crude
Operator: PetroTal (Canadian-listed)
Role: Currently PERU&apos;S LEADING jungle producer — while Lotes 192 and 8 were shut for years, Lote 95 was the country&apos;s most reliable Amazon source, averaging 7,100-13,700+ b/d and growing via a 4-well development campaign (including Bretaña Norte) targeting over 11,000 b/d
Why it matters: The clearest sign that Peru&apos;s jungle oil future depends on newer, better-managed operators rather than the historically contamination-plagued legacy blocks
Export route: Crude moves by river barge to the ONP system at Station 1, San José de Saramuro
Risks: Single-block concentration of the country&apos;s active jungle output; river-level seasonality affecting barge logistics</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>-74.32867789320353,-5.244988998370742,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Medanito Field / Neuquén Basin (Vaca Muerta)</name>
				<description><![CDATA[Status: Operational
Crude grade: Medanito | API: ~40.8-42.5° | Sulfur: ~0.15-0.21% (light sweet)
Benchmark: Platts publishes a dedicated Medanito FOB Argentina assessment, quoted against ICE Brent and WTI futures strips — one of the few Latin American grades with its own named benchmark
Operator: Not a single-company field — Medanito is a BLENDED stream ("brand") gathered from many Vaca Muerta operators (YPF, Vista, Shell and others) across the Neuquén Basin, homogenised into one export-grade name via the Oldelval system
Role: Argentina's dominant light sweet export stream and the counterpart to Escalante in the national export basket — the crude that has turned Vaca Muerta into a genuine international exporter
Quality note: Low sulfur, very low total acid number (<0.1), high paraffin content and a pour point near -36°C — a versatile crude yielding both gasoline and diesel well; consistency across the basin is what makes it tradeable as a single "brand" rather than dozens of small streams
Export route: Oldelval pipeline via Allen to Puerto Rosales
Recent: Flows to Europe rose sharply in 2022-23 as the grade traded at a deep Brent discount just as the EU banned Russian seaborne crude — first cargoes to the UK and Italy since at least 2016; also reached Peru's La Pampilla refinery for the first time in 20 years; increasingly combined with Escalante on shared Suezmax loadings
Risks: Oldelval pipeline capacity has repeatedly lagged booming Neuquén production; blend consistency is being tested as new shale sub-areas add crude of varying quality; quality-bank compensation disputes between large shale producers and smaller conventional operators sharing the same system]]></description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>-68.20000145569237,-37.99998107494769,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Badr El-Din (BED) Field Complex — Capricorn/Bapetco</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Contributes to Western Desert Blend | light sweet (~40° API, ~0.3% sulfur, blended)
Benchmark: Priced against Dated Brent within the Western Desert Blend stream
Operator: Bapetco (Badr El-Din Petroleum Company), the EGPC joint venture; Capricorn Energy (formerly Cairn) holds 50% working interest in the BED concessions, with Cheiron as operator of the producing concessions — acquired from Shell in 2021 for up to ~$926 million
Role: One of the three main producer groups behind Egypt&apos;s Western Desert Blend (with Khalda/Apache and GPC). BED is Capricorn&apos;s flagship oil area, in the Abu Gharadig Basin on the edge of the Qattara Depression, ~300 km west of Cairo and ~90 km south of Alamein — five producing concessions of both oil and gas, discovered 1982, with Badr-3 the flagship oil field
Capricorn&apos;s wider Egypt portfolio: also Obaiyed (Egypt&apos;s largest onshore gas field, 50% WI), North East Abu Gharadig / NEAG (26% WI), and Alam El Shawish West (20% WI); eight concessions consolidated into one integrated agreement approved May 2025
Production: Capricorn prioritises liquids in the BED area via water injection and infill drilling; Bapetco&apos;s total output has run ~50,000 b/d of crude/condensate plus large gas volumes
Export route: Into the Western Desert gathering system to the El-Hamra terminal (both on this map)
Why it matters: Puts a named, London-listed independent producer (Capricorn) on the map behind Western Desert Blend, showing who actually produces the grade rather than leaving it generic
Risks: A small producer within the blend; mature stacked reservoirs needing active waterflood management; Egyptian payment-arrears cycles to foreign partners</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>27.3,29.9,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Sangomar Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational — first oil June 2024
Crude grade: Sangomar | API: ~31° | Sulfur: ~1.0% (medium sour)
Benchmark: Priced against Dated Brent; Woodside describes Sangomar as similar in quality to Oman and Norway&apos;s Johan Sverdrup — both already on this map — placing it as a medium sour grade for European and Asian refiners
Operator: Woodside Energy 82% (operator), PETROSEN 18%, in the RSSD joint venture (Rufisque, Sangomar and Sangomar Deep Offshore)
Role: Senegal&apos;s FIRST oil field — discovered ~2014, first oil June 2024, making Senegal the world&apos;s newest oil producer that year; ~100 km offshore south of Dakar, a $4.9-5.2 billion Phase 1 development of 24 wells
Capacity: ~100,000 b/d nameplate
Market note: Its medium sour quality and ~1% sulfur make it a distinct barrel from West Africa&apos;s typical light sweet grades — closer to Middle East or North Sea sour crude; first cargoes went to Europe
Export route: Produced into and exported FOB the Léopold Sédar Senghor FPSO
Regional context: Senegal also has the BP-operated Greater Tortue Ahmeyim (GTA) LNG project on its border with Mauritania, making 2024 the year the country became both an oil and gas producer
Risks: A single-field debut producer; ramp-up and reservoir performance still being proven</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>-17.55,14.1,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Rio del Rey Basin Fields (Kole)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational — mature
Crude grade: Kole | API: ~30.7° | Sulfur: low (medium sweet)
Benchmark: Priced against Dated Brent, like most West African grades; Cameroon&apos;s main export stream, ~50,000-57,000 b/d, two to three cargoes a month, in 950,000-barrel parcels
Operator: A blend of fields operated by Perenco (which took over Total&apos;s assets in 2011), Shell/Pecten and state SNH, in the shallow-water Rio del Rey Basin off south-west Cameroon — the basin producing since 1977 and ~90% of national output
Role: Kole is Cameroon&apos;s flagship grade — the country became a producer in 1977 when the Kole field came on stream, peaking at 185,000 b/d in 1986 before long decline; Perenco has revived mature fields including the South Kole marine project (2023)
Related grades: Cameroon also exports heavier Lokele (~24° API) and lighter Ebome (~38° API), both smaller streams
Export route: To the offshore Kole FSO terminal for tanker loading; markets in Europe, the Mediterranean, the US and increasingly Asia
Risks: Mature basin in decline; small individual fields sustained by workovers and marginal-field redevelopment</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>8.6,4.55,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Agadem Rift Basin Fields (Koulélé)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational — exporting since May 2024
Crude grade: Meleck | API: 24.4° | Sulfur: 0.354% (medium-heavy, sweet)
Benchmark: Priced against Dated Brent; CNPC compares Meleck to Angola&apos;s heavy sweet Pazflor and Dalia grades, which sell mainly into China — the maiden 2024 cargoes went to the Mediterranean (France/Spain)
Operator: CNPC (57%), Niger government (25%), a unit of Taiwan&apos;s CPC (~18%); Savannah Energy holds Agadem PSCs covering ~half the basin
Role: The oil fields of the Agadem Rift Basin in remote south-east Niger — the source of Meleck, the grade that made landlocked Niger the world&apos;s newest crude exporter in May 2024
Capacity: Domestic production was ~20,000 b/d (refined locally) until the export pipeline quintupled potential output toward 90,000-110,000 b/d
Export route: The 1,950 km Niger-Benin pipeline to the Sème terminal on Benin&apos;s Atlantic coast
Risks: Extreme transit dependence; political instability after the July 2023 coup; militant attacks on the pipeline; CNPC executives expelled in 2025; Chinese buying has been intermittent</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>13.1,16.3,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Western Desert Fields (Khalda / Apache)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Western Desert Blend | API: ~41.1° | Sulfur: ~0.34% (light sweet, paraffinic)
Benchmark: Priced against Dated Brent; the highest-quality of Egypt&apos;s three grades, far lighter and sweeter than the medium-sour Suez and Belayim blends from the Gulf of Suez
Operator: Mainly Khalda Petroleum (EGPC / Apache joint venture), with other Western Desert independents; about half of output comes from Khalda/Apache
Role: A blend from the newer ONSHORE fields of Egypt&apos;s Western Desert (El Alamein, Abu Gharadig, Faghur Basin and others) — the region that revived Egyptian oil as the old Gulf of Suez fields declined
Quality note: Light, low-sulfur, paraffinic — a high-yield gasoline and middle-distillate feed; the El Alamein discovery (1966) first opened the region
Export route: Pipeline north to the El-Hamra terminal on the Mediterranean coast
Risks: Onshore fields of modest individual size; Egypt&apos;s overall crude output is declining and domestic demand now outpaces production</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>27.5,30,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Jubilee Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Jubilee | API: 37.4-37.6° | Sulfur: 0.3% (light sweet)
Benchmark: Priced against Dated Brent; a clean light sweet grade with very low metals (vanadium 1.9 ppm, nickel 3 ppm) and a very low TAN of 0.05, making it an easy, high-value refinery feed
Operator: Tullow Oil (operator, ~35%), with Kosmos Energy, Anadarko/Oxy, GNPC and PetroSA — straddling the Deepwater Tano and West Cape Three Points blocks
Role: Ghana&apos;s FIRST significant offshore field and the one that made the country an oil producer — discovered 2007 by Kosmos (Mahogany-1), on stream December 2010 just 41 months after discovery, an industry record for a deepwater project. ~60 km offshore in 1,000-1,800 m of water; gross reserves once ~490 million-3 billion bbl
Capacity: ~75,000-120,000 b/d, with the Jubilee South-East expansion lifting output past 100,000 b/d
Export route: Produced into and exported FOB the FPSO Kwame Nkrumah
Risks: Early sand-contamination flowline problems once cut output and government revenue; a maturing single-field dependence</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>2.85,-4.62,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Cabinda Field (Block 0)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational — mature
Crude grade: Cabinda | API: ~32° | Sulfur: ~0.12% (medium sweet, WAXY)
Benchmark: Priced against Dated Brent; historically Angola&apos;s flagship, once nearly 50% of national exports
Operator: Cabinda Gulf Oil Company (Chevron 39.2%), with Sonangol 41%, TotalEnergies 10%, Eni 9.8% — Block 0 extended to 2050
Role: Angola&apos;s original benchmark grade off the Cabinda enclave, discovered 1966; Block 0 holds 21 fields but now runs at a fraction of its 1980s peak
Quality note: HIGH WAX content restricts sales to warm seas or ports with heated tankage — but its high middle-distillate yield made it popular in Asia, and its low sulfur suited direct burning as boiler fuel by Japanese utilities
Blend use: Widely used to blend with higher- and lower-quality grades, so it suits both simple and complex refineries
Export route: The Malongo terminal system in Cabinda
Risks: Mature decline; wax-handling constraint</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>11.6,-5.6,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Girassol Field (Block 17)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Girassol | API: ~30.8° | Sulfur: ~0.34% (medium sweet)
Benchmark: Priced against Dated Brent; a widely-traded West African medium sweet marker, produced at the Girassol and Jasmin fields
Operator: TotalEnergies 38% (operator), Equinor 22.16%, ExxonMobil 19%, BP 15.84%, Sonangol 5% — Block 17
Role: The first of Block 17&apos;s four FPSO hubs (Girassol, Dalia, Pazflor, CLOV), on stream 2001 ~140 km off Luanda — the field that opened Angola&apos;s deepwater era
Export route: The Girassol CALM export buoy (already on this map) to tankers
Risks: Mature deepwater field; Block 17 in managed decline</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>11.4,-8.1,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Dalia Field (Block 17)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Dalia | API: ~23.7° | Sulfur: ~0.49% (medium, sweet — heavier end of Block 17)
Benchmark: Priced against Dated Brent at a discount to lighter Girassol
Operator: TotalEnergies-led Block 17 consortium
Role: The second Block 17 FPSO hub, on stream 2006 ~40 km from Girassol — a heavier, more viscous stream than its neighbours
Export route: Dedicated Dalia export buoy (route already on this map)
Risks: Heavier grade; mature deepwater decline</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>11.3,-8.300000000000001,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Pazflor Field (Block 17)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Pazflor | API: ~26-30° | Sulfur: medium (medium sweet)
Benchmark: Priced against Dated Brent
Operator: TotalEnergies-led Block 17 consortium
Role: The third Block 17 FPSO, on stream 2011 — the world&apos;s largest FPSO at launch (325 m, 1.9 million bbl storage), producing two oils of very different characteristics from four reservoirs, a major subsea engineering feat (49 subsea wells, 180 km of lines)
Export route: FPSO offloading via export buoy
Risks: Complex multi-reservoir production; deepwater decline</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>11.55,-8.35,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>CLOV Fields (Cravo-Lirio-Orquidea-Violeta, Block 17)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: CLOV | API: ~20-35° (two blended streams) | Sulfur: low-medium (medium sweet)
Benchmark: Priced against Dated Brent
Operator: TotalEnergies-led Block 17 consortium
Role: The fourth Block 17 FPSO, on stream 2014 ~140 km off Luanda — named for its four fields Cravo, Lirio, Orquidea, Violeta. Blends a light 32-35 API oil from Oligocene reservoirs (Cravo-Lirio) with a heavier 20-30 API Miocene oil (Orquidea-Violeta) into one commingled stream
Distinction: The CLOV FPSO is the only fully-electric FPSO on Block 17 and has not flared since start-up — a low-emissions showcase
Export route: FPSO offloading via export buoy
Risks: Two-reservoir blend management; deepwater decline</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>11.2,-8.199999999999999,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Hungo Fields (Kizomba A, Block 15)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Hungo | API: ~28.5° | Sulfur: ~0.71% (medium, semi-sweet — the sourest of the main Angolan grades)
Benchmark: Priced against Dated Brent; produced at the Hungo and Chocalho fields on Block 15
Operator: ExxonMobil 40% (operator), with BP, Eni, Equinor — Block 15
Role: The Kizomba A development stream, on stream 2004, later boosted by the Marimba North tie-in; Block 15 holds the giant Kizomba A/B/C developments
Quality note: At 0.71% sulfur, Hungo is notably more sour than Girassol, Cabinda or Dalia — closer to semi-sweet
Export route: FPSO offloading to tankers
Risks: Higher sulfur narrows its premium; mature deepwater decline</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>12.1,-9.1,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Plutonio Fields (Greater Plutonio, Block 18)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Plutonio | API: ~33° | Sulfur: low (medium, low-sulfur — similar to Girassol/Dalia)
Benchmark: Priced against Dated Brent
Operator: BP 50% (operator), with Sonangol — Block 18; BP&apos;s first operated asset in Angola
Role: The Greater Plutonio development, on stream October 2007, tied to a single FPSO draining five fields — BP&apos;s flagship Angolan stream, ~220,000 b/d at plateau
Export route: FPSO offloading to tankers
Risks: Mature deepwater decline; single-FPSO concentration</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>12.1,-8,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Saturno Field (Block 15/06)</name>
				<description>Status: Recent development — new deepwater project
Crude grade: Saturno | API: light sweet (~34-37°, part of Eni&apos;s Block 15/06 light oil cluster) | Sulfur: low
Benchmark: Priced against Dated Brent; a new addition to Eni&apos;s Block 15/06 output
Operator: Eni 36.84% (operator), Sonangol P&amp;P 36.84%, SSI Fifteen 26.32%
Role: One of Eni&apos;s newer Block 15/06 deepwater developments in the same high-quality light-oil cluster as Agogo, Kalimba, Afoxé and Ndungu — the block Eni has aggressively expanded since relaunching exploration in 2018
Note: A newer stream — assay figures approximate, pending confirmed public assay; grouped with the block&apos;s ~34-37 API light sweet oils
Export route: FPSO offloading (West Hub / N&apos;Goma system)
Risks: A young grade still building marketing history; figures provisional</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>12.4,-9.4,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Rabi-Kounga Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational — mature, in deep decline
Crude grade: Rabi Blend (Rabi Export Blend) | API: ~34-36° | Sulfur: low (mid-gravity, sweet)
Benchmark: Priced against Dated Brent; Rabi&apos;s low sulfur historically fetched ~$1.20/b more than other Gabonese crude
Operator: Shell Gabon (later Assala Energy / Carlyle, then acquired by Gabon&apos;s state), with Elf/Total historically
Role: Gabon&apos;s LARGEST field, discovered by Shell in 1985 in dense rainforest 140 km south of Port-Gentil — the discovery that drove Gabon&apos;s early-1990s boom and prompted it to leave OPEC in 1996. Estimated 850 million-1.3 billion bbl in place
Capacity: Peaked at ~217,000 b/d in 1997; declined to ~55,000 b/d by 2005 and lower since, despite gas-reinjection (Phase 3) work to shore up output
Blend note: Rabi Export Blend combines Rabi crude with Shell&apos;s heavier GAMBA crude (from the Gamba/Ivinga field, Gabon&apos;s oldest, onstream 1967); Total separately markets an unblended, slightly higher-quality Rabi Light
Export route: A 135 km pipeline to Shell&apos;s Gamba terminal on the coast
Risks: A mature field in long decline; a waxy crude needing minimum flow rates to prevent paraffin buildup in the pipeline; ownership has passed from Shell through Assala to the Gabonese state</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>9.880000000000001,-1.93,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Zafiro Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational — mature, declining
Crude grade: Zafiro | API: ~29.5-32° | Sulfur: low (medium sweet)
Benchmark: Sold at a differential to Dated Brent; markets in the US, Europe and Asia-Pacific, historically ~1 million bbl cargoes, five a month
Operator: ExxonMobil (operator), with Devon/Ocean Energy and the state (GEPetrol)
Role: Discovered March 1995 northwest of Bioko Island — the FIRST deepwater field brought on stream in West Africa and Equatorial Guinea&apos;s largest oil producer, peaking above 280,000 b/d in 2004; recoverable reserves once estimated over 400 million-1.1 billion bbl
Blend note: In 2005 Zafiro was merged with the adjacent Topacio field to form a ~29.5° API Zafiro blend (from an earlier 32° stream)
Technology: Produced via the converted Zafiro Producer FPSO (from a 270,000 dwt VLCC) and later the Serpentina FPSO, with the Jade platform — pioneering floating-production technology in West Africa
Export route: FPSO offloading via single-point mooring to export tankers
Risks: A mature field in long decline; ExxonMobil has been winding down its Equatorial Guinea position</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>8.35,3.85,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Alba Field (condensate)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Liquids grade: Alba Condensate | API: very light (condensate) | Sulfur: very low
Benchmark: Sold against Dated Brent and regional condensate assessments; feeds the country&apos;s LPG, methanol and LNG plants as much as the export market
Operator: Marathon Oil (52%), with Noble Energy and the state (GEPetrol)
Role: Equatorial Guinea&apos;s major CONDENSATE field, ~19 km north of Bioko Island — the country&apos;s oldest producing asset (natural gas liquids since the early 1990s), holding an estimated 400 million barrels of liquids; production grew from ~20,000 b/d to ~65,000 b/d of condensate
Integrated use: Alba is the feedstock backbone of the Punta Europa industrial complex on Bioko — condensate, LPG, a methanol plant and the EG LNG plant all run off this field&apos;s output, making it more of a gas-liquids hub than a pure crude stream
Export route: Piped ashore to the Punta Europa complex on Bioko Island for processing and export
Risks: Volumes follow gas production; a mature field underpinning the country&apos;s whole gas-processing economy</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>8.75,3.950000000000001,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Doba Basin Fields (Komé, Miandoum, Bolobo)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Doba Blend | API: ~25.8° (blended; base crude ~21°) | Sulfur: ~0.07-0.1% (heavy sweet, HIGHLY ACIDIC and very waxy)
Benchmark: Priced against Dated Brent — historically at a steep discount for its acidity, but Doba made history on 19 March 2019 by trading ABOVE Dated Brent, the first heavy crude to do so that year, on Chinese demand after US sanctions tightened alternative heavy supply
Operator: Esso Exploration &amp; Production Chad (ExxonMobil) 40%, Petronas 35%, Chevron 25% — Chad later took stakes via SHT
Role: Chad&apos;s flagship grade, from the Komé, Miandoum and Bolobo fields in the southern Doba Basin; ~100,000-180,000 b/d, and crude is 96% of Chad&apos;s exports
Two hard problems in one crude:
— ACIDITY: total acid number around 4.1-4.8 mg KOH/g (versus 0.09 for Brent), so it can only be run by refineries with corrosion-resistant metallurgy — inherently more expensive to process, the same constraint as Usan and Kikeh but far more extreme
— WAX: a very high pour point near 21°C, so ExxonMobil doses it with polymer pour-point depressants to keep it flowing — the same wax challenge as Daqing, Uzen and Bu Attifel
Blending note: The base ~21° API Komé/Miandoum crude is blended with lighter streams to reach a marketable ~25.8° API Doba Blend
Export route: The 1,070 km Chad-Cameroon pipeline south to the Kome-Kribi FSO off Cameroon
Risks: Landlocked — total dependence on the pipeline through Cameroon; acidic, waxy quality; chronic political instability, crude is 96% of exports</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>16.85,8.300000000000001,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Waha Fields (Es Sider)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational — subject to repeated disruption
Crude grade: Es Sider | API: ~36.25° | Sulfur: ~0.44% (light sweet)
Benchmark: Priced against Dated Brent; Es Sider is Libya&apos;s flagship and largest-volume grade, valued in Europe and China for its high yield of middle distillates and gasoline
Operator: Waha Oil Company — NOC 59.18%, TotalEnergies 20.42%, ConocoPhillips 20.42%
Role: The Waha concession fields in the Sirte Basin — Waha, Gialo, Dahra, Defa, Samah, Al-Fargh — feed Es Sider, the country&apos;s benchmark stream; Waha typically produces 280,000-300,000 b/d
Export route: Pipelines north to the Es Sider terminal (already on this map), with volumes sometimes rerouted to Ras Lanuf when storage or security forces it
Recent: Repeatedly hit by pipeline leaks, force majeure, port blockades and civil conflict — output has swung from a 1970s peak near 1 million b/d to frequent shut-ins; the 30-inch Dahra–Es Sider line has ruptured more than once
Risks: The single most disruption-prone flagship grade on this map — war, blockades, funding shortfalls and ageing infrastructure all recur</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>19.3,28.2,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Al Jurf Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Al Jurf | API: light sweet (a small offshore Mediterranean stream)
Benchmark: Priced against Dated Brent; a minor grade blended/exported through the offshore system
Operator: TotalEnergies 37.5%, with NOC and partners — in the offshore NC-137 area (Block C137) in the Mediterranean off north-west Libya
Role: One of TotalEnergies&apos; core Libyan assets alongside its stakes in El Sharara, Mabruk and the Waha concessions — the company&apos;s Libyan output averaged ~113,000 boe/d in 2025
Export route: Offshore loading via the Farwah floating terminal (FSO) in the Mediterranean, separate from the eastern Oil Crescent terminals
Recent: Part of TotalEnergies&apos; long-term Libyan portfolio; the company restarted the nearby onshore Mabruk field in February 2026 after a decade shut
Risks: Small volumes; offshore FSO dependence; Libya&apos;s chronic political and security instability</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>12.9,33.6,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Unity State Fields (Nile Blend)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Nile Blend | API: ~33-34° | Sulfur: low (light sweet, HIGHLY WAXY)
Benchmark: Priced against Dated Brent; medium/light sweet but with a very high pour point from its wax content
Operator: GPOC / GNPOC consortium (Chinese-led, CNPC and Petronas with the state Nilepet)
Role: Produced in Unity State, one of South Sudan&apos;s two export streams — a light sweet waxy crude, the same wax-handling challenge seen in Daqing, Uzen and Bach Ho
Export route: The Greater Nile Oil Pipeline north across Sudan to the Bashayer terminal on the Red Sea — a SEPARATE line from the Dar Blend system, which is why Nile Blend kept flowing (60,000-65,000 b/d) when Dar Blend was shut
Risks: Landlocked — entirely dependent on pipelines through war-torn Sudan; waxy crude needs heated handling; fields damaged by past cross-border conflict</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>29.8,9,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Palogue / Upper Nile Fields (Dar Blend)</name>
				<description>Status: Intermittent — repeatedly shut since February 2024
Crude grade: Dar Blend | API: ~26° | Sulfur: low but highly ACIDIC (heavy sweet, high TAN)
Benchmark: Priced against Dated Brent at a discount reflecting its heavy, acidic quality
Operator: Dar Petroleum Operating Company (DPOC), a Chinese-led consortium; Palogue is the country&apos;s biggest oilfield
Role: Produced in Upper Nile State, South Sudan&apos;s larger export stream (~100,000-150,000 b/d when flowing) — a heavy sweet crude whose high acidity limits it to refiners with corrosion-resistant equipment
Export route: The Petrodar pipeline north through Sudan — but it PASSES THROUGH the Khartoum refinery area, and fighting there since February 2024 cut ~100,000 b/d of Dar Blend for extended periods, forcing DPOC to shut production for lack of any alternative outlet
Risks: The clearest case on this map of geopolitics halting a grade — export depends entirely on a pipeline through an active civil-war zone; high-TAN quality narrows the buyer pool</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>32.5,10.5,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>N&apos;Kossa Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: N&apos;Kossa Blend | API: ~42.4° | Sulfur: ~0.04% (very light, sweet)
Benchmark: Priced against Dated Brent at a narrow discount (~-$1/b), far tighter than heavier Djeno — a blend of N&apos;Kossa and Kitina crudes
Operator: TotalEnergies (majority), with Eni
Role: Congo&apos;s second-largest crude stream, an offshore field ~60 km off Pointe-Noire, just north of Angola&apos;s Cabinda — recoverable reserves once estimated near 500 million bbl; peaked ~70,000 b/d in 2000
Blending note: Lighter fields such as M&apos;Boundi and Kouakouala (39° API) were re-routed FROM the heavier Djeno blend INTO N&apos;Kossa, because N&apos;Kossa&apos;s ~-$1/b discount is far better than Djeno&apos;s ~-$3.70/b — a deliberate move to capture more value by putting light crude in the lighter blend
Export route: To the Djeno terminal at Pointe-Noire for tanker loading
Risks: Reservoir-pressure decline; a 2007 platform fire cut output sharply</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>10.9,-4.2,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Moho-Bilondo / Moho Nord Fields (Djeno)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Djeno Blend | API: ~26.4° | Sulfur: ~0.54% (medium, sweet by West African standards)
Benchmark: Priced against Dated Brent, historically ~-$3.70/b — heavier and cheaper than N&apos;Kossa
Operator: TotalEnergies 65%, Eni 35% of the ~150,000 b/d Djeno stream
Role: The fields feeding Djeno, Congo&apos;s PRIMARY export grade — including Total&apos;s deepwater Moho-Bilondo (on stream 2008) and the later Moho Nord tension-leg-platform project, which pushed national output to record highs
Quality note: Relatively heavy but low-sulfur, typical of West African crude; the US Gulf Coast was long the main outlet, though Asian demand for heavy sweet grades has pulled volumes east
Export route: To the Djeno terminal at Pointe-Noire
Risks: Deepwater cost; the blend&apos;s heavier quality caps its price versus lighter regional grades</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>11.1,-4.6,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Utapate Field (OML 13, Akwa Ibom)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational — new grade, first production May 2024
Crude grade: Utapate | API: ~44° | Sulfur: ~0.0655% (light sweet, low carbon footprint)
Benchmark: NNPC sets a monthly Official Selling Price as a differential to Dated Brent; launched at the Argus European Crude Conference in late 2024, marketed to compete with Amenam, Agbami and Azeri Light
Operator: NNPC with venture partner Natural Oilfield Services, on OML 13, offshore Akwa Ibom State
Role: One of Nigeria&apos;s newest export grades, part of the country&apos;s push to lift output despite theft and unrest — production began May 2024 at ~40,000 b/d, targeting 80,000 b/d; the block holds ~926 million barrels of proven reserves
Market note: Its very low sulfur and low carbon intensity suit stringent European specs; early cargoes went to Spain&apos;s Repsol and to US East Coast refiners — similar in quality to the new Nembe grade
Export route: Offshore loading, blended and exported as its own stream
Risks: A young grade still building term customers and liquidity; ramp-up dependent on security in the Niger Delta</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>7.900000000000001,4.9,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Qua Iboe Terminal Fields (ExxonMobil)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Qua Iboe | API: ~36° | Sulfur: ~0.13% (light sweet)
Benchmark: NNPC sets a monthly Official Selling Price as a differential to Dated Brent; one of Nigeria&apos;s flagship light-sweet grades, historically a premium European refinery feed
Operator: ExxonMobil, with NNPC
Role: A long-established light sweet stream from the shallow-water and swamp fields of Akwa Ibom, gathered at the Qua Iboe terminal — one of Nigeria&apos;s largest and most reliable export grades, ~158,000 b/d
Export route: Qua Iboe terminal on the Atlantic coast
Risks: Mature fields; pipeline sabotage and crude theft have historically disrupted onshore/shallow streams</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>8,4.55,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Brass River Terminal Fields (Eni/Agip)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Brass River | API: ~40-42° | Sulfur: ~0.09% (light sweet)
Benchmark: NNPC sets a monthly Official Selling Price as a differential to Dated Brent
Operator: Eni/Agip (NAOC), with NNPC
Role: A light sweet grade from the central Niger Delta, gathered and exported at the Brass River terminal on the Brass estuary — one of Nigeria&apos;s traditional light-sweet streams alongside Bonny Light and Qua Iboe
Export route: Brass terminal on the Atlantic coast
Risks: Onshore/terminal exposure to sabotage, theft and force majeure; the Brass estuary is also where the new Nembe grade now loads</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>6.239999999999999,4.32,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Forcados Terminal Fields (Shell/SPDC)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Forcados | API: ~29.6° | Sulfur: ~0.18% (medium sweet)
Benchmark: NNPC sets a monthly Official Selling Price as a differential to Dated Brent
Operator: Shell (SPDC), with NNPC
Role: One of Nigeria&apos;s largest medium sweet blends, gathered from a wide area of the western Niger Delta and exported at the Forcados terminal — ~183,000 b/d, a workhorse grade for European and Asian refiners
Export route: Forcados terminal on the Atlantic coast
Risks: Repeatedly hit by pipeline sabotage, theft and force majeure — Forcados has one of the most disruption-prone supply chains in Nigeria</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>5.38,5.35,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Agbami Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Agbami | API: 47.2-47.9° | Sulfur: 0.04% (very light, very sweet)
Benchmark: NNPC sets a monthly Official Selling Price as a differential to Dated Brent; Agbami usually trades at one of the deepest discounts of the Nigerian grades (around -$5/b) despite its high quality, because it is so light
Operator: Chevron 68.15%, Equinor 18.85%, Petrobras 13%, on OML 127/128, ~68 miles offshore
Role: Nigeria&apos;s largest deepwater field, on stream 2008, plateau ~250,000 b/d — subsea wells at ~1,500 m tied to the world&apos;s largest FPSO (2.2 million bbl storage)
OPEC note: Nigeria&apos;s oil ministry counts Agbami as a CONDENSATE to keep it outside OPEC+ crude quotas, but Chevron, Equinor and traders market it as light sweet CRUDE since it is not from a gas-condensate field — a genuine classification dispute
Export route: FPSO offloading to tankers; a popular grade for refiners in India, China, Spain, the Netherlands and Brazil
Risks: Very light barrel with a narrow yield; quota-classification disputes</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>4.3,4.72,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Akpo Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Liquids grade: Akpo | API: ~45-50° | Sulfur: ~0.02% (ultra-light condensate)
Benchmark: NNPC sets a monthly Official Selling Price as a differential to Dated Brent; among the deepest-discounted Nigerian streams (around -$6/b) because it is essentially a condensate
Operator: TotalEnergies 24% (operator), with NNPC, CNOOC, Petrobras and Sapetro on OML 130, ~200 km off Port Harcourt in 1,100-1,700 m of water
Role: A gas-and-condensate field on stream late 2008, peak ~225,000 boe/d of which ~80% condensate — tied to a 2-million-bbl FPSO
OPEC note: Officially counted by Nigeria as a condensate (outside crude quotas)
Export route: Condensate exported via a single-point mooring buoy 2 km from the FPSO; the gas is piped 150 km to Amenam/Kpono and on to the Bonny LNG plant
Risks: Volumes follow gas output; ultra-light quality limits the refinery buyer pool</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>6.499999999999999,4.2,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Erha Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Erha | API: ~31.7° | Sulfur: ~0.21% (medium sweet)
Benchmark: NNPC sets a monthly Official Selling Price as a differential to Dated Brent
Operator: ExxonMobil (operator), with Shell, on OML 133, ~97 km offshore in ~1,200 m of water
Role: An ExxonMobil deepwater field on stream 2006, tied to an FPSO; a steady medium sweet stream, though loadings have been declining (down to a single ~32,000 b/d cargo some months)
Export route: FPSO offloading to tankers
Risks: Mature deepwater field with falling volumes</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>5,4.2,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Usan Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Usan | API: ~30° | Sulfur: ~0.21% (medium sweet, highly ACIDIC — high TAN)
Benchmark: NNPC sets a monthly Official Selling Price as a differential to Dated Brent
Operator: TotalEnergies (operator), with Chevron, Esso, Nexen on OML 138, ~100 km off the Niger Delta in ~750 m of water
Role: A deepwater field on stream 2012, tied to an FPSO; notable for its HIGH acidity (total acid number), which limits it to refiners equipped to handle corrosive crude
Export route: FPSO offloading to tankers
Risks: High-TAN crude needs corrosion-resistant refinery metallurgy, narrowing its buyer pool — the same constraint as Doba or Kikeh</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>7.900000000000001,4.15,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Egina Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Egina | API: 27.4° | Sulfur: 0.17% (medium sweet)
Benchmark: NNPC sets a monthly Official Selling Price as a differential to Dated Brent; NNPC prices Egina among the highest of the big streams, at a narrow discount to Dated Brent
Operator: TotalEnergies 24% (operator), with NNPC, CNOOC, Petrobras, Sapetro on OML 130
Role: On stream 2018, Nigeria&apos;s newest major deepwater grade at the time — a 330 m FPSO with 2.3 million bbl storage, integrated at Ladol Island near Lagos, ~200,000 b/d capacity
OPEC note: Nigeria tried in 2019 to classify Egina as a condensate to escape quotas, but NNPC now lists it as a medium sweet CRUDE (its 27.4 API is clearly crude, not condensate)
Export route: FPSO offloading; buyers include CNOOC and Prime 130
Risks: Heavier than Nigeria&apos;s flagship light-sweet grades; competes with Angolan and Brazilian medium crudes</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>6,4,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Hassi Messaoud Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Saharan Blend | API: ~45.3° | Sulfur: ~0.09% (light sweet)
Benchmark: Priced against Dated Brent, usually at a premium reflecting its very high quality and short haul to the Mediterranean and NW Europe
Operator: Sonatrach
Role: Algeria&apos;s largest oil field and the backbone of Saharan Blend — discovered 1956, producing around 440,000 b/d, the single biggest contributor to the blend that carries the country&apos;s export crude
Quality note: Light and very sweet, yielding a high proportion of gasoline and middle distillates — a premium European refinery feed
Export route: Pipeline north to the Haoud El Hamra gathering hub, then to the Mediterranean export terminals at Bejaia, Skikda and Arzew
Strategic note: The Hassi Messaoud–Arzew line is the longest pipeline in Algeria; Sonatrach is doubling its capacity with a parallel line
Risks: A mature field sustained by gas and water injection; heavy dependence of national revenue on this one field</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>6.07,31.68,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Hassi R&apos;Mel Field (condensate)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Liquids grade: Algerian Condensate | API: ~68.7° | Sulfur: very low (ultra-light, sweet)
Benchmark: Sold against Dated Brent and regional condensate assessments; a splitter and petrochemical feedstock rather than a refinery crude
Operator: Sonatrach
Role: Algeria&apos;s giant gas field, north of Hassi Messaoud — mapped here for its CONDENSATE, the ultra-light liquid stripped from the wet gas, not for the gas itself. At 68.7° API it is one of the lightest liquids traded, behind only Malaysia&apos;s Terengganu Condensate on the Platts periodic table
Capacity: The field produces around 180,000 b/d of liquids alongside its vast gas output; a major 2022 discovery added 100-340 billion m3 of gas condensate reserves
Market position: Too light for conventional refining — feeds condensate splitters and petrochemical crackers, much like Qatar&apos;s LSC or Iran&apos;s South Pars condensate
Export route: The dedicated Hassi R&apos;Mel–Arzew condensate pipeline (separate from the crude system) to the Arzew complex on the Mediterranean
Risks: Volumes follow gas production rather than oil economics; a narrow buyer set of splitter operators</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>3.27,32.93,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Búzios Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Búzios | API: 27.5-28.4° | Sulfur: ~0.3% (medium, sweet)
Blend contribution: Búzios does NOT blend into Tupi or any other stream — it is sold as its own separately traded grade, distinct from Brazil&apos;s other pre-salt crude
Benchmark: Platts launched a dedicated CFR Rotterdam Búzios assessment in November 2024, plus an FOB Brazil netback; Búzios has been pulled into Europe specifically as a substitute for lost Russian barrels since the EU&apos;s 2022 import ban
Operator: Petrobras 90%, CNOOC 5%, CNPC 5%
Role: The world&apos;s largest ultra-deepwater field and now Brazil&apos;s largest producer, in the Santos Basin 180 km off Rio de Janeiro under more than 2,000 m of water. Discovered 2010; reservoir up to 480 m thick across 852 km2
Capacity: Surpassed 1.1 million b/d in June 2026 across eight production units (platforms P-74 through P-79, FPSOs Almirante Barroso and Almirante Tamandaré); a twelve-FPSO build-out is targeting 2 million b/d by 2030
Market note: Slightly heavier and sourer than Tupi (27.5-28.4° vs Tupi&apos;s 29-30°), and currently sells at a discount to it in China — CNOOC and CNPC are marketing it there directly as Chinese refiners grow more familiar with the grade
Export route: FPSO offloading to shuttle tankers, then largely to China and, increasingly, Rotterdam
Risks: Ultra-deepwater complexity; a buyer base still concentrated and less established than Tupi&apos;s</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>-42.05,-24.65,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Amal Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Contributes heavy crude to the Oman Blend | blend API: ~32° | Sulfur: ~1.33% (medium sour)
Benchmark: DME Oman futures — physically delivered FOB Mina al-Fahal, and one of the two components of the Oman/Dubai average used to price most Gulf crude sold to Asia
Operator: Petroleum Development Oman — Government of Oman 60%, Shell 34%, TotalEnergies 4%, Partex 2%
Role: A heavy, viscous oil field in southern Oman produced by steam injection, and one of the streams that must be offset by lighter crude and condensate to keep the Oman Blend within specification
Technical note: Home to MIRAAH, a 330 MW-thermal solar plant built with GlassPoint that generates around 2,000 tonnes of solar steam a day for enhanced oil recovery — among the largest solar installations in the world by peak thermal output
Why it matters: Half of PDO&apos;s portfolio is heavy oil requiring thermal recovery, and steam is normally made by burning natural gas. Miraah displaces that gas, freeing it for power generation and industry — an unusually direct case of solar economics changing an oil field&apos;s operating cost
Export route: Southern gathering network north to Mina al-Fahal on the Gulf of Oman, outside the Strait of Hormuz
Risks: Steam demand makes heavy-oil economics sensitive to gas prices and solar availability; blend-quality pressure on Oman Blend</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>55.3,18,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Nimr Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Contributes to the Oman Blend | blend API: ~32° | Sulfur: ~1.33% (medium sour)
Benchmark: DME Oman futures — physically delivered FOB Mina al-Fahal, and one of the two components of the Oman/Dubai average used to price most Gulf crude sold to Asia
Operator: Petroleum Development Oman
Role: One of PDO&apos;s largest producing areas in southern Oman, and a long-standing contributor to the Oman Export Blend
Technical note: Nimr hosts one of the world&apos;s largest constructed wetlands for produced-water treatment — the field generates far more water than oil, and reed beds handle the volume instead of deep-well disposal
Export route: Southern gathering network to Mina al-Fahal, or south-east to the Ras Markaz crude park at Duqm
Risks: Very high water cut raising lifting costs; mature reservoir requiring continuous pressure support</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>54.4,17.6,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Missan Fields (Buzurgan, Abu Ghirab, Fakka)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Basrah Heavy | API: 24° | Sulfur: 4.05% (heavy, very sour)
Benchmark: SOMO Official Selling Price, set monthly against the Oman/Dubai average for Asia and Dated Brent for Europe; Basrah Heavy carries the deepest discount of Iraq&apos;s three southern grades
Operator: Missan Oil Company, with CNOOC as lead contractor on Buzurgan and Abu Ghirab
Role: The heavy-crude cluster of south-eastern Iraq along the Iranian border — SOMO named Missan explicitly, alongside Halfaya and West Qurna 2, as the fields whose heavier output degraded Basrah Light&apos;s quality and forced the creation of Basrah Medium in 2021
Export route: Pipelines south to the Fao tank farm, then subsea lines to the Al-Basra Oil Terminal, where Basrah Heavy loads from the single point moorings
Strategic note: At 4.05% sulfur this is one of the sourest crudes exported anywhere, suited only to refiners with deep conversion and sulfur-handling capacity
Risks: Hormuz dependence; border-area location; heavy sour quality competes with Arab Heavy and discounted Iranian barrels in Asia</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>47.45,32.1,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Nahr Bin Umar Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Basrah Heavy | API: 24° | Sulfur: 4.05% (heavy, very sour)
Benchmark: SOMO Official Selling Price, set monthly against the Oman/Dubai average for Asia and Dated Brent for Europe; Basrah Heavy carries the deepest discount of Iraq&apos;s three southern grades
Operator: Basra Oil Company
Role: A southern Iraqi field north of Basra producing heavy sour crude that feeds the Basrah Heavy stream rather than the lighter Rumaila and Zubair blend
Export route: Into the southern gathering network to Fao, then ABOT
Strategic note: Basrah Heavy was created as a separate grade in 2015 precisely so barrels like these could be segregated instead of dragging down the flagship Basrah Light blend — the first of SOMO&apos;s two grade splits, followed by Basrah Medium in 2021
Risks: Hormuz dependence; a narrow buyer pool for very sour crude; regional security</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>47.75,30.6,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Dukhan Oil Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Qatar Land | API: ~41° | Sulfur: ~1.22% (light sour)
Benchmark: QatarEnergy Official Selling Price, set monthly at a premium to Platts Dubai
Operator: QatarEnergy
Role: Qatar&apos;s only onshore field and the birthplace of its oil industry — first well 1939, first export December 1949; runs ~70 km down the west coast over three oil reservoirs
Capacity: Up to ~335,000 b/d; crude is stabilised at four degassing stations before export
Export route: Cross-peninsula pipeline east to Mesaieed, since Dukhan&apos;s own west-coast waters are too shallow for tankers
Risks: Eight decades of depletion requiring gas-lift and re-injection; Hormuz dependence</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>50.785,25.43,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Al-Shaheen Oil Field (Block 5)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Al-Shaheen | API: ~28° | Sulfur: ~2.37% (medium-heavy sour)
Benchmark: One of the five deliverable grades in the Platts Dubai basket, alongside Dubai, Oman, Upper Zakum and Murban. Because Dubai settles on whichever grade is cheapest to deliver, and Al-Shaheen is the heaviest and sourest of the five, it frequently sets the Dubai price outright
Operator: North Oil Company — QatarEnergy 70%, TotalEnergies 30%, under a 25-year concession from 2017 (previously Maersk Oil)
Role: Qatar&apos;s largest oil field and among the largest offshore fields in the world — 33 platforms and over 300 wells, accounting for ~45% of national oil output
Capacity: ~300,000-310,000 b/d, of which roughly 250,000 b/d is exported; ~$6bn of contracts awarded to exceed 400,000 b/d by 2027
Market significance: Sold by monthly spot tender rather than by OSP, and among the first Middle East grades offered each month — traders read the tender result as an early signal for Dubai and for Saudi Arab Light&apos;s OSP
Risks: Hormuz dependence; medium-heavy sour quality competes directly with discounted Russian and Iranian barrels in Asia</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>51.94620648059984,26.53097014344351,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>North Field (condensate)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Liquids grade: Qatar Low Sulphur Condensate (LSC) | API: ~57.8° | Sulfur: very low (ultra-light, very sweet)
Related grade: Deodorized Field Condensate (DFC) at ~57.0° API also loads from Ras Laffan; Platts publishes a dedicated DFC assessment
Benchmark: Sold by monthly tender against Platts Dubai
Operator: QatarEnergy
Role: The world&apos;s largest non-associated gas reservoir, and the source of Qatar&apos;s condensate — an ultra-light liquid that emerges as vapour under reservoir pressure and condenses to liquid at the surface. Mapped for its liquids stream, not its gas
Capacity: Qatar is the world&apos;s largest condensate producer; Phase I alone yields ~18,000 b/d of stabilised condensate
Export route: To Ras Laffan — either loaded as LSC cargoes or split into products at the Laffan Refinery complex
Risks: Volumes governed by LNG demand rather than oil economics, since condensate is a by-product of gas output; Hormuz dependence</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>51.9,26.4,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Hawtah Field (Central Arabia)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Arab Super Light | API: ~50-51° | Sulfur: ~0.04% (ultra-light, ultra-sweet)
Benchmark: Saudi Aramco Official Selling Price, set monthly against the Oman/Dubai average for Asia, against ICE Brent for Europe and against ASCI for the US
Operator: Saudi Aramco
Role: The largest of the Central Arabian (Najd) fields about 200 km south of Riyadh, and the anchor of Arab Super Light — Saudi Arabia&apos;s lightest and sweetest grade, closer in quality to North African crude than to its own Gulf-coast barrels
Field group: Developed as one project in the early 1990s with Ghinah, Hazmiyah, Umm Jurf and later Nuayyim, all linked by pipeline to a central gas-oil separation plant on Hawtah
Capacity: Inaugurated September 1994 at 170,000 b/d, reaching 200,000 b/d by March 1995; a 325 km pipeline connects the Central Arabian fields to pump station PS-3 on the East-West line
Markets: Sold chiefly to Asian buyers, where its very low sulfur commands a premium over the heavier Arab grades
Strategic note: Aramco has deliberately shifted sales toward its lighter grades, tightening terms and trimming volumes on Arab Heavy and Medium
Risks: A small volume relative to the Arab Light system; remote inland location far from the Gulf terminals</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>46.85,23.4,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Nuayyim Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational — on stream August 2009
Crude grade: Arab Super Light | API: ~50-51° | Sulfur: ~0.04% (ultra-light, ultra-sweet)
Benchmark: Saudi Aramco Official Selling Price, set monthly against the Oman/Dubai average for Asia, against ICE Brent for Europe and against ASCI for the US
Operator: Saudi Aramco
Role: The newest of the Central Arabian Super Light fields, 250 km south of Riyadh and about 50 km north-east of the Hawtah plant — it added a second gas-oil separation plant to the group
Capacity: 100,000 b/d of Arab Super Light plus 100 million cf/d of associated gas
Export route: Central Arabian pipeline network north-east to the Gulf coast terminals
Risks: Inland desert operations; modest volumes within Aramco&apos;s overall portfolio</description>
