			<Placemark>
				<name>Abqaiq Field (Buqayq)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Blended into Arab Extra Light | API: ~38-40° | Sulfur: ~0.7-1.0% (light sour)
Benchmark: Saudi Aramco Official Selling Price, set monthly against the Oman/Dubai average for Asia, against ICE Brent for Europe and against ASCI for the US
Operator: Saudi Aramco
Role: One of Aramco&apos;s oldest producing fields, discovered 1940 — its crude is blended with Berri and Shaybah output to form the Arab Extra Light export grade
Note: The field sits beneath the Abqaiq processing complex, the world&apos;s largest crude stabilisation plant, which handles crude from Ghawar and much of the eastern province — the field and the plant are different assets sharing a name
Export route: Into the Abqaiq stabilisation system, then Ras Tanura and Ju&apos;aymah, or west to Yanbu
Risks: Eight decades of depletion; drone and missile exposure, demonstrated by the 2019 attack on the adjacent processing complex</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
