			<Placemark>
				<name>Fateh Field (Dubai)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational — in deep decline
Crude grade: Dubai | API: ~31° | Sulfur: ~2% (medium sour)
Benchmark: THE namesake of the Platts Dubai benchmark, against which most Middle East crude sold to Asia is priced. Dubai is now assessed on a basket of five deliverable grades — Dubai, Oman, Upper Zakum, Al-Shaheen and Murban — because Fateh alone can no longer support it
Operator: Dubai Petroleum Establishment
Role: The field that gave the world&apos;s most widely used sour-crude benchmark its name, comprising Fateh, Southwest Fateh, Falah and Rashid
Capacity: Down to roughly 60,000-90,000 b/d from a far higher peak
Export route: The Dubai Offshore Terminal, loading from underwater storage tanks at the field
Strategic note: A benchmark that outlived the physical stream behind it — the same fate as Brent and Tapis, and the reason Platts widened the deliverable basket
Risks: Terminal depletion; the benchmark&apos;s credibility now rests on the other four grades, not this one</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>54.43,25.601,0</coordinates>
