			<Placemark>
				<name>Umm Lulu Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational — first oil October 2014, exports from July 2018
Crude grade: Umm Lulu | API: 38.7-39° | Sulfur: 0.7% (light, low sulfur and low metals)
Benchmark: ADNOC publishes a retroactive monthly OSP; Platts began publishing it in January 2019 and launched daily assessments in March 2019, quoted as a differential to Dubai
Operator: ADNOC Offshore — ADNOC 60%, Spain&apos;s Cepsa 20%, Austria&apos;s OMV 20%
Role: The newest member of the ADNOC crude family, joining Murban, Das Blend and Upper Zakum — the Umm Lulu super complex is among the largest offshore installations in the Middle East
Capacity: Around 55,000 b/d at launch, rising toward a 215,000 b/d target; roughly five cargoes a month
Export route: Blended with SARB crude and processed, stored and loaded at Zirku Island
Market note: Rarely traded on the spot market — ADNOC, Cepsa and OMV sell under private monthly arrangements to specific refiners in Japan, India and Southeast Asia, so public price discovery is thin
Risks: Limited spot liquidity; competes directly with Murban, which is close in quality and far more liquid</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>53.7,24.85,0</coordinates>
