			<Placemark>
				<name>Bahrain Field (Awali)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational — mature
Crude grade: Bahrain crude, refined domestically rather than exported
Operator: Tatweer Petroleum, on behalf of the Bahraini government
Role: The onshore field that made Bahrain the first Gulf state to produce oil, discovered 1931 and on stream from 1932 — decades before Kuwait, Saudi Arabia or Abu Dhabi
Capacity: Peaked around 75,000 b/d; ran near 35,000 b/d before a redevelopment programme lifted it to roughly 50,000 b/d
Reserves: About 124.6 million barrels, among the smallest of any Gulf producer
Export route: None — the crude goes by short pipeline to the Sitra refinery, supplying roughly one-sixth of its feedstock
Strategic note: Bahrain&apos;s oil income comes from refining margins and its Abu Safah share, not from exporting its own crude
Risks: Small and mature reserve base; offshore exploration has repeatedly disappointed</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>50.52,26.08,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Abu Safah Field (Banoco Arab Medium)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Banoco Arab Medium | API: 29° | Sulfur: 2.85% (medium sour)
Benchmark: Marketed by Bapco on behalf of the Bahraini government, in 500,000-barrel cargoes sold mainly East of Suez to the Far East and Southeast Asia
Operator: Saudi Aramco operates the field; output is split 50:50 with Bahrain under a 1970s agreement
Role: An offshore field in SAUDI waters whose 300,000 b/d capacity is shared equally with Bahrain — Bahrain&apos;s 150,000 b/d share is sold as Banoco Arab Medium, while Aramco blends its own half into the Arab Medium export grade
Export route: Loaded at RAS TANURA in Saudi Arabia, not from Bahrain — the grade is Bahraini in ownership only
Recent: Aramco began marketing part of Bahrain&apos;s share directly under an agreement running to end-2023
Strategic note: Identical crude sold under two different names depending on which government owns the barrel — the same certificate-of-origin logic as Kazakhstan&apos;s KEBCO against Urals
Risks: Bahrain has no operational control over its largest revenue source; dependence on Saudi marketing and loading infrastructure</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>50.15,27.05,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Champion Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Champion | API: 28.83° | Sulfur: 0.11% (medium, sweet)
Benchmark: Platts LAUNCHED a daily Champion assessment on 3 August 2026, reflecting 300,000-barrel cargoes FOB Seria Crude Oil Terminal; pricing has historically been linked to Malaysia&apos;s Tapis
Operator: Brunei Shell Petroleum — Shell 50%, Government of Brunei 50%
Role: Brunei&apos;s largest field, 40 km north-north-west of Bandar Seri Begawan in 10-45 m of water, discovered 1970 with first production 1972 — it holds around 40% of national reserves
Capacity: Raised to 100,000-120,000 b/d using smart-well technology, with flexible snake-type tubing reaching reservoir pockets 3 km beneath the seabed that had been known for 30 years but were previously untappable; those strata hold roughly 500 million recoverable barrels
Dual role: Part of Champion&apos;s output is exported under its own name; the rest is blended with lighter Brunei Light to create Seria Light Export Blend
Risks: Mature field sustained by advanced recovery techniques; shallow coral-reef environment; Brunei&apos;s overall output has fallen from a 1979 peak of 261,000 b/d</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>114.7419,5.21,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Bach Ho Field (White Tiger, Block 09-1)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational — mature, in long decline
Crude grade: Bach Ho | API: ~35-38.6° | Sulfur: ~0.05% (light sweet, high wax)
Benchmark: Priced under Vietnam&apos;s export formula with reference to Indonesia&apos;s Minas, the regional marker for waxy light sweet crude
Operator: Vietsovpetro JVC — PetroVietnam 51%, Russia&apos;s Zarubezhneft 49%
Role: Vietnam&apos;s largest oil field, offshore Vung Tau, discovered 1975 with first production in June 1986 — it made the country the third-largest oil producer in ASEAN after Indonesia and Malaysia
Capacity: Peaked at 267,370 b/d in 2001; now around 16% of national output. Cumulative production passed 255 million tonnes in June 2026, nearly forty years after first oil
Geological significance: The first field anywhere to produce commercially from FRACTURED GRANITE BASEMENT rock — a reservoir type never previously seen across more than 400 fields in 50 Pacific-rim basins. The discovery redirected exploration across the region
Export route: Offshore loading to FSO vessels; since 2021 the bulk of output goes under long-term contract to the Dung Quat refinery rather than for export
Risks: Deep maturity after four decades; Vietnam now imports heavily, having removed its 5% crude import tax in 2019 to feed domestic refineries</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>107.98,9.75,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Minas Field (Central Sumatra)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational — mature, in decline
Crude grade: Minas (Sumatran Light Crude, SLC) | API: ~34.5° | Sulfur: very low (light sweet, highly waxy)
Benchmark: Priced under the Indonesian Crude Price, a monthly basket average plus published differentials; Minas was long the regional reference for waxy light sweet crude, and other Asian grades such as Malaysia&apos;s Dulang were priced off it
Operator: Pertamina Hulu Rokan, which took over the Rokan block from Chevron in 2021
Role: Indonesia&apos;s largest oil field, in the Rokan block of Central Sumatra — the field that made the country an OPEC member and, at around 300,000 b/d in 2004, the anchor of its export trade
Quality note: Very high wax content with a pour point around 30°C, so it needs heated storage and pipelines; it sits alongside the neighbouring heavy 20.8° Duri stream, which is produced by steam flooding
Export route: Pipeline west to the Dumai terminal on the Strait of Malacca
Risks: Long-running decline that investment has slowed rather than reversed; Indonesia left OPEC in 2008 and is now a large net importer</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>101.45,0.85,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Banyu Urip Field (Cepu Block, East Java)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Banyu Urip | API: 32° | Sulfur: 0.305% | Pour point: 27°C (medium light, sweet, waxy)
Benchmark: Platts has published a daily Banyu Urip assessment since October 2017, quoted outright and as a differential to Dated Brent and to the Indonesian Crude Price
Operator: ExxonMobil Cepu Limited 45%, Pertamina EP Cepu 45%, four local government companies 10%
Role: Indonesia&apos;s largest-producing oil field, discovered 2001 — one of the biggest Asian finds in fifteen years, though disputes between ExxonMobil and Pertamina delayed development for years
Capacity: Around 200,000 b/d, roughly 6 million barrels a month; recoverable resource estimated at 450 million barrels
Export route: A 95 km pipeline to the FSO Gagak Rimang, a 1.7 million barrel floating storage vessel in the Java Sea, where tankers load cargoes of 650,000 or 900,000 barrels
Market note: Volumes swing between export and domestic sale — Pertamina buys Banyu Urip to displace imported crude, but when its refineries cannot absorb the specification, cargoes go to export instead of cutting production
Risks: A single field carrying much of Indonesia&apos;s output; loading depends on one FSO</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>111.6,-7.16,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Mumbai High Field (Bombay High)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational — mature, in managed decline
Crude grade: Mumbai High (formerly Bombay High) | API: 40.0-40.8° | Sulfur: 0.13-0.14% (extra light, sweet, highly waxy)
Benchmark: Domestic refinery feedstock rather than an export grade — India imports over 85% of its crude, so this barrel never reaches the international market
Operator: ONGC, with BP Exploration (Alpha) appointed Technical Services Provider in January 2025 to target up to a 60% production increase by FY28
Role: India&apos;s largest oil field, 160-176 km west of Mumbai in the Arabian Sea at about 75 m water depth — discovered February 1974 by an Indo-Soviet team aboard the survey vessel Academic Arkhangelsky
Capacity: First oil May 1976 at 3,500 b/d, reaching 80,000 b/d within three years and peaking at 400,000 b/d in 1985. Around 134,000-135,000 b/d in 2024
Cumulative: 527 million barrels of oil and 221 billion m3 of gas over fifty years, roughly 70% of India&apos;s domestic output
Quality note: Over 60% paraffinic against about 25% for Arab Light, with a pour point near 30°C — a very waxy crude needing heated handling, the same constraint as Daqing and Uzen
Export route: Two trunk pipelines, 261 km for oil and 246 km for gas, to the Uran terminal south of Mumbai
Risks: Five decades of depletion managed by water injection, gas lift and infill drilling; a carbonate reservoir with a difficult recovery factor</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>71.91160000000001,19.2499,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Daqing Field (Songliao Basin)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational — in managed decline
Crude grade: Daqing | API: ~32-33° | Sulfur: ~0.1% (light sweet, very waxy)
Benchmark: Historically the only Chinese grade widely exported, priced off Indonesia&apos;s Minas OSP for Asian buyers; today almost entirely domestic
Operator: PetroChina
Role: China&apos;s largest and most symbolically important field, discovered 1959 in Heilongjiang — it made China self-sufficient in oil for two decades and remains the anchor of the northeast refining system
Quality note: Very high wax content gives it a pour point near 30°C, so it needs heated pipelines throughout the year — the same handling problem as Uzen in Kazakhstan and Bu Attifel in Libya
Export history: Japan took up to 200,000 b/d under government-to-government contracts in the mid-1990s for direct burning at power plants, falling to around 60,000 b/d by 2001 as output declined and pricing disputes grew
Export route: Pipeline to Dalian and the northeast refineries; the ESPO spur from Russia now terminates in the same region
Risks: Six decades of depletion sustained by intensive water flooding; effectively no longer an export grade</description>
