				<Point>
					<coordinates>125.1,46.58,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Shengli Field (Shandong)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Shengli | API: ~24° | Sulfur: ~0.8-1.0% (heavy, moderately sour, waxy)
Benchmark: Rarely traded internationally; historically hard to sell abroad because it is both heavier and sourer than competing Asian grades
Operator: Sinopec
Role: China&apos;s second-largest field, in the Yellow River delta of Shandong — the crude backbone of Sinopec&apos;s eastern refining system
Market position: Used mainly as domestic refinery feedstock and as boiler fuel at power plants, in the same way as Indonesian and Vietnamese heavy grades. A long-standing government agreement sells around 10,000 b/d to North Korea&apos;s refining system
Strategic note: Shandong is also the heart of China&apos;s independent &quot;teapot&quot; refining sector, which runs largely on imported heavy sour crude — Shengli competes for the same units
Risks: Quality limits its buyer pool; mature field requiring enhanced recovery</description>
