			<Placemark>
				<name>North West Shelf (Carnarvon Basin)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: North West Shelf Condensate | API: 63° | Sulfur: 0.003% (ultra-light, essentially sulfur-free)
Benchmark: Priced against Dated Brent — at a DISCOUNT of roughly $12-13/b despite being one of the lightest, cleanest liquids traded anywhere
Operator: Woodside Energy, with Shell and other North West Shelf joint-venture partners
Role: Australia&apos;s flagship condensate stream, produced alongside gas from the Carnarvon Basin off Western Australia, including the Angel platform
Cargoes: Typically three 650,000-barrel cargoes a month, held by Woodside and Shell
Markets: Predominantly Asia-Pacific, with Chinese buying the main swing factor; some replaces declining Bass Strait volumes domestically
Why the discount: At 63° API it is too light for conventional refining — it yields mostly naphtha and light ends, so it serves splitters and petrochemical crackers rather than fuel refineries
Risks: Chinese demand drives the differential; weak light-distillate cracks hit it directly; volumes follow LNG output rather than oil economics</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>116.1,-19.6,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Vincent Field (Exmouth Sub-basin)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Vincent | API: 19° | Sulfur: 0.15% (heavy but sweet)
Benchmark: Priced against Dated Brent at a PREMIUM — cargoes have traded at $12-13/b above the marker, reaching a three-year high in the low $13s/b
Operator: Woodside Energy 60%, Mitsui E&amp;P Australia 40%
Role: A heavy sweet field 50 km north-west of Exmouth, producing through the Ngujima-Yin FPSO since 2008
Why the premium: Vincent&apos;s value comes from its HIGH FLASH POINT, not its gravity. That made it one of the key feedstocks for blending IMO-compliant low-sulfur marine fuel, a use that pays far more than refining it would
Recent: When the Cimatti field resumed within the Greater Enfield project, Vincent&apos;s flash point fell and the crude became unsuitable for LSFO blending. Differentials collapsed — a later cargo sold at roughly $5/b over Dated Brent, against low-$13s/b before. Traders described it as &quot;gradually transferring into a refinery grade&quot;
Cargoes: Around one 550,000-barrel cargo a month, to Malaysia, the US and India
Risks: Its premium depends entirely on a narrow blending specification; buyers are few, and traders call it the hardest heavy grade to sell when that use disappears</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>114,-21.6,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Van Gogh Field (Exmouth Sub-basin)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Van Gogh | API: ~17-18° | Sulfur: very low (heavy sweet)
Benchmark: Priced against Dated Brent at a premium, alongside Vincent and Pyrenees, when marine-fuel blending demand is strong
Operator: Santos, following its acquisition of the Quadrant/Apache Australian assets
Role: A heavy sweet field in the Exmouth sub-basin next to Vincent, produced through the Ningaloo Vision FPSO
Market position: One of only three Australian heavy sweet grades — with Vincent and Pyrenees — used as low-sulfur fuel oil blendstock. When Vincent lost its flash point advantage, Van Gogh was expected to take share in that market
Cargoes: Small parcels, typically around 300,000 barrels
Risks: A very small buyer pool; its premium rises and falls with LSFO blending economics rather than refinery demand</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>114.2,-21.8,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Cold Lake Blend Production Facility</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Cold Lake Blend | API: 21.1° | Sulfur: 3.5% (heavy sour)
Benchmark: Priced against WTI at a heavy-crude discount, alongside WCS
Operator: Imperial Oil and other Cold Lake in-situ producers
Role: The Cold Lake field, about 185 miles north-east of Edmonton, produces bitumen at 11° API and 5.5% sulfur — far too thick to pipeline
Blending note: That bitumen is cut with condensate at 65° API and 0.1% sulfur to reach a 21.1° API, 3.5% sulfur blend. The ratio shifts seasonally with pipeline temperature and viscosity requirements — more diluent in winter
Capacity: Around 200,000 b/d of blend
Export route: Pipeline to Edmonton and Hardisty, then east to Ontario and the US Midwest, or west on Trans Mountain to the Westridge terminal in Vancouver for seaborne export
Risks: Diluent cost drives the economics; heavy sour crude competes with Maya and Venezuelan barrels for the same coking refineries</description>
