				<Point>
					<coordinates>-110.5,55.1,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Athabasca Oil Sands (Fort McMurray)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Feeds Western Canadian Select (WCS) | blend API: 20.9° | Sulfur: 3.50% (heavy sour)
Benchmark: WCS is Canada&apos;s benchmark heavy crude and historically the cheapest heavy sour stream in North America, priced at Hardisty as a differential to WTI
Operator: Canadian Natural Resources, Suncor and Cenovus — three Calgary companies producing roughly 77% of all oil sands output, with CNRL alone contributing about 45% of WCS
Role: The mining and in-situ heartland of the Alberta oil sands and the main bitumen source behind WCS
How WCS is made: NOT a field — WCS is a blend created at the Hardisty terminal in 2004 by Suncor, Cenovus, CNRL and Talisman, pooling around 20 incompatible Alberta streams into one pipelineable, priceable grade: conventional heavy oil, in-situ bitumen from Cold Lake, synthetic crude from upgraders, and condensate diluent
Export route: Pipelines south to Hardisty for blending, then Enbridge Mainline and Keystone to the US Midwest and Gulf Coast, or Trans Mountain west to Burnaby
Risks: Pipeline egress has repeatedly capped Alberta production; the WCS-WTI differential widens sharply whenever takeaway is tight</description>
