			<Placemark>
				<name>Louisiana Gulf Coast Fields (LLS)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Light Louisiana Sweet (LLS) | API: ~35-37° | Sulfur: ~0.4% (light sweet)
Benchmark: Assessed at St. James, Louisiana — historically the premium US waterborne light sweet marker and the domestic counterpart to Brent, trading above WTI when Gulf Coast refiners are short
Operator: Multiple onshore Louisiana and shelf producers
Role: The pooled light sweet stream of onshore Louisiana and the Gulf shelf, delivered to the St. James hub
Strategic role: LLS and WTI diverging is the classic signal that Cushing is oversupplied relative to the coast — the spread that drove the shale-era pipeline build-out
Risks: Declining conventional Louisiana production; increasingly displaced at St. James by shale barrels arriving from Cushing and the Permian</description>
