			<Placemark>
				<name>Chiapas–Tabasco Mesozoic Fields (Olmeca)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Olmeca | API: 38-39° | Sulfur: 0.73-0.95% (extra light, low sulfur)
Benchmark: Priced against WTI; the highest-value grade in Pemex&apos;s slate, prized as feedstock for lubricants and petrochemicals
Operator: Pemex
Role: NOT a single field — Olmeca is blended from the diverse light crudes of the Mesozoic area of Tabasco and Chiapas, including the Samaria, Luna, Cactus and Jujo-Tecominoacán fields, pooled to meet a consistent export specification
Volumes: Historically around 150,000-325,000 b/d, far smaller than Maya
Export route: Dos Bocas in Tabasco and, historically, Pajaritos in Veracruz
Strategic note: Mexico increasingly retains Olmeca and Isthmus for its own refineries and exports the heavy Maya instead — the opposite of most producers, and a consequence of its refineries lacking coking capacity
Naming note: The new Dos Bocas refinery is also called Olmeca, which is a different asset from this crude grade
Risks: Declining light-crude output; competing claims between export sales and domestic refining</description>
