				<name>Ku-Maloob-Zaap (KMZ) Complex</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Maya | API: 21-22° | Sulfur: 3.4-3.8% (heavy sour), vanadium ~308 ppm
Benchmark: THE reference grade for heavy sour crude on the US Gulf Coast, priced by PMI&apos;s official selling-price formula at a discount to WTI, historically around WTI minus $5-7/b and wider when the light-heavy spread opens
Operator: Pemex
Role: Mexico&apos;s largest producing complex and now the main source of Maya, at over 800,000 b/d — it overtook Cantarell as that field collapsed
Export route: Offshore loading at Cayo Arcas and the FPSO Yùum K&apos;ak Náab in the Bay of Campeche, plus Dos Bocas on the Gulf and Salina Cruz on the Pacific
Why it matters: Maya is about half of Mexican production and has historically been the bulk of its exports — Pemex&apos;s own refineries largely lack the coking capacity to process it, so it goes to US Gulf Coast refiners built for heavy sour
Risks: Heavy reliance on one complex as Cantarell declines; buyers increasingly shifting toward Europe and Asia</description>
