			<Placemark>
				<name>Cantarell Complex</name>
				<description>Status: Operational — in deep decline
Crude grade: Maya | API: 21-22° | Sulfur: 3.4-3.8% (heavy sour)
Benchmark: THE reference grade for heavy sour crude on the US Gulf Coast, priced by PMI&apos;s official selling-price formula at a discount to WTI, historically around WTI minus $5-7/b and wider when the light-heavy spread opens
Operator: Pemex
Role: The original source of &quot;true&quot; Maya crude, discovered in 1976 after a fisherman reported an oil sheen. Four fields — Akal, Nohoch, Chac and Kutz — in the Bay of Campeche, formed in breccia thrown up by the Chicxulub asteroid impact
Capacity: Peaked above 2.1 million b/d in 2004 under nitrogen injection; down roughly 90% from that level
Export route: Cayo Arcas and the Yùum K&apos;ak Náab FPSO
Strategic role: One of the most spectacular field declines in oil history — its collapse is the single reason Mexico went from a 2 million b/d exporter to a net importer of refined products
Risks: Terminal depletion; nitrogen injection reached peak output at a high long-term cost</description>
