				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Tupi (marketed as Lula until the 2019 renaming) | API: ~29-30° | Sulfur: ~0.35-0.4% (medium, sweet)
Benchmark: Priced against Dated Brent; Brazil&apos;s flagship export grade and the one that dominates shipments to China
Market: Brazilian pre-salt crude sells overwhelmingly to China, where independent &apos;teapot&apos; refiners value its heavy gasoil yield and low sulfur; Petrobras cargoes have traded at a premium to Brent there
Operator: Petrobras 65%, Shell 25%, Petrogal 10%
Role: The discovery that opened Brazil&apos;s pre-salt era — in the Santos Basin roughly 230 km off Rio, beneath 2 km of water and a 2 km salt layer. Brazil&apos;s largest producing field, cited above 830,000 b/d
Export route: FPSO offloading directly to shuttle tankers, then mostly to Chinese ports; Petrobras also stores Brazilian crude in Shandong to sit closer to teapot buyers
Risks: Deepwater sub-salt operating complexity; heavy concentration of sales in a single buyer market</description>
