			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Uzen (Ozen) Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: KEBCO — Kazakhstan Export Blend Crude Oil | quality as delivered matches Urals (~31-32° API, ~1.3% sulfur) after commingling
Benchmark: Assessed by Platts, which also publishes a dedicated KEBCO/Urals spread; recently around a $2/b PREMIUM to Dated Brent while Urals trades at a deep discount
Operator: Ozenmunaigas, a KazMunayGas subsidiary
Role: The sole origin field of KEBCO — the grade Kazakhstan created in June 2022 so its barrels would not be mistaken for sanctioned Russian crude. The oil physically commingles with Urals inside Transneft, but a Kazakh certificate of origin makes the cargo sanctions-free
Capacity: KEBCO exports run around 10 million t/yr (~200,000 b/d), expected to hold roughly flat for several years
Export route: Uzen–Atyrau–Samara pipeline into Transneft, then mostly Novorossiysk on the Black Sea, over 20% via Ust-Luga on the Baltic, and 15% by pipeline direct to Germany
Technical note: Mangyshlak crude has a pour point as high as +30°C, so the entire Uzen–Atyrau–Samara line runs in hot-pumping mode to keep it flowing
Strategic role: A grade created by paperwork rather than geology — identical molecules to Urals, sold at a premium because of where the certificate says they came from
Risks: Total dependence on Russian pipelines and ports; the premium exists only while Urals remains sanctioned</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>52.85,43.3,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Kumkol Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Contributes to CPC Blend | API: ~45° blend | Sulfur: ~0.55% (light sour)
Benchmark: Sold within CPC Blend against Dated Brent; Kumkol is named in CPC documentation as a constituent stream alongside Karachaganak condensate, Aktobe, Martyshi and Arman
Operator: PetroKazakhstan (CNPC-controlled), with KazMunayGas partnership
Role: The anchor field of central Kazakhstan&apos;s Kyzylorda province, with a dual outlet — part of its output feeds CPC Blend for export, and most of the Shymkent refinery&apos;s crude comes from this field group
Connectivity: Sits at the Kumkol main pump station, where crude from western Kazakhstan arriving via the Kenkiyak–Kumkol line meets local production
Strategic role: The meeting point of Kazakhstan&apos;s western and eastern pipeline systems, and the reason Shymkent can run on domestic rather than Russian crude
Risks: Mature field in decline; Chinese operational control; competing domestic and export claims on the same barrels</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>65.68000000000001,45.87,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Ekofisk oil field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Ekofisk Blend | API: ~38.9° | Sulfur: ~0.21% (light sweet)
Benchmark: One of the five North Sea grades in the Dated Brent (BFOET) basket — Brent, Forties, Oseberg, Ekofisk, Troll — plus WTI Midland since 2023. Dated Brent settles on whichever is cheapest to deliver
Operator: ConocoPhillips Skandinavia
Role: Norway&apos;s founding oil field, discovered 1969 — the discovery that started the Norwegian petroleum industry; today the hub of a wider Greater Ekofisk Area gathering Eldfisk, Embla and Tor into one export blend
Capacity: Area production around 200,000 boe/d; licence extended to 2050
Export route: Norpipe subsea line ~354 km south-west to the Teesside terminal in the UK — the only major Norwegian crude stream that lands by pipeline rather than tanker
Risks: Six decades of depletion requiring water injection and seabed-subsidence management; single-pipeline dependence on Norpipe</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>2.911074482369678,56.76988925135856,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Eldfisk Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Feeds Ekofisk Blend | API: ~38.9° blend | Sulfur: ~0.21% (light sweet)
Benchmark: Sold as part of Ekofisk Blend, a Dated Brent basket grade
Operator: ConocoPhillips Skandinavia
Role: The second-largest field of the Greater Ekofisk Area, ~15 km south of Ekofisk; discovered 1970, redeveloped under the Eldfisk II project. Its output is commingled into the Ekofisk export stream rather than sold under its own name
Export route: Via the Ekofisk complex into Norpipe to Teesside
Risks: Mature reservoir needing continued water injection; shares Ekofisk&apos;s single-corridor dependence on Norpipe</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>3.238,56.373,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Oseberg Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Oseberg Blend | API: ~39.9° | Sulfur: ~0.19% (light sweet)
Benchmark: One of the five North Sea grades in the Dated Brent (BFOET) basket — Brent, Forties, Oseberg, Ekofisk, Troll — plus WTI Midland since 2023. Dated Brent settles on whichever is cheapest to deliver — Oseberg joined the basket in 2002, the first grade added after the original Brent-Forties pairing
Operator: Equinor
Role: A major Norwegian North Sea field ~140 km north-west of Bergen, producing since 1988; anchors the Oseberg Transport System which also carries Grane and Edvard Grieg volumes
Export route: Oseberg Transport System pipeline to the Sture Terminal on the Norwegian coast
Risks: Mature field with rising gas-injection needs; blend quality depends on the mix of fields feeding the Sture system</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>2.830000000000001,60.49,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Troll Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Troll Blend | API: ~38.8° | Sulfur: ~0.13% (light sweet — the sweetest of the basket grades)
Benchmark: One of the five North Sea grades in the Dated Brent (BFOET) basket — Brent, Forties, Oseberg, Ekofisk, Troll — plus WTI Midland since 2023. Dated Brent settles on whichever is cheapest to deliver — added in 2018, chosen specifically because its quality sat closest to the existing basket grades
Operator: Equinor
Role: Norway&apos;s largest gas field, but its thin oil column beneath the gas cap makes it a significant crude producer too — the oil is produced through long horizontal wells drilled into a zone only metres thick
Export route: Troll Oil Pipeline to the Mongstad terminal
Risks: Oil rim depletion as gas production continues; technically demanding thin-zone drilling</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>3.72,60.64,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Johan Sverdrup</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Johan Sverdrup | API: ~28.3° | Sulfur: ~0.81% (medium sour)
Benchmark: Priced against Dated Brent but deliberately EXCLUDED from the BFOET basket — at 28° API and 0.8% sulfur it would have dragged the benchmark heavy and sour, the same blend-protection logic that keeps Shaikan out of the Kurdistan pipeline
Operator: Equinor
Role: The largest field on the Norwegian shelf and the single biggest North Sea crude stream — over half a million b/d, comparable in scale to major OPEC grades
Capacity: Phase 3 lifting plateau capacity toward ~755,000 b/d
Export route: Pipeline to the Mongstad terminal
Recent: Found a natural home with Chinese refiners, with further buying from South Korea and India — the grade that turned the North Sea from a light-sweet region into a medium-sour exporter
Risks: Its rise has structurally shifted North Sea quality; Platts forecasts medium sour rising from ~2% of regional volumes in 2010 to around a third by 2040</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>2.060689510003799,59.04554284978791,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Statfjord Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Statfjord Blend | API: ~39.3° | Sulfur: ~0.25% (light sweet)
Benchmark: Assessed against Dated Brent
Operator: Equinor
Role: One of the largest fields ever found in the North Sea and a rare straddler of the UK–Norway median line, developed jointly across both jurisdictions; producing since 1979 and extended well beyond its original planned life
Export route: Offshore loading buoys directly to shuttle tankers
Risks: Deep maturity after more than four decades; late-life economics dependent on tail production efficiency</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>1.853,61.25,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Gullfaks Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Gullfaks Blend | API: ~39.3° | Sulfur: ~0.21% (light sweet)
Benchmark: Assessed against Dated Brent
Operator: Equinor
Role: A major Norwegian block-34 field producing since 1986, developed with three large concrete gravity-base platforms; historically one of Equinor&apos;s flagship operated assets
Export route: Offshore loading to shuttle tankers
Risks: Mature field in decline; high fixed cost of ageing gravity-base infrastructure</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>2.18,61.2,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Grane Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Grane Blend | API: ~27.1° | Sulfur: ~0.67% (medium, heavier than the basket grades)
Benchmark: Assessed against Dated Brent at a discount reflecting its heavier quality
Operator: Equinor
Role: A heavy-oil field on the Utsira High requiring gas injection to keep the viscous crude mobile — one of the Norwegian grades pulling the regional average heavier
Export route: Grane Oil Pipeline to the Sture Terminal
Risks: Heavy crude economics; dependence on injection gas availability</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>2.49,59.17,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Alvheim Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Alvheim Blend | API: ~34.5° | Sulfur: ~0.4% (light sweet)
Benchmark: Assessed against Dated Brent, frequently trading at a premium to it — market sources describe Alvheim-type quality as highly sought after
Operator: Aker BP
Role: An FPSO-based development in the northern North Sea; its favourable yield profile makes it one of the most prized Norwegian streams despite modest volumes
Export route: FPSO offloading directly to shuttle tankers
Risks: Modest and declining reserve base; FPSO-dependent with no pipeline fallback</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>1.85,59.58000000000001,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Åsgard Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Åsgard Blend | API: ~47.5° | Sulfur: ~0.2% (very light, condensate-rich)
Benchmark: Assessed against Dated Brent
Operator: Equinor
Role: A large Norwegian Sea development (Haltenbanken, not the North Sea proper) combining subsea templates with an FPSO and a semi-submersible gas platform; the lightest crude stream in the North Sea assessment family
Export route: FPSO offloading to shuttle tankers
Risks: Very light, condensate-rich quality suits a narrower refinery set than mid-range grades; harsh Norwegian Sea operating conditions</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>6.789999999999999,65.11,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Heidrun Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Heidrun | API: ~24.4° | Sulfur: ~0.53% (heavy)
Benchmark: Assessed against Dated Brent at a discount for its heavier quality
Operator: Equinor
Role: A Norwegian Sea field produced from a floating concrete tension-leg platform — one of the few of its kind; among the heaviest Norwegian streams
Export route: Offshore loading to shuttle tankers
Risks: Heavy-crude discount; ageing floating infrastructure in exposed Norwegian Sea conditions</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>7.32,65.31999999999999,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Johan Castberg Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational — first oil March 2025
Crude grade: Johan Castberg | API: ~33.3° | Sulfur: ~0.16% (light sweet)
Benchmark: Assessed against Dated Brent; early cargoes reportedly fetched strong prices, with quality likened by traders to the sought-after Alvheim stream
Operator: Equinor, with Vår Energi (majority-owned by Eni)
Role: The newest grade in the North Sea family and only the second producing oil field in the Norwegian Barents Sea after Goliat — 240 km north of the Norwegian mainland, developed around an FPSO after lengthy delays and cost overruns on an $8.2 billion budget
Capacity: Plateau around 220,000 b/d; reserves estimated at 450-650 million barrels
Refining appeal: Low vacuum gasoil yield and high middle-distillate output (jet and gasoil)
Export route: FPSO offloading to shuttle tankers, then the long Arctic voyage south
Risks: Remote Arctic logistics and ice-class shipping requirements; far from established loading infrastructure</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>20.25,72.45,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Brent Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational — in terminal decline, decommissioning largely complete
Crude grade: Brent Blend (BNB — Brent/Ninian Blend) | API: ~38° | Sulfur: ~0.42% (light sweet)
Benchmark: One of the five North Sea grades in the Dated Brent (BFOET) basket — Brent, Forties, Oseberg, Ekofisk, Troll — plus WTI Midland since 2023. Dated Brent settles on whichever is cheapest to deliver — the field that gave the global benchmark its name
Operator: Shell UK
Role: The namesake of Dated Brent. Discovered 1971, it once anchored North Sea production; today its output is blended with Ninian and sold as Brent/Ninian Blend
Capacity: Down to roughly 23,000 b/d — about one cargo a month, with August 2026 set to be its first ever month with no cargo at all
Export route: Brent System pipeline to the Sullom Voe terminal, Shetland
Strategic note: The benchmark has outlived the field — Dated Brent now prices a basket of six grades precisely because Brent itself can no longer support it
Risks: Terminal depletion; ongoing decommissioning of the Brent platforms</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>1.72,61.07,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Ninian Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Feeds Brent Blend (BNB) | API: ~38° blend | Sulfur: ~0.42% (light sweet)
Benchmark: Sold within Brent/Ninian Blend, a Dated Brent basket grade
Operator: CNR International
Role: The co-equal partner in the Brent/Ninian Blend stream — Platts names the assessed grade after both fields, since Ninian volumes have become essential to keeping cargo sizes viable as Brent itself declines
Export route: Ninian pipeline system to the Sullom Voe terminal, Shetland
Risks: Mature field; the viability of the whole BNB stream depends on combined Brent and Ninian volumes staying above cargo-forming levels</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>1.46,60.83,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Forties field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Forties Blend | API: ~42.1° | Sulfur: ~0.49% (light sweet)
Benchmark: One of the five North Sea grades in the Dated Brent (BFOET) basket — Brent, Forties, Oseberg, Ekofisk, Troll — plus WTI Midland since 2023. Dated Brent settles on whichever is cheapest to deliver — historically the most frequently delivered grade in the basket, so Forties often sets Dated Brent outright
Operator: Apache (field); INEOS FPS (pipeline system)
Role: The original UK North Sea giant, producing since 1975; today the Forties field is only one contributor to a blend gathering crude from roughly 80 fields via the Forties Pipeline System
Export route: Forties Pipeline System to Cruden Bay, then Kinneil for processing, then loading at Hound Point
Blend quality: Highly variable by design — INEOS publishes a live table showing Forties Blend ranging from 43.3° API / 0.29% sulfur at 0% Buzzard content down to 36.6° API / 1.01% sulfur at 60% Buzzard
Risks: Quality swings with Buzzard&apos;s share of the stream; single-system dependence on FPS for ~80 fields</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>0.9187,57.7308,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Buzzard Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Feeds Forties Blend | API: ~32.6° | Sulfur: ~1.44% (medium sour)
Benchmark: Its share of the stream directly moves Dated Brent, since Forties is a basket grade
Operator: CNOOC International
Role: The largest UK field found in decades and the single most important quality driver in the North Sea. Buzzard crude is markedly heavier and sourer than the Forties stream it joins, so the proportion of Buzzard in the blend changes the benchmark&apos;s specification week to week
Blend impact: INEOS&apos;s published table runs from 43.3° API / 0.29% sulfur at 0% Buzzard to 36.6° API / 1.01% sulfur at 60% Buzzard
Export route: Pipeline tie-in to the Forties Pipeline System
Strategic note: The clearest demonstration on this map that a benchmark grade is not a fixed specification but a moving average of whatever is flowing
Risks: Outages at Buzzard visibly shift Dated Brent quality and pricing</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>0.4,57.9,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Nelson Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Feeds Forties Blend | API: ~42.1° blend | Sulfur: ~0.49% (light sweet)
Benchmark: Sold within Forties Blend, a Dated Brent basket grade
Operator: Shell UK
Role: A light-sweet contributor to the Forties Pipeline System, helping offset the heavier, sourer Buzzard volumes that would otherwise degrade the blend further
Export route: Tie-in to the Forties Pipeline System, then Kinneil and Hound Point
Risks: Mature field in decline; its light volumes are part of what keeps Forties Blend within spec</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>1.1,57.6,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Clair Field / Clair Ridge</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Clair | API: ~23.4° | Sulfur: ~0.47% (heavy)
Benchmark: Assessed against Dated Brent at a discount for its heavy quality
Operator: BP
Role: The largest hydrocarbon accumulation on the UK continental shelf, West of Shetland — a fractured-reservoir field so technically difficult it took roughly 25 years from discovery to first oil; Clair Ridge is the second-phase development
Export route: Pipeline to the Sullom Voe terminal, Shetland
Strategic note: Represents the UK&apos;s remaining reserve base, which sits West of Shetland in deeper, harsher water rather than in the mature central North Sea
Risks: Harsh Atlantic-margin conditions; complex fractured reservoir with difficult recovery rates</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>-2.5,60.85,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Schiehallion / Loyal Fields</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Schiehallion Blend | API: ~25.7° | Sulfur: ~0.43% (heavy)
Benchmark: Assessed against Dated Brent at a discount
Operator: BP
Role: A West of Shetland Atlantic-margin development redeveloped under the Quad 204 project with the purpose-built Glen Lyon FPSO, extending field life toward 2035
Export route: Glen Lyon FPSO offloading to shuttle tankers
Risks: Severe Atlantic weather limiting offloading windows; FPSO-dependent with no pipeline alternative</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>-4.1,60.4,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Golden Eagle Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Flotta Gold Blend | API: ~36.3° | Sulfur: ~0.63% (light, moderately sour)
Benchmark: Assessed against Dated Brent
Operator: NEO Energy
Role: The main modern contributor to the Flotta Gold stream, a distinct Orkney-loaded grade separate from the Brent and Forties systems
Export route: Pipeline to the Flotta terminal on Orkney, then tanker loading — one of the few UK streams not routed through Sullom Voe or Hound Point
Risks: Declining volumes through the Flotta system raise questions over the long-term viability of a separate terminal and grade</description>
