				<Point>
					<coordinates>0.3,56.98,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Kraken Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Kraken | API: ~13.7-14° | Sulfur: ~0.7% (very heavy)
Benchmark: Assessed against Dated Brent at a steep discount
Operator: EnQuest
Role: A very heavy UK field, discovered by Occidental in 1985 while drilling for light Brent crude and then ignored by the industry for over a decade; brought onstream 2017 via FPSO
Capacity: Around 50,000 b/d
Unusual market: Kraken&apos;s density and high flash point make it usable almost directly as marine fuel blendstock rather than refinery feed — its sulfur sits only slightly above the IMO 0.5% bunker limit, so it is blended down rather than refined
Export route: FPSO offloading, with ship-to-ship transfer at Scapa Flow for cargoes heading to the US Gulf Coast
Risks: Heavy-crude discount; a narrow buyer set of specialist heavy-oil refiners and bunker blenders</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>1.55,61.41999999999999,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Mariner Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Mariner | API: ~15.3° | Sulfur: ~1.12% (heavy sour)
Benchmark: Assessed against Dated Brent at the steepest discount of the UK grades
Operator: Equinor
Role: The heaviest and sourest stream in the North Sea assessment family — a heavy-oil development in UK Quadrant 9 alongside Kraken, Bressay and Bentley, brought onstream 2019 after decades of stalled attempts at the region&apos;s viscous heavy oil
Export route: Offshore loading to shuttle tankers
Strategic note: With Kraken and Captain, part of the heavy-sour tail that contradicts the North Sea&apos;s light-sweet reputation
Risks: Viscosity and sulfur restrict the buyer pool; heavy-oil economics sensitive to the light-heavy spread</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>1.75,61.5,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Captain Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Captain | API: ~19.2° | Sulfur: ~0.7% (heavy)
Benchmark: Assessed against Dated Brent at a discount
Operator: Chevron
Role: A heavy-oil field in the Moray Firth producing since 1997, developed with polymer flooding to improve recovery from its viscous reservoir
Export route: Loads from its own FPSO onto shuttle tankers; cargoes regularly sail to St James, Louisiana, often via ship-to-ship transfer at Scapa Flow
Strategic note: One of the few North Sea grades with an established transatlantic route to US Gulf Coast heavy-crude refiners
Risks: Heavy-crude discount; transatlantic economics depend on the North Sea premium over WTI staying narrow</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>-1.75,58.35,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
