			<Placemark>
				<name>Rumaila Oil Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Basrah Light | API: ~31.4° | Sulfur: ~2.74% (medium sour)
Benchmark: SOMO Official Selling Price, set against Platts Dubai / DME Oman for Asia
Operator: Basra Oil Company with BP and PetroChina as partners
Role: Iraq&apos;s largest producing field and historically, alongside Zubair, the source of the lighter crude that defined the original Basrah Light grade
Export route: Pipelines to the Fao tank farm, then subsea to Al-Basra Oil Terminal (ABOT) for tanker loading
Risks: 100% Hormuz dependence for seaborne exports; regional security; Iraq&apos;s grade quality has drifted heavier as newer fields came online</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>47.34999999999999,30.35,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>West Qurna 1 Oil Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Basrah Medium | API: ~27.9° | Sulfur: ~3.0% (medium/heavy sour)
Benchmark: SOMO Official Selling Price, against Platts Dubai / DME Oman
Operator: Basra Oil Company with ExxonMobil and PetroChina as partners
Role: One of the heavier newer fields whose output quality prompted SOMO to split Basrah Light and create the separate Basrah Medium grade in 2021
Export route: Pipelines to Fao, then ABOT
Risks: Hormuz dependence; heavier quality competes with Arab Medium, Urals and Iranian Heavy for the same Asian buyers</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>47.32931550724308,30.65228549676802,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
