			<Placemark>
				<name>West Qurna 2 Oil Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Basrah Medium | API: ~27.9° | Sulfur: ~3.0% (medium/heavy sour)
Benchmark: SOMO Official Selling Price, against Platts Dubai / DME Oman
Operator: Basra Oil Company with Lukoil as lead contractor
Role: Explicitly named by SOMO — alongside Halfaya and Missan — as one of the heavier fields whose ramp-up degraded Basrah Light&apos;s quality and forced the creation of Basrah Medium
Export route: Pipelines to Fao, then ABOT
Risks: Hormuz dependence; Lukoil&apos;s involvement exposes the field to Russia-related sanctions complications</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>47.2,30.85,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark id="06E2D3E90A3F2D157379">
				<name>Kirkuk Oil Field (Baba Gurgur)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Kirkuk Blend | API: ~35-36° | Sulfur: ~2% (medium sour)
Benchmark: SOMO official pricing against Dated Brent for Mediterranean buyers — unlike Basrah grades, which price against Dubai/Oman for Asia
Operator: North Oil Company; BP under a 2025 redevelopment agreement covering the Kirkuk fields (Baba and Avanah domes, Bai Hassan and adjacent fields)
Role: The historic northern supergiant — Baba Gurgur, drilled in 1927, launched Iraqi oil; anchor of the northern system feeding the Iraq-Türkiye export corridor
Capacity: ~250,000-300,000 b/d area production; BP&apos;s redevelopment targets long-underinvested capacity
Export route: Injects at Khurmala into the KRG pipeline to Fishkhabur and on to Ceyhan on the Mediterranean — entirely bypassing the Strait of Hormuz
Recent: Returned to market in late 2025 after more than two years offline; BP&apos;s return to the field it discovered a century ago
Risks: Export-route politics (KRG leverage, Türkiye treaty vacuum after the ITP treaty&apos;s July 2026 termination); decades of underinvestment; security around Kirkuk</description>
