			<Placemark id="00FD4B720E3F39F9EB09">
				<name>Shaikan oil filed</name>
				<description>Status: Offline
Crude grade: Shaikan heavy — marketed standalone, NOT blended into the Kurdistan pipeline grade | API: heavy (low-teens to ~20°) | Sulfur: high (heavy sour)
Benchmark: Sold separately at a steep discount reflecting its heavy sour quality, rather than as part of the KBT blend
Operator: Gulf Keystone Petroleum 80% (operator); MOL Group 20%
Role: Onshore field ~60 km north-west of Erbil, producing since 2013 — the clearest illustration on this map of blend-quality management: the KRG excluded Shaikan crude from the Ceyhan pipeline in 2017 specifically to protect the export blend&apos;s API and sulfur spec, forcing it onto trucks instead
Capacity: ~60,000 b/d processing capacity; averaged ~41,560 b/d in 2025; 2P reserves 416 million bbl (Dec 2025)
Export route: Trucked to Turkey as a standalone heavy cargo, bypassing the pipeline blend entirely
Recent: Suspended late July 2026 amid regional conflict, the second shut-in of the year
Risks: No pipeline access, so trucking economics govern viability; recurring conflict shutdowns; Baghdad-Erbil payment disputes</description>
				<LookAt>
