			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Fahud Oil Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Oman Blend (Oman Export Blend) | API: ~32° | Sulfur: ~1.33% (medium sour)
Benchmark: DME Oman futures — physically delivered FOB Mina al-Fahal; Oman also typically sets the floor of the Platts Dubai basket
Operator: Petroleum Development Oman (PDO)
Role: Oman&apos;s founding commercial field (oil found 1964, exports from 1967) and the historic core of the Oman Blend
Export route: ~280 km pipeline to Mina al-Fahal terminal on the Gulf of Oman — outside the Strait of Hormuz
Risks: Six decades of depletion requiring enhanced-recovery investment; blend quality sensitive to the mix of heavy EOR crude and light condensate feeding it</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>56.48,22.35,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Yibal Oil Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Oman Blend (Oman Export Blend) | API: ~32° blend (Yibal&apos;s own stream is lighter, historically ~40°) | Sulfur: ~1.33% (medium sour)
Benchmark: DME Oman futures, delivered FOB Mina al-Fahal
Operator: Petroleum Development Oman (PDO)
Role: Historically PDO&apos;s largest field and the source of the lighter crude that upgrades the Oman Blend — its light, low-sulfur stream is blended with heavier crude to keep the export grade saleable
Export route: Pipeline network to Mina al-Fahal
Risks: Mature field in long-term decline; the blend depends on light streams like this offsetting heavy EOR volumes from Mukhaizna and Marmul</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>56.03,22.19,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Marmul Oil Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Contributes heavy crude to the Oman Blend | API: ~20° (own stream) | Sulfur: sour
Benchmark: DME Oman futures via the blended stream, delivered FOB Mina al-Fahal
Operator: Petroleum Development Oman (PDO)
Role: A southern Oman heavy-oil field, produced using large-scale polymer flooding — one of the heavy streams that must be offset by lighter crude and condensate to protect Oman Blend quality
Export route: Southern pipeline network north to Mina al-Fahal
Risks: Heavy crude requiring energy-intensive enhanced recovery; contributes to the long-running quality-dilution pressure on the Oman Blend</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>55.18000000000001,18.14,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Mukhaizna Oil Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Contributes very heavy crude to the Oman Blend | API: ~15-16° (own stream) | Sulfur: sour
Benchmark: DME Oman futures via the blended stream, delivered FOB Mina al-Fahal
Operator: Occidental Petroleum — Oman&apos;s largest producer outside PDO&apos;s own framework
Role: A very heavy oil development produced by massive steam injection; the single biggest quality-dilution pressure on the Oman Blend, which is why Oman&apos;s condensate output matters so much to the export grade
Export route: Southern pipeline network to Mina al-Fahal
Risks: Steam injection requires large volumes of natural gas, competing with domestic demand; heavy crude economics sensitive to price and gas availability</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>56.40000000000001,19.4,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Idd El-Shargi Field (North &amp; South Domes)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Qatar Marine | API: ~32.6° | Sulfur: ~1.84% (medium sour)
Benchmark: QatarEnergy Official Selling Price, set monthly against Platts Dubai. Sold on term contracts rather than spot, which is why Qatar Marine carries an administered OSP instead of a Platts spot assessment
Operator: QatarEnergy
Role: The oldest of Qatar&apos;s offshore fields, producing since 1962, and the largest single contributor to the Qatar Marine blend; developed as two separate structures, the North Dome and the later South Dome
Export route: Subsea pipeline via production stations PS-1/PS-2/PS-3 to Halul Island, where the field streams are blended to the Qatar Marine specification
Risks: Six decades of depletion requiring pressure support; Hormuz dependence</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>52.2,25.7,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Maydan Mahzam Oil Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Qatar Marine | API: ~32.6° | Sulfur: ~1.84% (medium sour)
Benchmark: QatarEnergy Official Selling Price, set monthly against Platts Dubai. Sold on term contracts rather than spot, which is why Qatar Marine carries an administered OSP instead of a Platts spot assessment
Operator: QatarEnergy
Role: Producing since 1965, the second of the three fields feeding the Qatar Marine blend; output has fallen substantially, reported down roughly 50% from 2008 levels
Export route: Subsea pipeline to Halul Island for treatment and blending
Risks: Advanced field decline; Hormuz dependence</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>51.99999999999999,25.6,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Bul Hanine Oil Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Qatar Marine | API: ~32.6° | Sulfur: ~1.84% (medium sour)
Benchmark: QatarEnergy Official Selling Price, set monthly against Platts Dubai. Sold on term contracts rather than spot, which is why Qatar Marine carries an administered OSP instead of a Platts spot assessment
Operator: QatarEnergy
Role: Producing since 1972, the third field in the Qatar Marine system and the focus of QatarEnergy&apos;s largest offshore redevelopment — a programme of over $6bn aimed at lifting output from roughly 45,000 b/d toward 90,000 b/d
Export route: Subsea pipeline to Halul Island for treatment and blending
Risks: Mature reservoir requiring heavy capital to arrest decline; Hormuz dependence</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>52.04999999999999,25.75,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Ghawar Oil Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Arab Light | API: ~33.3° | Sulfur: ~1.96% (medium sour)
Benchmark: Saudi Aramco Official Selling Price (OSP), set monthly against the Oman/Dubai average for Asian buyers
Operator: Saudi Aramco
Role: The world&apos;s largest conventional oil field and the volume backbone of Arab Light — Saudi Arabia&apos;s flagship export stream, averaging around 5.4 million b/d of production
Export route: Trunk pipelines east to Ras Tanura and Ju&apos;aymah; west via the East-West Pipeline to Yanbu on the Red Sea, bypassing Hormuz
Risks: Seven decades of depletion management; drone/missile exposure; Hormuz dependence for eastbound barrels</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>49.29999999999999,25.2,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Khurais Oil Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Arab Light | API: ~33° | Sulfur: ~1.9% (medium sour)
Benchmark: Saudi Aramco OSP, set monthly against Oman/Dubai for Asia
Operator: Saudi Aramco
Role: A major onshore field redeveloped from mothballed status into one of Aramco&apos;s largest single-field capacity additions (~1.2 million b/d), supporting the Arab Light stream
Export route: Trunk pipelines to Ras Tanura/Ju&apos;aymah, or west via the East-West Pipeline to Yanbu
Risks: Drone/missile exposure (targeted in past attacks on Saudi infrastructure); Hormuz dependence for eastbound barrels</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>48.07999999999999,25.1,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Shaybah Oil Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Arab Extra Light | API: ~38-40° | Sulfur: ~0.7-1.0% (light sour)
Benchmark: Saudi Aramco OSP, set monthly against Oman/Dubai for Asia
Operator: Saudi Aramco
Role: The main source of Arab Extra Light, blended with Berri and Abqaiq streams — Saudi Arabia&apos;s premium light grade, closer in quality to North African crude than to its own heavier grades; produces up to ~590,000 b/d
Export route: ~640 km pipeline north to Abqaiq, then to Ras Tanura/Ju&apos;aymah
Risks: Extreme remoteness in the Rub&apos; al Khali (Empty Quarter); dependence on a single long pipeline to Abqaiq; Hormuz dependence</description>
