			<Placemark>
				<name>Azeri–Chirag–Gunashli (ACG) offshore fields</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Azeri Light (marketed as &quot;Azeri&quot; at BTC) | API: ~34.8° | Sulfur: ~0.15% (medium sweet)
Benchmark: Sold against Dated Brent, typically at a PREMIUM — its low sulfur and Mediterranean delivery at Ceyhan make it a sought-after feedstock for European refiners producing low-sulfur fuels
Operator: BP 30.37% (operator, AIOC consortium); SOCAR 31.66%, MOL 9.57%, INPEX, ExxonMobil, TPAO, Itochu, ONGC
Role: Giant Caspian complex ~120 km off Azerbaijan — the Azeri, Chirag and Deepwater Gunashli structures; the sole source of the Azeri Light grade
Capacity: Peaked at 885,000 b/d in 2010; ~339,000 b/d by Q1 2024; ~5 billion barrels in place
Export route: Subsea lines to the Sangachal terminal, then BTC to Ceyhan on the Mediterranean — bypassing both Russian and Iranian territory; smaller volumes via the Western Route to Supsa
Recent: PSA runs to 2049; first non-associated gas production began 2026 at West Chirag, repositioning ACG as an integrated oil-and-gas asset
Grade discipline: SOCAR caps Kazakh crude entering BTC at ~2.2 million t/yr specifically to protect Azeri Light&apos;s low-sulfur specification
Risks: Mature oil decline; Caspian geopolitics; single export corridor through two transit countries</description>
