			<Placemark>
				<name>Chayvo Field (Sakhalin-1)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational (much reduced)
Crude grade: Sokol | API: ~36-37° | Sulfur: ~0.2% (light sweet)
Benchmark: Priced against Dubai for Asian buyers; historically a premium grade competing with Middle Eastern light sweet barrels
Operator: Originally ExxonMobil&apos;s Exxon Neftegas; seized and transferred to a Russian state entity in 2022 after Exxon&apos;s exit
Role: The main producing field of the Sakhalin-1 project, off north-east Sakhalin — drilled with some of the world&apos;s longest extended-reach wells from an onshore pad
Recent: Output collapsed from about 220,000 b/d to roughly 10,000 b/d after Exxon declared force majeure and withdrew in 2022; volumes have since partially recovered, with India a principal buyer
Export route: Pipeline across Sakhalin and under the Tatar Strait to the De-Kastri terminal on the mainland, then tanker
Risks: Loss of operator expertise after the Exxon exit; sanctions limiting the buyer pool; Sea of Okhotsk ice conditions</description>
