			<Placemark>
				<name>Kashagan Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: CPC Blend | API: ~45° | Sulfur: ~0.55% (light sour)
Benchmark: Sold against Dated Brent, historically at a discount of roughly $3-5/b reflecting its sulfur and mercaptan content; loaded at the CPC marine terminal near Novorossiysk
Operator: North Caspian Operating Company — KazMunayGas, Shell, ExxonMobil, TotalEnergies, Eni, CNPC and Inpex
Role: The largest oil discovery in decades outside the Middle East, in the shallow northern Caspian; the second-biggest contributor to CPC Blend after Tengiz, on stream since October 2016 after years of delay
Capacity: Around 400,000 b/d
Technical note: Its crude is extremely high in hydrogen sulphide and corrosive mercaptans, which is what delayed first oil for over a decade and gives CPC Blend its characteristic sour edge despite a light gravity
Export route: Pipeline to the CPC system and on to Novorossiysk; some volumes have been shipped separately via Transneft
Risks: Highly sour, corrosive crude requiring specialised handling; harsh shallow-water ice conditions; CPC corridor dependence</description>
