				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Vityaz / Sakhalin Blend | API: very light (traders describe it as too light for many Chinese independent refiners) | Sulfur: low (sweet)
Benchmark: Priced against Dubai for Asian buyers; its lightness suits refiners seeking naphtha and middle distillates rather than the simple topping units common in Shandong
Operator: Sakhalin Energy — Gazprom ~77.5% (absorbed Shell&apos;s 27.5% stake in 2022); Mitsui 12.5%; Mitsubishi 10%
Role: Sakhalin-2&apos;s oil leg, ~12-16 km off north-east Sakhalin Island — two fixed platforms, Molikpaq (PA-A, producing since 1999) and PA-B (2007)
Capacity: Molikpaq ~90,000 b/d; PA-B ~70,000 b/d; combined design around 160,000 b/d
Export route: Trans-Sakhalin pipeline south to the Prigorodnoye terminal, then tanker — a chain entirely separate from Transneft, which is why Sakhalin Blend keeps its own identity
Risks: Mature-field decline; single-corridor dependence on the Trans-Sakhalin line; Japan retains a sanctions carve-out for Sakhalin-related cargoes given its LNG dependence</description>
