			<Placemark>
				<name>Samotlor Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: Urals | API: ~31-32° | Sulfur: ~1.3-1.4% (medium sour)
Benchmark: Russia&apos;s flagship export blend and the reference for medium sour crude in Europe; priced at a discount to Dated Brent, widened sharply by post-2022 sanctions and the G7 price cap
Operator: Rosneft (Samotlorneftegaz)
Role: The largest oil field ever found in Russia and the sixth largest in the world — discovered 1965 in the Khanty-Mansi region of West Siberia, it was the field that made the USSR an oil superpower
Capacity: Peaked above 3 million b/d in 1980; now heavily depleted with a water cut above 90%, producing a fraction of that under tax-relief incentives
Export route: Transneft trunk network west to Primorsk and Ust-Luga on the Baltic, to Novorossiysk on the Black Sea, and into Druzhba
Risks: Extreme maturity requiring constant water handling; its decline is a structural reason Urals quality has worsened over time</description>
