				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: CPC Blend | API: ~45° | Sulfur: ~0.55% (light sour)
Benchmark: Sold against Dated Brent, historically at a discount of roughly $3-5/b reflecting its sulfur and mercaptan content; loaded at the CPC marine terminal near Novorossiysk
Operator: Tengizchevroil — Chevron 50% (operator), ExxonMobil 25%, KazMunayGas 20%, LukArco 5%
Role: Kazakhstan&apos;s largest field and by far the biggest contributor to CPC Blend — onshore beside the Caspian, on stream since 1993
Capacity: ~950,000 b/d after the $49 billion Future Growth / Wellhead Pressure Management expansion (first oil January 2025), up from ~700,000 b/d
Export route: CPC pipeline, ~1,500 km to Novorossiysk on the Black Sea; small volumes also move via BTC and by rail
Recent: January 2026 fires at on-site power facilities forced a near-total shutdown for most of the month, with full output targeted for 23 February
Risks: Near-total dependence on the single CPC corridor, itself hit by the 2026 drone campaign at the marine terminal; power-system reliability; sour gas reinjection demands</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>53.4062187167094,46.15216146839361,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Vankor Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: ESPO Blend | API: ~34-35° | Sulfur: ~0.58-0.65% (light sour)
Benchmark: ESPO Blend is priced against Dubai for Asian buyers and trades at a premium to Urals; Transneft protects its low sulfur deliberately, which is why Urals quality has degraded
Operator: Rosneft (Vankorneft), with Indian consortium stakes (ONGC Videsh and others)
Role: The anchor field of the ESPO system — a large East Siberian development brought on stream in 2009 specifically to give Russia a crude stream for Asian markets distinct from Urals
Capacity: Peaked around 440,000 b/d
Export route: Vankor–Purpe pipeline south into the Transneft network, then the ESPO trunk line east to Kozmino, or the Skovorodino spur into China
Strategic role: Vankor&apos;s low-sulfur crude is what makes ESPO sweeter than Urals — and diverting it east is precisely what degraded the western blend
Risks: Mature plateau passed; Arctic logistics; Indian partners exposed to sanctions complications</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>83.3,67.8,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Verkhnechonskoye Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: ESPO Blend | API: ~34-35° | Sulfur: ~0.58-0.65% (light sour)
Benchmark: ESPO Blend is priced against Dubai for Asian buyers and trades at a premium to Urals; Transneft protects its low sulfur deliberately, which is why Urals quality has degraded
Operator: Rosneft (Verkhnechonskneftegaz)
Role: One of the two East Siberian fields that feed the ESPO pipeline directly rather than through the wider Transneft grid, alongside Talakan; its crude is sweeter than Siberian Light and lighter than West Siberian grades
Export route: Direct tie-in to the ESPO trunk line toward Kozmino and the China spur
Risks: Remote East Siberian operating conditions; permafrost infrastructure</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>111.5,58.5,0</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Yuzhno-Khylchuyu (YK) Field</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Crude grade: YK Blend (market also calls cargoes &quot;Varandey&quot; after the loading port) | API: ~35.5° | Sulfur: ~0.71% (light, moderately sweet)
Benchmark: Sold against Dated Brent, historically at a premium to Urals given its lighter, sweeter quality; now heavily discounted to Asian buyers
Operator: Naryanmarneftegaz — LUKOIL 70%, originally with ConocoPhillips 30%
Role: One of the largest fields in northern Timan-Pechora, on stream 2008; explicitly developed to produce a grade better than Urals — 35.5° API against Urals&apos; 32°, and 0.71% sulfur against 1.3%
Capacity: Designed for 7.5 million t/yr (over 150,000 b/d); proved reserves above 500 million barrels
Export route: 158 km pipeline north to the Varandey terminal on the Barents Sea, then ice-class tankers to a floating storage vessel in Kola Bay for transfer to larger ships
Risks: Arctic operating conditions; dependence on a single terminal and the Kola Bay transshipment chain</description>
				<styleUrl>#default10</styleUrl>
