				<Placemark>
					<name>ExxonMobil Joliet Refinery</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner: ExxonMobil
Capacity: 250,000-275,000 b/d
Location: Joliet, Illinois, ~40 miles south-west of Chicago, near the junction of Interstate 55 and the Des Plaines River
History: Built in 1972, making it one of the newer US refineries — designed from the outset to run CANADIAN crude, with specialised metallurgy and processing equipment for heavy, sour, diluted-bitumen blends rather than conventional domestic crude
Feedstock: Western Canadian crude delivered by pipeline — sits directly on the Enbridge Mainline/Flanagan corridor that carries Alberta crude (including WCS) south from Superior toward Chicago and on to Cushing and the Gulf, already on this map
Main products: Gasoline, ultra-low-sulfur diesel, LPG, asphalt, sulfur and petroleum coke — about 11 million gallons of gasoline and diesel a day, chiefly for the Midwest market
Why it matters: One of the &quot;Chicagoland&quot; refinery trio (with BP Whiting, 440,000 b/d, and Citgo Lemont, 194,000 b/d) that anchors Midwest fuel supply and absorbs a large share of Canadian oil-sands exports — a direct downstream customer for the WCS barrels priced at Hardisty
Risks: Severe-weather exposure (a 2024 storm-driven power outage forced a full shutdown); heavy reliance on a single crude source (Western Canada) and single delivery corridor</description>
					<styleUrl>#default</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-88.18479480957377,41.41306886818454,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>CNOOC Huizhou Refinery</name>
					<description>Status: Operational (Phase 1 2009, Phase 2 2017)
Owner: CNOOC (state-owned); petrochemical JV with Shell
Capacity: 440,000 b/d — on the Guangdong coast of southern China
Feedstock: Configured for medium-sour grades — typically Arab Medium from Saudi Arabia and similar Kuwaiti crude, under a framework supply deal with Aramco
Role: A major southern-China refining-and-petrochemical base; the petrochemical side is a 50-50 JV with Shell producing ~2 million t/yr of ethylene
Why here: Below the 500k mega line but a strategically important Chinese coastal refinery — Standard tier
Risks: Chinese domestic fuel surplus; margin pressure; feedstock dependence on Gulf sour crude</description>
