				<Placemark>
					<name>Pemex Olmeca Refinery (Dos Bocas)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational — ramping up since 2024, still below nameplate
Owner: Pemex (Petroleos Mexicanos), state-owned
Capacity: 340,000 b/d nameplate — Mexico&apos;s NEWEST and largest refinery, at Dos Bocas, Tabasco, on the Gulf coast
History: The flagship project of the Lopez Obrador government&apos;s fuel self-sufficiency drive; ~$12-17 billion, first units 2024. Designed for 170,000 b/d gasoline + 120,000 b/d diesel
Reality check: Has struggled to run near capacity — as low as 43% (~147,000 b/d) in early 2026, hit by oil spills and fires; ramp-up ongoing
Feedstock: Domestic heavy Maya and lighter Isthmus crude
Why it matters: Built specifically to cut Mexico&apos;s heavy dependence on US gasoline imports — the centrepiece of the policy to refine more Mexican crude at home rather than export it and re-import fuel
Risks: Chronic start-up problems; the whole self-sufficiency strategy hinges on it running reliably</description>
					<styleUrl>#default</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-93.19927231022689,18.42319813720023,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Pemex Salina Cruz Refinery</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner: Pemex (Petroleos Mexicanos), state-owned
Capacity: 330,000 b/d — Mexico&apos;s second-largest, on the PACIFIC coast in Oaxaca (the only major one facing the Pacific)
Role: Supplies western and central Mexico; a coking unit is due by late 2027 to lift high-value fuel yield
Feedstock: Domestic Isthmus and Maya crude
Expansion: A planned +30,000 b/d addition; Pemex says it has the tankage and pipeline to take more crude here
Risks: High sulfur-fuel-oil output limits run rates; a 2025 fire cut output; Pacific-coast isolation from the Gulf crude system</description>
