				<Placemark>
					<name>Shell Scotford Complex (Upgrader + Refinery), Fort Saskatchewan</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner: Scotford Upgrader owned by the Athabasca Oil Sands Project (AOSP) — Canadian Natural Resources 90%, Shell Canada 10% (Shell sold most of its AOSP stake to CNRL in 2017); Shell owns and operates the refinery and chemicals plant and remains operator of the upgrader and CCS
Location: Fort Saskatchewan, ~40 km north-east of Edmonton, Alberta
What it is: A fully INTEGRATED complex combining four facilities on one site — a bitumen upgrader, an oil refinery, a chemicals plant, and the Quest carbon-capture facility
UPGRADER: Processes up to ~320,000 b/d of diluted bitumen using hydrogen-addition technology, converting oil-sands bitumen into light synthetic crude oil (SCO). Feedstock is piped from the Muskeg River and Jackpine mines north of Fort McMurray. Opened 2003, expanded 2011
REFINERY: ~100,000 b/d, running synthetic crude straight from the adjacent upgrader — one of the few refineries in the world fed almost entirely by on-site upgraded bitumen rather than conventional crude. Makes gasoline, diesel and jet fuel; operating since 1984
CHEMICALS: Up to 475,000 t/yr styrene monomer and 548,000 t/yr ethylene glycol, plus aromatics and olefins
QUEST CCS: The world&apos;s first commercial-scale carbon-capture facility on an oil-sands operation (2015) — captures ~1 million t/yr of CO2 from the upgrader&apos;s hydrogen units, piped 65 km and stored 2 km underground; a Polaris CCS expansion is planned for the refinery/chemicals emissions
Why it matters: The clearest single example on this map of the oil-sands value chain compressed into one site — mined bitumen in, synthetic crude and finished fuel and petrochemicals out, with carbon capture bolted on. It shows why &quot;upgrading&quot; is a distinct step: raw bitumen is too heavy to refine or pipeline directly, so it is first converted to synthetic crude here
Risks: Oil-sands cost and carbon-policy exposure; CO2 intensity despite Quest; dependence on the AOSP mines upstream</description>
					<styleUrl>#default</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-113.1013452104198,53.80067748954115,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Suncor Edmonton Refinery (Strathcona)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner: Suncor Energy
Capacity: 146,000 b/d — the 4th-largest refinery in Canada, on 247 hectares at Sherwood Park in Strathcona County, east of Edmonton
Main products: Gasoline, diesel and jet fuel, supplied across most of Western Canada by truck, rail and pipeline
Feedstock: Entirely oil-sands crude — primarily Suncor&apos;s own oil-sands production, plus Syncrude and other Wood Buffalo and Cold Lake barrels; a fully integrated oil-sands-to-fuel operation
Distinction: NOT to be confused with Suncor&apos;s Montreal refinery (137,000 b/d) at the far end of the eastern pipeline chain — this is the Alberta refinery, at the crude source itself
Why here: Sits inside the Edmonton/Strathcona industrial cluster — Canada&apos;s upstream refining and pipeline-origin heartland, alongside the Enbridge and Trans Mountain terminals
Risks: CO2 intensity ~56% above the median refinery; oil-sands feedstock cost cycles</description>
