				<Placemark>
					<name>Ruhr Oel Refinery — Werk Scholven (Gelsenkirchen)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner: Klesch Group (acquired from BP, March 2026) — operating as Ruhr Oel GmbH-Gelsenkirchen
Capacity: The larger, more complex of the two Gelsenkirchen sites — ~7.8 million t/yr crude distillation on its own; the combined Scholven + Horst complex is ~265,000 b/d (down from ~12 Mt/yr after BP&apos;s 2024 restructuring)
Location: Gelsenkirchen-Scholven, North Rhine-Westphalia, in the heart of Germany&apos;s Ruhr chemical cluster
Units: The primary crude-processing hub of the integrated complex, plus two steam crackers producing ethylene and propylene — the conversion and petrochemical heart of the site; ~24 production units
History: Founded early 1930s as a coal-hydrogenation plant (synthetic gasoline) by IG Farben and Hibernia — a key WWII strategic target — later converted to crude refining; integrated with Horst in 1979, joined the Ruhr Oel JV (Veba Oel / PDVSA) in 1983, BP-Rosneft until BP became sole owner in 2017, then sold to Klesch in March 2026
Crude supply: Fed by the NWO crude pipeline from Wilhelmshaven via the adjacent Scholven tank farm — critically, this pipeline supply is why Gelsenkirchen can keep running when low Rhine water halts barge-delivered crude to other German refineries
Risks: 2024 capacity cut and 2026 ownership change; exposure to European refining margin pressure; petrochemical demand cycles</description>
