				<Placemark>
					<name>Phillips 66 Los Angeles Refinery (CLOSED 2025)</name>
					<description>Status: Offline — ceased refining October 2025
Owner: Phillips 66
Former capacity: 139,000 b/d, across the Wilmington and Carson sites in the Los Angeles basin, linked by pipeline
Location: Wilmington / Carson, Los Angeles basin, California
Why closed: Phillips 66 announced in October 2024 that it would shut the refinery; refining operations ended in October 2025. The company cited weak long-term margins and California&apos;s regulatory environment
Impact: Its closure, together with Valero&apos;s Benicia refinery shutting by April 2026, removes about 17% of California&apos;s refining capacity in roughly a year — a supply loss the West Coast cannot easily backfill because it has no crude or product pipeline link to the US Gulf Coast refining centers
Wider context: Part of a broader West Coast contraction — following Marathon&apos;s Martinez closure (2020) and Phillips 66&apos;s own Rodeo conversion to renewable diesel (2024) — that is removing roughly 590,000 b/d of crude throughput from the region in under five years
Status on map: Retained as Offline to keep the region&apos;s shrinking refinery base visible rather than simply disappearing
Risks / aftermath: Site expected to be redeveloped; loss of local fuel supply raises California pump-price volatility</description>
