				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Singapore Refining Company (SRC)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner: 50/50 joint venture between Chevron Singapore and PetroChina (via Singapore Petroleum Company); Chevron&apos;s 50% stake is currently up for sale, with Japan&apos;s ENEOS reported as the frontrunner buyer as of 2026
Capacity: 290,000 b/d — the smallest of Singapore&apos;s three Jurong Island refineries, but with seven shipping berths including VLCC capability
Main products: Naphtha, reformate, alkylate, gasoline, jet fuel, diesel, low-sulphur fuel oil
Markets: Domestic Singapore market plus export across Asia-Pacific; some naphtha piped directly to the Petrochemical Corporation of Singapore
Recent: A 2017 fire shut a crude distillation unit; Chevron&apos;s ongoing divestment (2025-2026) marks the second major exit by a global oil major from Singapore refining, after Shell sold Bukom in 2024
Risks: Ownership transition uncertainty; Singapore&apos;s carbon tax has raised operating costs relative to other Asian refining hubs</description>
					<styleUrl>#default</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>103.7094695908819,1.284435884172143,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>LUKOIL Ukhta Refinery</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner: LUKOIL (LUKOIL-Ukhtaneftepererabotka)
Capacity: ~3.9 Mt/yr (~78,000 b/d) — small by Russian standards but strategically placed
Feedstock: Timan-Pechora crude gathered via the Usa-Ukhta trunk pipeline from fields further north
Main products: Gasoline, diesel, LPG (propane-butane), gas condensate products
Markets: Local Komi Republic market; surplus crude beyond local refining capacity moves onward via the Ukhta-Yaroslavl trunk line
Recent: Struck by a Ukrainian intelligence drone operation in August 2024, damaging a products tank and a gas/condensate processing unit
Strategic role: Where Timan-Pechora crude enters the corridor toward Yaroslavl and, from there, the Baltic Pipeline System — the northern anchor of the corridor
Risks: Long-range drone-strike exposure despite its remote location; harsh subarctic operating conditions; single-corridor dependence on the Ukhta-Yaroslavl line</description>
					<styleUrl>#default</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>53.7226,63.5754,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>CNOOC Ningbo Daxie Petrochemical Complex</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Operator: CNOOC-controlled joint venture
Capacity: Approximately 240,000 b/d following expansion
Main products: Transport fuels, polypropylene and petrochemical feedstocks
Recent news:  major integration project completed in 2025
Connectivity: Receives imported crude through the adjacent Daxie terminal system
Strategic role: CNOOC’s principal refining and petrochemical position in eastern China
Risks: Imported-crude dependence, operating ramp-up, port congestion and typhoons</description>
