					<Point>
						<coordinates>43.74655186425505,36.31408315354128,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>ENOC Jebel Ali Refinery</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner: Emirates National Oil Company (Dubai government)
Capacity: ~210,000 b/d of condensate processing after the 2019 expansion (from 140,000)
Feedstock: Condensate rather than crude — lighter feed suited to Dubai&apos;s product needs
Main products: Euro-5 gasoline and diesel, jet fuel, LPG, naphtha, fuel oil for the local and bunkering markets
Markets: Dubai and the northern emirates; jet fuel for the region&apos;s aviation hub; marine fuels into the Jebel Ali/Fujairah bunkering system
Recent: Running as Dubai&apos;s own supply anchor inside the Jebel Ali free-zone economy through the 2026 disruptions
Strategic role: The non-Abu Dhabi half of UAE refining — a city-state&apos;s condensate refinery serving the world&apos;s busiest international airport and one of its largest ports
Risks: Condensate supply dependence; Hormuz exposure for feedstock and exports; single site for Dubai&apos;s refining</description>
					<styleUrl>#default</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>55.05705131199897,25.00979115164894,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Cosmo Oil - Yokkaichi Refinery</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner: Cosmo Oil (Cosmo Energy Holdings) — Japan&apos;s third-largest refiner
Capacity: 86,000 b/d — the smallest of Cosmo&apos;s three refineries (with Chiba 177,000 and Sakai 100,000), nameplate reduced in the 2017 industry-wide capacity restructuring
Feedstock: Middle Eastern crude — UAE and Saudi grades prominent across the Cosmo system
Main products: Gasoline, kerosene, jet, diesel, heavy fuel oil, naphtha
Markets: Chubu and Kansai regions
Recent: An unplanned crude-unit shutdown and restart in 2025; no SAF or CCS project at this site — Cosmo&apos;s SAF is produced at Sakai
Strategic role: The smaller half of a two-refinery city — with Showa Yokkaichi Sekiyu next door, Yokkaichi concentrates ~340,000 b/d on one bay shore
Risks: Sub-scale economics in a shrinking market — the natural candidate in any future Cosmo rationalisation; earthquake and typhoon exposure</description>
