				<Placemark>
					<name>Idemitsu Kosan – Aichi Complex, Chita</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner: Idemitsu Kosan
Capacity: ~160,000–165,000 b/d — commissioned 1975, the last refinery ever built in Japan and the group&apos;s most modern
Feedstock: Middle Eastern crude by VLCC at the Chita piers on Ise Bay
Main products: Gasoline, kerosene, jet, diesel, naphtha; petrochemicals — 400,000 t/yr paraxylene and a 170,000 t/yr mixed-xylene unit (2018); heavy-oil power generation on site
Markets: Chubu, Kansai and Hokuriku — one of Japan&apos;s largest land-delivery capacities; exports via upgraded shipping berths
Recent: Renamed &quot;Aichi Complex&quot; in Oct 2022 after acquiring ENEOS&apos;s Chita petrochemical plants — one complex, two districts; May 2026 — the tanker Idemitsu Maru docked at Chita with ~2 million bbl of Saudi crude a month after transiting Hormuz during the closure crisis
Strategic role: Idemitsu&apos;s fuel-to-chemicals conversion play — squeezing petrochemical value from gasoline streams as fuel demand declines
Risks: Earthquake and typhoon exposure; petrochemical margin weakness; Hormuz-dependent crude supply</description>
