Capacity: ~125,000–130,000 b/d nominal; ~95,000 b/d effective in late 2025 due to degraded facilities; full production reportedly resumed May 2026 after maintenance
Feedstock: Syrian light and heavy crudes from north-eastern fields plus imports — historically Iranian, more recently Saudi; four trains sized for lighter Euphrates and heavier Syrian grades
Main products: Gasoline, diesel, kerosene, LPG, naphtha, fuel oil, bitumen
Markets: Syria&apos;s domestic market; growing regional transit/re-export potential
Recent: Connected to the Baniyas Mediterranean terminal, now being developed as an alternative transit route for Iraqi crude, fuel oil and naphtha
Strategic role: Syria&apos;s largest refinery and central to post-war fuel security
Risks: Ageing infrastructure; uncertain crude availability; pipeline damage; sanctions legacy; power interruptions; spills; regional security</description>
