					<Point>
						<coordinates>-90.07014680501794,38.84145802070584,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Valero St. Charles Refinery</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner: Valero Energy
Capacity: ~340,000 b/d throughput at Norco on the river&apos;s west bank — the largest plant in Valero&apos;s Gulf system
Feedstock: Heavy sour and medium grades — deep conversion with coking and hydrocracking
Main products: Gasoline, diesel, jet — heavily export-oriented toward Latin America
Markets: US Gulf and the Atlantic Basin product trade
Strategic role: The export specialist of the river row — Louisiana products loading straight into the hemispheric market
Risks: Hurricane corridor (Ida 2021 shut the river row for weeks); heavy-crude supply economics; export-demand cycles</description>
					<styleUrl>#default</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-90.44,29.98,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Mina Abdullah Refinery</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner: Kuwait National Petroleum Company (KNPC)
Capacity: 454,000 b/d — the larger half of the merged 800,000 b/d KNPC complex created with Mina al-Ahmadi under the ~$16bn Clean Fuels Project
Feedstock: Kuwaiti crude from the Ahmadi gathering system
Main products: Euro-5 gasoline and ultra-low-sulphur diesel, jet fuel, LPG, low-sulphur fuel oil, petcoke, sulphur
Markets: Domestic supply and Asian/European product exports via the Shuaiba oil berth and the refinery&apos;s Sea Island
Recent: Running as the volume engine of the merged complex while KNPC pushes combined capacity from ~798,600 toward 825,000 b/d; the 2026 war put the whole coastal strip inside the drone-threat perimeter demonstrated at Mina al-Ahmadi in March
Strategic role: With MAA and Al Zour, one of the three pillars that make Kuwait a top-tier product exporter despite having no crude bypass
Risks: Hormuz dependence for every cargo; single coastal strip concentration; drone and missile exposure</description>
					<styleUrl>#default</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>48.15082373212186,29.0046758806138,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>SATORP Jubail Refinery</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner: Saudi Aramco 62.5%, TotalEnergies 37.5%
Capacity: ~460,000 b/d — one of the world&apos;s most complex full-conversion refineries, built to run Arabian Heavy
Feedstock: Arabian Heavy crude from the eastern system
Main products: Ultra-low-sulphur diesel and gasoline, jet fuel, LPG, petcoke, paraxylene, benzene
Markets: Export-oriented toward Asia and Europe, plus domestic supply
Recent: The $11bn Amiral expansion under construction — a mixed-feed cracker integrating the refinery into one of the region&apos;s largest petrochemical complexes, start-up targeted 2027
Strategic role: The model Aramco downstream JV — heavy crude in, premium products and chemicals out, on the Gulf coast
Risks: Hormuz dependence for exports; construction execution; petrochemical margin cycles</description>
					<styleUrl>#default</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>49.49794482580032,26.96144651057338,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>SASREF Jubail Refinery</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner: Saudi Aramco (100% since buying out Shell&apos;s 50% in 2019)
Capacity: ~305,000 b/d
Feedstock: Arabian Light from the eastern system
Main products: Diesel, jet/kerosene, naphtha, LPG, fuel oil, sulphur
Markets: Export-focused with domestic supply obligations
Recent: Integration deepened into Aramco&apos;s wholly-owned refining system after the Shell exit; debottlenecking studies toward higher capacity
Strategic role: The older half of the Jubail refining pair — Aramco&apos;s proof it can run ex-JV assets alone
Risks: Hormuz dependence; competition from newer, more complex plants; ageing units</description>
					<styleUrl>#default</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>49.60856603511597,27.04594220541318,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Jazan Refinery &amp; IGCC Complex</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner: Saudi Aramco
Capacity: ~400,000 b/d, integrated with one of the world&apos;s largest gasification/combined-cycle power complexes (IGCC) running on refinery residues
Feedstock: Arabian Heavy and Medium delivered by tanker to the adjacent terminal
Main products: Gasoline, ultra-low-sulphur diesel, jet fuel; power for the southern grid
Markets: Product exports via the Red Sea plus southern domestic supply
Recent: Ramped through the mid-2020s after phased commissioning; sits in the far south-west — inside the zone the Houthi campaign has made the Red Sea&apos;s most dangerous, and the July 2026 threat declaration against Saudi ports lands closest here
Strategic role: Aramco&apos;s southern outpost — a Red Sea export refinery deliberately far from the Gulf, now testing whether distance from Hormuz equals safety
Risks: Houthi missile/drone range; remote logistics; IGCC operational complexity</description>
					<styleUrl>#default</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>42.6,16.8,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Petro Rabigh Refinery &amp; Petrochemical Complex</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner: Petro Rabigh — Saudi Aramco ~60% (raised in 2024 by acquiring half of Sumitomo Chemical&apos;s stake), Sumitomo Chemical retaining a reduced share
Capacity: ~400,000 b/d integrated with a large ethane/naphtha petrochemical complex on the Red Sea
Feedstock: Arabian crude delivered via the west-coast system, plus ethane
Main products: Gasoline, diesel, jet, fuel oil; ethylene and propylene derivatives, aromatics
Markets: Red Sea product exports and Asian petrochemical sales
Recent: Recapitalised and restructured in 2024 as Aramco took control — years of losses made it the problem child of the downstream portfolio
Strategic role: The Red Sea&apos;s integrated refining-petrochemical site, between Jeddah and Yanbu on the non-Hormuz coast
Risks: Chronic financial underperformance; petrochemical margins; Red Sea shipping insecurity</description>
					<styleUrl>#default</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>39.01570632996394,22.71634539179698,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>YASREF Yanbu Refinery</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner: Saudi Aramco 62.5%, Sinopec 37.5%
Capacity: ~400,000 b/d full-conversion, running Arabian Heavy
Main products: Ultra-low-sulphur diesel and gasoline, petcoke, sulphur — an export machine
Markets: Asia and Europe from the Yanbu terminals
Recent: The Chinese anchor in Saudi downstream — Sinopec&apos;s stake mirrors Aramco&apos;s push into Chinese refining; central to the 2026 westbound rerouting as Yanbu became the kingdom&apos;s working export gate
Strategic role: One-third of the Yanbu refining cluster (with SAMREF and the domestic Aramco plant) that turns Petroline crude into Red Sea product exports without touching Hormuz
Risks: Red Sea shipping insecurity and the July 2026 Houthi threat perimeter; heavy-crude conversion economics</description>
