				<Placemark>
					<name>Hengyi Pulau Muara Besar Refinery</name>
					<description>Status: Operational since November 2019
Owner: Hengyi Industries — China&apos;s Zhejiang Hengyi Group 70%, Damai Holdings 30% (Brunei&apos;s Strategic Development Capital Fund)
Capacity: 175,000 b/d (8 million t/yr), on a 276-hectare site on Muara Besar Island near Bandar Seri Begawan
Units: Atmospheric and vacuum distillation, hydrocracking, reforming, flexicoking, diesel and jet hydrotreating, naphtha isomerisation, sulphur recovery, plus an aromatics unit
Main products: Petrol, diesel and jet fuel, plus ~1.5 million t/yr paraxylene and 500,000 t/yr benzene
Feedstock: MOSTLY IMPORTED crude — Brunei supplies only a small share. It took its first direct Saudi cargo in June 2020, about 1 million barrels of Arabian Medium
Investment: $3.45 billion for Phase 1, one of the largest single foreign investments in Brunei and a flagship Belt and Road project
Phase 2: Implementation agreement signed November 2023 — a 1.65 Mt/yr ethylene cracker, a PTA/PET plant and three new jetties, lifting crude capacity to 11 Mt/yr from 2029
Risks: Dependent on imported crude and on Chinese demand for its petrochemical output; CO2 intensity runs around 60% above the median refinery</description>
