		<Folder>
			<name>Biofuel &amp; Low-Carbon Production</name>
			<Folder>
				<name>HVO / SAF plants</name>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Phillips 66 Rodeo Renewable Energy Complex</name>
					<description>Status: Operational as a renewable fuels plant since 2024
Owner: Phillips 66
Type: Former crude refinery CONVERTED to renewable diesel and SAF — no longer processes crude oil
Capacity: Around 50,000 b/d (800 million gallons/year) of renewable diesel, sustainable aviation fuel and renewable naphtha — one of the world&apos;s largest such facilities
Feedstock: Used cooking oil, fats, greases and vegetable oils instead of crude
History: The former Rodeo crude refinery ceased conventional refining in early 2024; the conversion is part of why California lost ~280,000 b/d of crude capacity to renewable projects
Why here: Filed under Biofuel &amp; Low-Carbon because it is now a renewable fuels plant, not a crude refinery — the West Coast&apos;s regulatory push turned crude capacity into biofuel capacity
Risks: Feedstock cost and availability; policy-dependent economics (LCFS credits, RFS)</description>
					<styleUrl>#default00</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-122.2542486580876,38.04323152617269,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Marathon Martinez Renewable Fuels (Martinez Renewables)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational as a renewable fuels plant
Owner: Marathon Petroleum, in a JV with Neste (Martinez Renewables)
Type: Former crude refinery CONVERTED to renewable diesel — no longer a crude refinery
Capacity: Up to ~48,000 b/d (730 million gallons/year) of renewable diesel at full build-out
Feedstock: Waste oils, fats and vegetable oils
History: Marathon shut the Martinez crude refinery in 2020 and redeveloped it into renewable diesel; distinct from the neighbouring PBF Martinez crude refinery still operating
Why here: Biofuel &amp; Low-Carbon — a renewable fuels plant, part of the ~280,000 b/d of California crude capacity lost to renewable conversions
Risks: Feedstock supply; policy-dependent margins</description>
					<styleUrl>#default00</styleUrl>
