			<Folder>
				<name>Import &amp; Export Terminal</name>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Bajo Grande Jetty (Terminal)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational — capacity-constrained
Owner/Operator: PDVSA, serving the Petroboscán JV (PDVSA 60%, Chevron 40%)
Role: The actual loading jetty for Bajo Grande — a narrow pier reaching into Lake Maracaibo with a small offshore mooring point, NOT a multi-berth port; visibly modest infrastructure matching its low throughput
Capacity: 96,000 b/d shipping capacity; 1.345 million barrels storage
Location: Verified at 10°30&apos;40.52&quot;N, 71°36&apos;27.92&quot;W — approximately 3.6 km east of the Bajo Grande refinery itself
Connectivity: Receives crude via the Boscán-Bajo Grande feeder pipeline; loads small tankers (capped ~250,000 bbl by the lake channel&apos;s 9.8 m draft limit) for ship-to-ship transfer at Amuay Bay
Why the infrastructure looks minimal: This terminal was built for asphalt-grade crude in small volumes, not for large tanker traffic — a single narrow jetty and mooring point is genuinely all the site requires
Risks: Vapour-generation equipment shortfalls have repeatedly cut loading rates; export volume tied directly to Chevron&apos;s OFAC licence status</description>
					<styleUrl>#mfailed20</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-71.6078,10.5113,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>La Salina Terminal (Cabimas)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational — underutilised (~25% utilisation per PDVSA&apos;s own reporting)
Owner/Operator: PDVSA
Classification: Import &amp; Export Terminal — large, general-purpose terminal (NOT a gathering/blending hub; no confirmed blending of multiple streams into a new grade occurs here)
Capacity: 535,000 b/d average shipping and receiving capacity; 5.27 million barrels storage of crude and products — one of the largest terminals in western Venezuela
Role: Handles a broad range of lake crudes and refined products, distinct from Bajo Grande&apos;s single-grade Boscán focus
Location: Cabimas, on Lake Maracaibo&apos;s eastern shore — geographically separate from Bajo Grande on the western shore
Recent: PDVSA&apos;s own internal reporting flagged the Substation 40-L electrical supply as obsolete, and 14 storage tanks overdue for major maintenance — a terminal running at a quarter of its rated capacity due to infrastructure decay rather than lack of crude
Risks: Ageing electrical and storage infrastructure; a large terminal running far below its designed throughput</description>
					<styleUrl>#mfailed20</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-71.45659960786662,10.38271260235905,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Callao Import Terminal</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Various, serving Repsol&apos;s adjacent La Pampilla refinery and the port of Callao
Role: Peru&apos;s main crude and product IMPORT gateway — as domestic jungle production has declined relative to demand, Peru increasingly imports foreign crude here to keep La Pampilla and the Lima market supplied
Why it matters: The clearest evidence that Peru&apos;s refining system is import-dependent, not export-oriented — this terminal brings crude IN, the opposite direction from Bayóvar
Risks: Exposure to international freight and crude-price volatility; concentrates import supply at one port serving the capital</description>
