					<Point>
						<coordinates>-77.12926312339569,-12.03965260091957,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Bayóvar Terminal (Sechura, Piura)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational — intermittent due to upstream pipeline disruptions
Owner/Operator: Petroperu
Role: The Pacific coast terminus of the Oleoducto Norperuano — where Amazon jungle crude finally reaches the sea after crossing the Andes
Facilities: Can berth tankers up to 250,000 dwt; four hydraulic arms load up to 100,000 barrels/hour, 24 hours a day; 14 tanks holding 1.8 million barrels
History: The tanker Trompeteros made the first-ever shipment from here to La Pampilla on 7 June 1977
Function: Receives ONP crude, stores it, and loads it onto coastal tankers for La Pampilla and other refineries — also holds enough stock (~30 days) to keep refineries supplied during the ONP&apos;s frequent shutdowns
Risks: Entirely dependent on the ONP for its crude supply; stock buffer is finite when the pipeline is down for extended repairs</description>
