						<coordinates>-81.06361127241121,-5.805070207129226,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Punta Colorada Onshore Tank Terminal (VMOS)</name>
					<description>Status: Planned — ~10-54% complete depending on component, first phase targeted December 2026
Owner/Operator: YPF-led VMOS consortium (Vaca Muerta Oil Sur)
Role: The onshore storage terminal at Sierra Grande, Río Negro, on the Golfo San Matías — the receiving point for crude arriving via the ~437-570 km VMOS pipeline from Vaca Muerta (Allen/Loma Campana)
Facilities: Six tanks of 120,000 m3 each (82 m diameter, 35 m tall, ~10 storeys) — ~720,000 m3 total storage, among the largest tank farms in Argentina. NO fixed marine berths or jetty — this is a pure storage/staging site; loading happens offshore
Why here: Selected over Bahía Blanca, Quequén and Comodoro Rivadavia specifically for water depth and metocean conditions that allow VLCC access, and for being ~600 km from the Vaca Muerta core — comparable to Bahía Blanca but far less congested
Strategic role: Designed to handle up to 550,000 b/d, positioning Punta Colorada as Argentina&apos;s largest crude export infrastructure
Risks: Major greenfield construction project on an accelerated schedule; first-of-its-kind scale for Argentina</description>
