				<Placemark>
					<name>Pajaritos / Coatzacoalcos Terminal (Veracruz)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner: Pemex (Petroleos Mexicanos), state-owned
Type: Onshore crude and petrochemical terminal/port — tanks and docks in the Coatzacoalcos estuary, NOT an offshore buoy
Role: Handles crude, products and petrochemical feedstocks, linked to the southern refining and petrochemical cluster
Connectivity: Serves the Minatitlan refinery and the Coatzacoalcos petrochemical complex; a historic export and cabotage point
Risks: Ageing infrastructure; a 2016 petrochemical-plant explosion nearby highlighted safety concerns</description>
					<styleUrl>#mfailed20</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-94.40383787103563,18.11999824519047,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Enbridge Edmonton Terminal (Mainline origin)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Enbridge
Role: One of the two great western origins of the Enbridge Mainline — the largest crude pipeline network in North America. Western Canadian crude gathered here (and at Hardisty) enters the Mainline for the journey east to Superior and on to the US Midwest and Quebec
Connectivity: A massive tank farm receiving oil-sands and conventional crude by feeder pipelines, feeding Mainline Lines 1-4 and 67; the head of the pipeline chain (Mainline → Line 5 → Line 9) that supplies the Quebec refineries
Why it matters: This is where the Enbridge export system BEGINS. Every Enbridge junction on this map — Superior, Flanagan, Cushing, the Gulf, and the eastern route to Montreal — traces back to crude entering here and at Hardisty
Twin hub: Works alongside the Hardisty terminal (~200 km south-east); Edmonton is the northern gathering point closest to the oil-sands upgraders, Hardisty the blending hub for WCS
Risks: Concentration of the nation&apos;s export pipeline origin at the Edmonton/Hardisty pair; apportionment when volumes exceed pipeline space</description>
					<styleUrl>#mfailed20</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-113.3647551720659,53.54410726842255,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Sorel-Tracy (Kildair) Rail-to-Tanker Terminal</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Kildair Service (a Sokol/IMTT-type terminal), on the St. Lawrence at Sorel-Tracy, ~50 km north-east of Montreal
Role: The rail-to-tidewater link that lets western Canadian crude reach EUROPE from a Canadian port — WCS railed from Alberta is stored here and loaded onto Aframax tankers on the St. Lawrence Seaway
Landmark cargo: In 2014 Suncor railed ~700,000 barrels of WCS to Kildair and loaded the Aframax Minerva Gloria, which sailed to a heavy-sour refinery in Sardinia (Italy) — one of the first shipments of Canadian heavy crude to Europe from a Canadian port
Why it matters: Because Canada has no eastern crude-export PIPELINE (Energy East was cancelled in 2017), the eastern route to Europe runs by RAIL to a St. Lawrence terminal, then tanker — smaller-scale and costlier than the western Trans Mountain pipeline chain, but the genuine Canada-to-Europe path
Connectivity: Alberta oil-sands by rail → Kildair storage → Aframax tanker → St. Lawrence → Atlantic → Rotterdam / Mediterranean refineries
Risks: Rail cost and capacity; St. Lawrence winter ice and draft limits (Aframax, not VLCC); a marginal route that widens/narrows with the WCS-Brent spread</description>
					<styleUrl>#mfailed20</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-73.16347048006234,45.99936500136825,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>NWO Crude Terminal (Umschlaganlage NWO), Wilhelmshaven</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Nord-West Oelleitung GmbH (NWO) — co-owned by Ruhr Oel, BP, Shell and Holborn Europa
Role: Germany&apos;s principal crude oil IMPORT terminal and the ORIGIN of the NWO pipeline — the point where imported crude enters the country before being piped to the Rhine-Ruhr refineries (Gelsenkirchen-Scholven and -Horst, Lingen and Wesseling), all on this map
Facilities: A long tanker pier reaching into the deep Jade channel, built in 1971 to handle VLCCs up to 250,000 dwt — Germany&apos;s only berth able to take fully-laden very large crude carriers; crude crosses a pipe bridge over the dike into the adjacent Rüstringer Groden tank farm
Storage: ~1.6 million m3 (about 11 million barrels) across 26 tanks of ~30,000 m3 and 9 tanks of ~100,000 m3, with onward connection to salt-cavern storage at Wilhelmshaven and Wittmund held by Germany&apos;s EBV strategic-stockpiling agency
Why it matters: This is the head of the chain that keeps western Germany&apos;s refineries supplied by pipeline rather than Rhine barge — the reason Gelsenkirchen can keep running when the Rhine falls to critical low water. Crude moves VLCC to pier, pier to tank farm, tank farm to pipeline, without ever touching the river
Throughput: The NWO pipeline system carries roughly 16 million t/yr of crude inland from this terminal
Distinction: Separate from the neighbouring HES Wilhelmshaven Tank Terminal (independent product storage) — this is the dedicated crude-import gateway feeding the NWO pipeline
Risks: A single deep-water crude entry point for much of western Germany; Jade-channel and North Sea weather exposure</description>
					<styleUrl>#mfailed20</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>8.149299932834897,53.55407493353481,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>El-Hamra Terminal (Mediterranean)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: EGPC / Western Desert Operating Petroleum Company (WEPCO)
Role: The Mediterranean export terminal for Western Desert Blend — receives light sweet crude by pipeline from the Western Desert fields and loads it for export
Distinction: Separate from Egypt&apos;s Gulf of Suez export chain (Ras Shukheir for Suez Blend, Wadi Feiran for Belayim) — the Western Desert has its own dedicated northern outlet on the Mediterranean, which is why Western Desert Blend stays a distinct grade
Strategic note: Western Desert crude is Egypt&apos;s main genuinely-exported grade; the sour Gulf of Suez blends are largely refined domestically
Risks: Ties the region&apos;s export solely to this terminal; declining upstream volumes</description>
					<styleUrl>#mfailed20</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>28.83090089144327,30.92870431138604,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Gamba Terminal (Cap Lopez area)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Shell Gabon legacy infrastructure (now under Gabonese state / Assala)
Role: The coastal export terminal for Rabi Export Blend — receives Rabi and Gamba crude by pipeline and loads it for export; a strike here in 2008 shut in ~30,000 b/d of Total and Perenco output as well, showing it as a shared bottleneck
Connectivity: Fed by the 135 km pipeline from Rabi-Kounga plus the local Gamba/Ivinga field
Note: Gabon&apos;s other main stream, Mandji (a sour grade marketed as a rough equivalent to Alaskan North Slope), exports separately via the Cap Lopez terminal near Port-Gentil
Risks: Ageing infrastructure serving declining fields; labour and reliability disruptions</description>
					<styleUrl>#mfailed20</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>8.711179552027041,-0.62994767853691,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Bashayer Terminal (Port Sudan)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational — throughput cut by war
Owner/Operator: Sudan&apos;s state oil infrastructure, handling both South Sudanese and Sudanese crude
Role: The single Red Sea export outlet for ALL South Sudanese crude — both Nile Blend and Dar Blend load here after their ~1,400-1,600 km pipeline journeys across Sudan
Throughput: Averaged ~130,000 b/d in 2023; fell to ~65,000 b/d after the Petrodar disruption from February 2024
Why it matters: A foreign country&apos;s terminal is the only way landlocked South Sudan can sell its oil — the ultimate expression of transit dependence
Risks: Located in a country at civil war; South Sudan has no control over its own export gateway</description>
					<styleUrl>#mfailed20</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>37.29427720864038,19.38430562983066,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Djeno Terminal (Pointe-Noire)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: TotalEnergies (20-year concession renewed 2020), with Eni and other shareholders
Role: The single export gateway for ALL crude produced in the Republic of Congo — every cargo of Djeno and N&apos;Kossa is stored here before export; the nerve centre of Congolese oil, at Pointe-Noire
Facilities: Built by Total in 1972; can accommodate VLCCs, with typical cargo size around 920,000 barrels
Connectivity: Receives crude from the offshore fields (Moho-Bilondo, Moho Nord, N&apos;Kossa and others) and supplies the local Pointe-Noire refinery
Recent: TotalEnergies is examining plans to solarize the terminal; the government sought unsuccessfully to nationalize it before the 2020 concession renewal
Risks: A single national export point — any disruption halts the country&apos;s crude exports; ageing 1972 infrastructure</description>
					<styleUrl>#mfailed20</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>11.9345554987072,-4.915502291507291,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Magellan East Houston (MEH) Terminal</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Magellan Midstream (now part of ONEOK)
Role: The crude terminal on the Houston Ship Channel that is the physical basis of the WTI Houston (MEH) price assessment — the point where Permian light sweet crude is priced on the Gulf coast
Connectivity: Receives unblended Midland-quality WTI from the Permian via the BridgeTex (300,000 b/d), Longhorn (275,000 b/d) and Wink-to-Webster pipelines, holding it in segregated tankage so its quality stays pure for pricing and export
Function: From here crude either feeds the 4.4 million b/d Houston-area refinery network or moves to Ship Channel docks for export by tanker
Why it matters: Active spot trading at this single terminal is what allows WTI Houston to exist as a benchmark — roughly 500,000 b/d of physical trade, giving the Gulf coast its own light-sweet price marker instead of relying on distant Cushing or Louisiana LLS
Risks: Ship Channel congestion and closures; hurricane exposure; competition with the neighbouring ECHO terminal for the pricing point</description>
					<styleUrl>#mfailed20</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-95.20426186564896,29.74771496697278,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Enterprise Hydrocarbon Terminal (EHT), Houston Ship Channel</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Enterprise Products Partners
Role: One of the largest crude, LPG and petrochemical EXPORT terminals on the Houston Ship Channel — the loading complex behind Enterprise&apos;s Gulf-coast export business
Facilities: Eight docks (an eighth added ~2020), one able to load a Suezmax — the largest tanker class that can navigate the channel; crude export capacity expanded by 840,000 b/d with that dock, plus large LPG and polymer-grade propylene export capacity (LPG export capacity over 1 million b/d)
Connectivity: Fed by Enterprise&apos;s crude system from the Permian (Wink-to-Webster) and its ECHO terminal; a major outlet for US crude and NGL exports
