				<Placemark>
					<name>Punta Colorada Offshore SPM Terminal (VMOS, Golfo San Matías)</name>
					<description>Status: Planned — monobuoys en route to site (2026), targeted online with the onshore terminal
Owner/Operator: YPF-led VMOS consortium
Role: The actual LOADING point for VMOS crude — two Single Point Mooring (SPM) monobuoys moored ~7-8 km offshore, connected to the onshore tank terminal by a 7 km subsea pipeline
Why offshore, not a port: Unlike a conventional port with fixed docks, Punta Colorada was built with NO physical berths. Tankers moor at the monobuoys in open water and load directly — the only way to accommodate VLCCs (up to 2 million barrels/cargo, ~350,000 m3) that no Argentine port could otherwise receive
Facilities: Each monobuoy weighs 272 tonnes and is 15.3 m in diameter, purpose-built for direct VLCC loading; shipped to Argentina from overseas (transiting the Strait of Hormuz en route)
Economics: VLCC access is projected to save up to $3/barrel in logistics costs versus smaller tankers, and the project is expected to generate $15-20 billion/year in export revenue once at scale
Why it matters: This is Argentina&apos;s FIRST deepwater oil export system able to load the largest tanker class — the physical answer to Vaca Muerta having outgrown Puerto Rosales
Risks: Complex, first-of-its-kind offshore construction in Argentina; weather-dependent loading typical of open-water SPM systems</description>
