				<Placemark>
					<name>ONP Station 1 &amp; River Port (San José de Saramuro)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational — repeatedly disrupted by community blockades
Owner/Operator: Petroperu
Role: The HEAD of the Oleoducto Norperuano — the gathering point where jungle crude (Lote 8, Lote 67) converges before the 854 km trunk pipeline to Bayóvar begins. Operating since 1976
River port: A pontoon dock on the Marañón — receives crude by barge from fields with no pipeline link, and ships crude onward by barge to the Iquitos refinery
Why here: The Marañón is the only practical access route in a roadless region
Risks: Repeated community blockades (2022, 2023) have halted both barge and pipeline flows for weeks at a time</description>
					<styleUrl>#default3</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-74.92190751990766,-4.719368945129132,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Allen Pumping Station (Río Negro)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Oldelval (Oleoductos del Valle) — YPF 37%, ExxonMobil 21%, Chevron 14%, Pluspetrol, Pan American Energy, Pampa Energía, Tecpetrol
Role: The central gathering and pumping hub of the Vaca Muerta export system — five Oldelval branches converge here (from Puesto Hernández, Medanito, Plaza Huincul, Challacó and Sierras Blancas), before one trunk line continues to the coast
Recent: Since March 2025 also the connection point for VMOS (Vaca Muerta Oil Sur), the new private pipeline to the deepwater port of Punta Colorada
Why it matters: Every drop of Vaca Muerta&apos;s booming shale output passes through this single station — the physical chokepoint of Argentina&apos;s oil boom
Risks: A single hub for the entire basin&apos;s export capacity; repeatedly the site of capacity expansions (Duplicar, Duplicar+, Duplicar Norte) as production outpaces pipeline space</description>
					<styleUrl>#default3</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-67.6736236093581,-38.9318828196656,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Enterprise Midland Crude Terminal</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Enterprise Products Partners
Role: One of the two pricing-basis terminals for the Argus WTI Midland assessment — the delivery point for unblended &quot;Midland-quality&quot; WTI in the heart of the Permian, near the fields where the crude is produced
Function: Gathers Permian crude from field pipelines and injects it into long-haul lines to the Gulf Coast (Houston, Corpus Christi) and north to Cushing
Pricing role: Argus proposed in 2019 to assess WTI Midland EXCLUSIVELY at this Enterprise terminal, after prices at the neighbouring Plains terminal diverged; the Enterprise terminal is the more consistently referenced of the two
Why it matters: Over 1 million b/d of prompt physical WTI trades are reported at the Midland terminals each month — roughly 50 times more pricing points than the entire Dated Brent basket, which sees about 100 cargo trades a year
Risks: Pipeline takeaway constraints; basis volatility when new lines shift flows between competing terminals</description>
					<styleUrl>#default3</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-101.98,31.9,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Plains Midland Crude Terminal</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Plains All American Pipeline
Role: The second pricing-basis terminal for WTI Midland — head of the Cactus and Cactus II pipeline systems carrying Permian crude to Corpus Christi
Pricing story: When Plains&apos; 670,000 b/d Cactus II line began service in 2019, WTI Midland at this terminal briefly traded as much as 50 cents/barrel ABOVE the Enterprise terminal — a divergence that prompted Argus to propose assessing WTI Midland at Enterprise alone. A single new pipeline startup literally split the benchmark in two for a period
Function: Field gathering plus injection into the Cactus systems for Gulf-coast export
Why it matters: Illustrates how pipeline economics directly move a physical benchmark — the same terminal-basis sensitivity that makes WTI Midland&apos;s exact delivery point a live methodology question
Risks: Basis divergence from Enterprise; takeaway-driven price swings</description>
					<styleUrl>#default3</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-102.05,31.95,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Almetyevsk–Kaleikino Junction (Druzhba head)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Transneft Prikamye
Role: The Tatarstan collection hub where crude from Romashkino and neighbouring fields enters the trunk system — widely regarded as a starting point of the Druzhba pipeline
Onward routes: West via the Samara region toward Unecha, then Belarus and central Europe
Recent: The Kaleikino pumping station was struck by drones in February, causing a large fire — the Druzhba system continued operating
Why it matters: Tatarstan&apos;s heavy, high-sulfur crude entering here is a principal reason the Urals blend is as sour as it is
Alignment: schematic — the junction and its connections are documented, the drawn corridor is indicative</description>
					<styleUrl>#default3</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>52.3,54.9,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Purpe Junction</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Transneft
Role: The Arctic collection point of the Yamal-Nenets region — the southern end of the Zapolyarye–Purpe line and the northern end of Purpe–Samotlor
Onward routes: South to Samotlor and the main trunk grid, where the crude joins either the western Urals stream or the eastern ESPO stream
Why it matters: Arctic onshore crude reaching Purpe loses its separate identity — unlike Novy Port and YK Blend, which stay distinct by exporting through their own Arctic terminals
Alignment: schematic — the junction and its connections are documented, the drawn corridor is indicative</description>
					<styleUrl>#default3</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>76.90000000000001,64.5,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Samara–Kuibyshev Junction</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Transneft
Role: The great crossroads of the western Russian system, in the Volga region — crude arriving from West Siberia, the Urals-Volga fields and Tatarstan converges here
Onward routes: THREE directions — north-west via Yaroslavl into BPS-1 for Primorsk on the Baltic; west via Unecha into Druzhba and BPS-2; south via Volgograd and Tikhoretsk to Novorossiysk on the Black Sea. Local refineries (Kuibyshev, Novokuibyshevsk, Syzran) also draw feedstock here
Why it matters: The single point that determines whether a western barrel leaves Russia by the Baltic, the Black Sea, or overland through Belarus
Alignment: schematic — the junction and its connections are documented, the drawn corridor is indicative</description>
					<styleUrl>#default3</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>50.15,53.2,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Skovorodino Junction (ESPO split)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Transneft
Role: The end of ESPO Phase 1 and the branch point of the whole eastern system — also hosts a rail loading terminal built when Phase 2 was still under construction
Onward routes: TWO choices — continue east on ESPO Phase 2 to Kozmino for seaborne export to Japan, Korea, India and China, or turn south on the Daqing spur for pipeline delivery straight into China
Why it matters: The most consequential fork in Russia&apos;s eastern exports. The Daqing route needs no ships at all, making it immune to tanker sanctions and insurance restrictions; the Kozmino route reaches more buyers but is fully exposed to maritime measures
Alignment: schematic — the junction and its connections are documented, the drawn corridor is indicative</description>
					<styleUrl>#default3</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>123.9785659455322,53.99700601238705,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Taishet Junction (ESPO head)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Transneft
Role: The head of the ESPO pipeline and the point where the western and eastern systems meet — West Siberian crude arrives here via the Omsk–Taishet line, East Siberian fields tie in nearby
Onward routes: East along ESPO toward Skovorodino, Kozmino and China
Why it matters: This is where Russia physically decides east or west. Crude that reaches Taishet and continues east becomes ESPO; crude held back in the western grid becomes Urals. Routing the sweeter barrels east is what has degraded Urals quality for European refiners
Alignment: schematic — the junction and its connections are documented, the drawn corridor is indicative</description>
					<styleUrl>#default3</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>97.99999999999999,55.93,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Tikhoretsk Junction</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Transneft
Role: The southern distribution point in Krasnodar Krai, where the trunk line from the Volga region splits toward the Black Sea coast
Onward routes: West to Novorossiysk and the Sheskharis terminal for seaborne export, and south-west to the Tuapse refinery
Connectivity: Also the point where the Baku–Novorossiysk line carrying Azeri, and more recently Turkmen, crude joins the Russian system
Why it matters: The last junction before Russia&apos;s main Black Sea outlet — everything shipped from Novorossiysk passes through here
Alignment: schematic — the junction and its connections are documented, the drawn corridor is indicative</description>
					<styleUrl>#default3</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>40.13,45.85,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Unecha Pumping &amp; Diversion Station</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Transneft
Role: The literal fork in Russia&apos;s westward crude export system — junction where the Druzhba pipeline (running overland to Belarus, Poland, Hungary, Slovakia, Czechia) meets the head of BPS-2, which instead routes crude to the Baltic coast for seaborne export
Connectivity: Renovated 2009-2011 as part of BPS-2 construction; designated NPS-1 of the new line
Recent: Built specifically to let Russia shift volumes away from Druzhba&apos;s overland transit states after the 2007 Russia-Belarus tariff dispute
Strategic role: The point where Transneft effectively chooses, barrel by barrel, whether Russian crude exits via pipeline transit through neighbouring states or by tanker from Ust-Luga — the physical manifestation of the &quot;bypass Belarus&quot; strategy behind BPS-2
Risks: A single junction concentrating the routing decision for a large share of Russia&apos;s westbound crude</description>
					<styleUrl>#default3</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>32.69947896065219,52.83495487999147,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Ust-Balyk Junction (Nefteyugansk)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Transneft Siberia
Role: The collection point for the Nefteyugansk / Priobskoye / Samotlor production area of West Siberia — where field gathering systems hand crude to the trunk network
Onward routes: Ust-Balyk–Omsk trunk line south-east to Omsk, from where crude can continue west toward the Volga and the Baltic, or east via Omsk–Taishet into ESPO
Why it matters: The first junction in the Urals chain — everything produced in Russia&apos;s largest oil province passes a point like this before it becomes part of a blend
Alignment: schematic — the junction and its connections are documented, the drawn corridor is indicative</description>
					<styleUrl>#default3</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>72.59999999999999,61.1,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Yaroslavl Junction (BPS-1 head)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Transneft
Role: Where the Ukhta–Yaroslavl trunk from Timan-Pechora meets the head of the Baltic Pipeline System, alongside the Slavneft-YANOS refinery
Onward routes: North-west on BPS-1 to the Kirishi refinery and the Primorsk export terminal; crude from the Volga crossroads at Samara also arrives here
Why it matters: The northern gateway of the western system — the last junction before Russia&apos;s largest Baltic crude outlet
Alignment: schematic — the connection is documented, the drawn corridor is indicative</description>
					<styleUrl>#default3</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>39.83,57.6,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Kinneil Oil &amp; Gas Processing Terminal</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: INEOS, which bought the terminal from BP in 2017 for $250 million, twelve years after acquiring the neighbouring Grangemouth refinery
Role: The onshore end of the Forties Pipeline System and the place where Forties Blend is actually made to specification — it separates gas, removes water, stabilises the crude and sweetens it by stripping hydrogen sulphide, making it safe for tanker shipment
Capacity: Over 1 million b/d, handling roughly 40% of total UK oil production; commissioned by BP in 1975
Connectivity: Receives crude from more than thirty offshore platforms via Cruden Bay; sends stabilised crude to the Grangemouth refinery and onward to the Dalmeny tank farm for export at Hound Point
Strategic note: With Halul Island and Malongo, one of the clearest examples on this map of a grade being manufactured at a processing point rather than pumped from a field
Risks: A single plant conditioning the entire Forties stream; any outage propagates directly to Dated Brent</description>
					<styleUrl>#default3</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-3.692830661266897,56.02074657342979,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Cruden Bay Landfall &amp; Booster Station</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: INEOS FPS
Role: The landfall of the Forties Pipeline System on the north-east Scottish coast, where the 36-inch subsea line from the Central North Sea comes ashore after 238 miles
Function: Blends the incoming crude with liquefied gas from the St Fergus gas terminal, then pumps it south to Kinneil via booster stations at Netherley, Brechin and Balbeggie
Connectivity: The junction between the offshore Forties gathering network and the onshore processing chain that ends at Hound Point
Risks: A single landfall point for a system carrying roughly 40% of UK oil production</description>
					<styleUrl>#default3</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-1.858,57.412,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Jose Tank Farms &amp; Merey Blending Complex</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Type: The commercial heart of Venezuelan oil — the tank farms where extra-heavy crude, diluent and upgraded barrels are blended into the export grades
Operator: PDVSA and JV segregations
Capacity: Millions of barrels of segregated tankage (estimate); the blending recipes here define what &quot;Venezuelan crude&quot; means to the market
Crude grades: Merey 16 made here — the flagship blend and the OPEC reference for Venezuela — plus JV-specific segregations
Role &amp; connections: Between the upgraders and the TAEJ buoys; every quality dispute, every dark-fleet cargo and every future framework barrel is mixed in these tanks
Strategic note: the Saih Nihayda / Hardisty of Venezuela — the node where a nation&apos;s geology becomes its brand
Source: Industry data; ~ estimated</description>
					<styleUrl>#default3</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-64.85090575018504,10.07093581292623,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Superior Terminal (Enbridge)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Enbridge
Role: The pivot of the Mainline system — the Wisconsin tank farm at the head of Lake Superior where Canadian crude arriving from the prairies is sorted between the system&apos;s US legs
Storage capacity: Millions of barrels of batching and break-out tankage
Connectivity: Mainline in from Hardisty/Edmonton via Gretna and Clearbrook; Line 61 out to Flanagan (the largest-capacity crude pipe in North America); Line 6A out to the Chicago area and Whiting; Line 5 east through Michigan — the politically contested Straits of Mackinac crossing
Strategic role: Where Canada&apos;s export river braids into its American channels — every downstream junction on this map (Flanagan, Patoka, Cushing) receives what Superior dispatches
Risks: System-wide single point; Line 5 shutdown litigation reshaping flows; winter operations</description>
					<styleUrl>#default3</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-92.06162531085339,46.68786930884374,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Flanagan Terminal (Enbridge)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Enbridge
Role: The Illinois junction the system turns on — a tank farm outside a village of a thousand people where Line 61 ends and two southbound choices begin
Storage capacity: Large break-out tankage for batching between systems
Connectivity: Line 61 in from Superior; Flanagan South out to Cushing (the WTI hub); the Southern Access Extension out to Patoka (the Capline corridor); a spur toward the Chicago refineries
Strategic role: The decision point of the Canadian barrel — from this field in Illinois it either becomes Cushing inventory priced against WTI or Patoka throughput bound for Louisiana; the junction is rural because that is exactly where junctions belong
Risks: Junction concentration; apportionment upstream deciding what arrives at all</description>
					<styleUrl>#default3</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-88.86,40.88,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Patoka Oil Terminal Hub</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Multiple — Marathon, Energy Transfer and partner tank farms around one Illinois village
Role: The crude crossroads of the Midwest — the junction where Bakken barrels (Dakota Access), Canadian barrels (Enbridge via the Southern Access Extension) and Cushing-system barrels (Ozark/Woodpat via Wood River) meet several million barrels of tankage
Connectivity: Two southbound exits compete for its crude — the reversed Capline to St. James, Louisiana, and Energy Transfer&apos;s ETCOP line to Nederland, Texas; local pipes feed the Robinson and Wood River refineries
Strategic role: The quiet answer to &quot;where do Canada, the Bakken and Cushing actually meet&quot; — a hub whose tanks decide whether northern crude becomes Gulf export, Louisiana refinery feed or Midwest gasoline
Risks: Junction concentration; utilisation competition between its two Gulf exits; tornado-belt weather</description>
					<styleUrl>#default3</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-89.07234831493921,38.79079123080378,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>St. James Crude Terminal Hub</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Multiple terminal operators (Plains, NuStar/Sunoco and others) around one river bend in St. James Parish
Role: Louisiana&apos;s onshore crude crossroads — the tank-farm hub where river barges, ocean tankers, Capline, LOCAP from the LOOP system and local pipelines interconnect
Benchmark connection: Light Louisiana Sweet (LLS) — the US Gulf&apos;s light sweet reference grade — is priced basis St. James, making this bend of the river a pricing point in its own right
Connectivity: LOCAP from Clovelly/LOOP; Capline northbound-turned-southbound; river docks; refinery feeds up and down the bank
Strategic role: What Cushing is to WTI, St. James is to LLS — the smaller, saltwater-connected sibling among US pricing locations
Risks: Hurricane and flood exposure; terminal concentration on one reach of river; competition from Texas export hubs</description>
					<styleUrl>#default3</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-90.85332413635959,30.01193579286889,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Saih Nihayda Blending &amp; Gathering Hub</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Petroleum Development Oman
Role: The midstream heart of the southern trunk — gathering, processing and custody point where southern crude streams are collected before joining the northern flow at the Nahada junction; project documents name it as the collection-and-mixing node of the Oman Blend&apos;s southern half
Connectivity: On the southern trunk from Marmul/Qarn Alam; ~130 km south of the Main Oil Line junction at Nahada, where southern and northern crudes physically merge for the run to Mina al-Fahal; designated tie-in of the planned Nahada–Ras Markaz export line
Recent: Also a major PDO gas hub — the Saih Nihayda gas plant processes gas and condensate from the central-Oman gas fields, alongside the Saih Rawl complex feeding Oman LNG
Strategic role: The node that decides what the southern half of a benchmark grade looks like — blend quality is made here and finalized in the Fahal tanks
Risks: Single-node concentration for southern evacuation; gas-oil facility interdependence on one site</description>
					<styleUrl>#default3</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>56.71353192278404,21.34296396673012,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Fao Storage &amp; Export Tank Farm</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Basra Oil Company / Iraqi Oil Ministry
Role: The staging tank farm of Iraqi exports on the Al-Faw peninsula — southern crude converges here before the subsea lines to the Al-Basra Oil Terminal and SPM buoys
Storage capacity: The buffer between steady field production and tanker liftings — and the system&apos;s proven pressure point
Connectivity: Trunklines from Rumaila, West Qurna, Zubair and Majnoon; subsea export lines to ABOT; the planned Basra–Haditha line would branch from this system
Recent: March 2026 — storage saturated within days of the Hormuz closure, forcing field shut-ins across the south: the textbook demonstration that storage depth, not production capacity, set Iraq&apos;s crisis output
Strategic role: Iraq&apos;s Fao is what Kuwait&apos;s Ahmadi and Saudi Abqaiq are — except with no second exit behind it
Risks: Saturation with nowhere to divert; single-peninsula concentration; naval and drone exposure at the head of the Gulf</description>
					<styleUrl>#default3</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>48.44,29.96,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Habshan Processing &amp; Gathering Complex</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: ADNOC
Role: The hinge of the UAE&apos;s onshore oil and gas system — gathering, processing and stabilisation for the Murban crude stream (Bab and the wider onshore fields) plus major gas processing trains (Habshan-Bab complex)
Connectivity: The origin of the Abu Dhabi Crude Oil Pipeline (~1.5 million b/d design) across the Hajar mountains to Fujairah — the pipe that lets Murban ignore the Strait of Hormuz
Recent: 2026 — the busiest node in the Gulf&apos;s most successful chokepoint workaround; the UAE held 82% of its 2025 export rate through the closure largely because this complex feeds a non-Hormuz outlet
Strategic role: The UAE&apos;s Abqaiq — the processing hinge every onshore barrel passes through, and the reason Fujairah works
Risks: Single-complex concentration for the Murban system; industrial and drone exposure; gas-oil interdependence on one site</description>
					<styleUrl>#default3</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>53.61610776152907,23.82631426915676,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Abqaiq Processing Complex</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Saudi Aramco
Role: The world&apos;s largest crude oil stabilisation and processing facility (~7 million b/d capacity) — Ghawar, Khurais and other fields&apos; crude is stabilised, sweetened and batched here before export
Connectivity: The hinge of the entire Saudi system — eastward to the Ras Tanura / Ju&apos;aymah terminals, westward as the origin of the Petroline to Yanbu; spheroids, stabilisation columns and tank farm on site
Recent: The 14 September 2019 drone-and-missile attack halved Saudi production (~5.7 million b/d offline) in one strike — the defining proof that processing nodes, not fields, are the system&apos;s true chokepoints; central to 2026 westbound rerouting during the Hormuz closure
Risks: Single-point concentration without equal; repeat-attack exposure; every Saudi barrel&apos;s path runs through or around this plant</description>
					<styleUrl>#default3</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>49.68335572150336,25.93157010605987,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Haoud El Hamra Gathering &amp; Pump Station</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Sonatrach TRC
Role: Central crude gathering hub of the Algerian Sahara — origin of the northbound export trunklines</description>
					<styleUrl>#default3</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>5.97306,31.88944,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
