		<Folder>
			<name>Ports &amp; Logistics Hubs</name>
			<open>1</open>
			<Folder>
				<name>Seaports &amp; energy ports</name>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Port of Algeciras</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Type: Seaport — one of Europe&apos;s busiest bunkering and transshipment hubs
Role: Sits directly on the Strait of Gibraltar, at the crossroads of shipping lanes connecting Europe, the Middle East, West Africa, the Americas and Asia. Together with Ceuta and Gibraltar, forms the SECOND-LARGEST bunkering market in Europe
Distinction from CEPSA San Roque: The neighbouring CEPSA refinery has its own dedicated port facilities — Algeciras itself is a separate, broader commercial port serving independent terminals, container traffic and bunker fuel supply for passing ships
Why it matters: A strategic chokepoint location makes this port critical to Mediterranean-Atlantic oil trade flows, similar in function to Fujairah&apos;s role outside the Strait of Hormuz
Risks: Strait of Gibraltar traffic congestion; high shipping-lane dependency</description>
					<styleUrl>#default0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-5.387894294647425,36.10326144369213,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Port of Wilhelmshaven</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Type: Seaport — Germany&apos;s ONLY deep-water oil port and its largest energy hub
Role: The entry point for crude oil into Germany — along the Jade shipping channel it has transshipment bridges for crude, LNG, mineral-oil products and coal, plus tank farms and power plants. Germany&apos;s third-largest seaport by tonnage
Crude role: Handles VLCCs (the NWO pier was built for tankers up to 250,000 dwt in 1971) — the deep draft that no other German port can offer, which is why Wilhelmshaven is the origin of the NWO crude pipeline to the Rhine-Ruhr refineries (Gelsenkirchen, Lingen, Wesseling) already on this map
Recent: Since 2022 also Germany&apos;s main LNG import gateway, with floating regasification terminals added at speed after Russian gas cutoff
Why it matters (2026 low-Rhine story): Because Wilhelmshaven feeds refineries by PIPELINE rather than Rhine barge, it is the supply route that keeps Gelsenkirchen running when the Rhine hits critical low water
Risks: A single deep-water crude gateway for much of western Germany; Jade-channel and North Sea weather exposure</description>
					<styleUrl>#default0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>8.181015756453341,53.7043678833796,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Port of Long Beach</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Type: Seaport — major container and liquid-bulk harbor complex
Role: With the adjoining Port of Los Angeles, forms the San Pedro Bay port complex — the busiest container gateway in the western hemisphere and the main crude-oil import harbor for Southern California
Crude role: Around 200 million barrels of oil a year enter through Long Beach; it currently hosts several crude oil terminals (BP on Pier T and Pier B, and a Marathon/Tesoro terminal), the crude-handling berths that feed the LA-basin refineries
Scale: ~3,200 acres, 25 miles of waterfront, ~$100 billion annual trade
Note: This is the PORT (the harbor). The specific crude-handling berths inside it are mapped separately as terminals
Risks: Ageing oil-transfer infrastructure; near capacity; the West Coast is not linked by crude pipeline to other US refining centers</description>
					<styleUrl>#default0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-118.1891639772165,33.72927669340935,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Port of Los Angeles</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Type: Seaport — the largest container port in the US, with seven liquid-bulk terminals
Role: The western half of the San Pedro Bay complex; alongside Long Beach it is Southern California&apos;s crude-oil import gateway and the outlet for the LA-basin refining system
Crude role: Hosts seven marine oil terminals under separate leases (including ExxonMobil at Berths 238-240 and Shell at Berths 167-169); the long-planned Pier 400 (Berth 408) deep-water VLCC crude terminal sits here
Scale: ~7,500 acres, 43 miles of waterfront, 25 major cargo terminals
Note: This is the PORT (the harbor). The specific crude berths inside it are mapped separately as terminals
Risks: Chronic crude-import capacity shortage; deep-draft limits forcing offshore lightering of VLCCs</description>
					<styleUrl>#default0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-118.2682110438064,33.71604992785489,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Ukrainian Danube Ports (Izmail &amp; Reni)</name>
					<description>Status: Reduced
Owner/Operator: Ukrainian port authorities — the river ports of the Danube delta&apos;s north bank, directly across from Romanian territory
Role: Ukraine&apos;s wartime lifeline — when the Black Sea ports were blockaded, Izmail and Reni became the export escape route, with cargo transferred to the Sulina channel and Constanța under NATO&apos;s shore
Handles: Grain outbound above all; fuel and supplies inbound — the products that keep the south of a country at war moving
Recent: Repeatedly struck by drones since 2023 — attacks landing within sight of Romanian (NATO) territory, with debris crossing the river; traffic continues under fire
Strategic role: The war&apos;s Danube front on this map — the reason the canal, Constanța and the whole Romanian corridor acquired strategic weight far beyond oil
Risks: Ongoing strikes; insurance and crewing under fire; escalation across a NATO border river</description>
					<styleUrl>#default0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>28.84809737560538,45.32126055418616,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Giurgiulești International Free Port (Moldova)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Moldova&apos;s only port, held via international investors and the EBRD&apos;s involvement — on the country&apos;s ~430 m of Danube frontage where Moldova, Romania and Ukraine meet
Role: The intake valve of a fuel-import-dependent nation — oil products terminal, tank farm and berths supplying a country with zero refineries of its own
Connectivity: Danube barge and river-sea vessels; road and rail into Moldova; Romanian refinery products dominate the supply
Strategic role: The end of the Romanian value chain across the border — Petromidia&apos;s and Petrobrazi&apos;s barrels become Moldova&apos;s energy security here; the map&apos;s clearest case of one nation&apos;s downstream being another&apos;s lifeline
Risks: Single-port national dependence; war next door (Ukrainian Danube ports are drone targets kilometres away); Transnistria and supply politics</description>
					<styleUrl>#default0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>28.2,45.47,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Duisburg Inland Port</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Duisburger Hafen AG (duisport) — State of North Rhine-Westphalia and City of Duisburg; fuel and chemical terminals privately run
Role: Europe&apos;s largest inland port — the pivot of the Rhine barge system at the Rhine–Ruhr confluence; refined products and chemicals, coal, ore, steel and containers; RMR tank farm at Homberg
Connectivity: The point where the deep Lower Rhine meets the shallower Middle Rhine — vessels loaded to ~1,200 t must lighten to ~460 t (300–400 t in August 2026) to pass Kaub; rail to the Ruhr and to Rotterdam/Antwerp
Recent: August 2026 — loading capacities down to about one third of normal; freight up to ~€200/tonne (previous record €130/t, August 2022); cargo shifting to rail
Strategic role: The reference point of Rhine freight economics — &quot;above Duisburg&quot; and &quot;below Duisburg&quot; are two different barge markets; the last major hub reachable at near-normal loads when Kaub bites
Risks: Low-water exposure downstream of the chokepoint; steel-industry decline in its hinterland; scarce rail capacity when barges fail</description>
					<LookAt>
						<longitude>6.748323800000017</longitude>
						<latitude>51.44992819999999</latitude>
						<altitude>0</altitude>
						<heading>-6.202081628853855e-14</heading>
						<tilt>0</tilt>
						<range>5166.487325496781</range>
