			<Folder>
				<name>STS / anchorage transfer zones</name>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Ship-to-Ship Transfer Zone, Amuay Bay</name>
					<description>Status: Active — capacity-constrained
Owner/Operator: Coordinated by Chevron-chartered logistics (AET) and other operators under PDVSA oversight
Role: The offshore transfer point where SMALL tankers loaded at Bajo Grande hand off their cargo to LARGER vessels (Aframax/Suezmax) for the onward export voyage, typically to US Gulf Coast refineries
Why this exists: Lake Maracaibo&apos;s channel to the Caribbean allows a maximum draft of only 9.8 m, capping any single loading at Bajo Grande to roughly 250,000 barrels — far short of a full cargo. A large vessel can NEVER enter the lake itself; the STS transfer in open water is the only way to assemble a full-sized export cargo
Mechanism: A small tanker departs Bajo Grande, transits the shallow lake channel, and moors alongside a larger vessel waiting in Amuay Bay/the Gulf of Venezuela; cargo is pumped across before the large vessel continues to its final destination
Recent: Named specifically in 2026 reporting as the model shipping companies (Maersk Tankers, AET) are trying to scale up as US-bound Venezuelan exports resume, alongside similar transfers off Aruba and Curaçao
Risks: A shortage of small feeder vessels and dredging neglect in the lake channel are the two chronic bottlenecks capping how much Boscán crude can actually reach export, regardless of field output</description>
					<styleUrl>#default30</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-70.44851030410835,11.74818097043418,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>T-Oil Transshipment Area</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Vast Infraestrutura (formerly Açu Petróleo), Prumo Logística; Oiltanking held a 20% stake from 2015
Role: Brazil&apos;s leading crude export terminal — the only private facility in the country able to transship VLCCs. Three berths, 1.4 km of breakwater, a 3 km access bridge, dredged to 25 m
Capacity: Licensed for 1.8 million b/d; moved 30.15 million tonnes to export in 2025, ~48% of all Brazilian crude exported via terminals
Function: Ship-to-ship transshipment — shuttle tankers from offshore FPSOs discharge directly into VLCCs and Suezmaxes, without the crude touching onshore storage
Clients: 11 operators including Petrobras, Shell, Equinor, TotalEnergies, PetroChina and PRIO; roughly two-thirds of volume goes to China
Risks: Chinese demand concentration; approaching its licensed capacity ceiling as pre-salt output grows</description>
					<styleUrl>#default30</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-40.97812085361822,-21.80951464961311,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Kola Bay Transshipment Anchorage (Murmansk)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Multiple — Gazprom Neft (Umba), LUKOIL (Kola), plus shore-based facilities
Role: The convergence point of Russia&apos;s entire Arctic oil export chain. Ice-class shuttle tankers arrive from Varandey, Arctic Gates, Prirazlomnoye and the Ob Bay terminals, discharge ship-to-ship onto stationed floating storage vessels, and the cargo is then loaded into conventional tankers for Europe or Asia
Why here: Kola Bay is ice-free year-round despite sitting above the Arctic Circle, thanks to the North Atlantic Drift — the only deep-water Russian coastline with that property
History: The 340 m Belokamenka served as the resident storage vessel for over a decade, handling more than 81 million tonnes before being replaced by the Umba in 2016
Strategic role: Converts small Arctic parcels into full-size cargoes, and in doing so also blurs cargo origin — which is why the anchorage features heavily in sanctions and shadow-fleet reporting
Risks: Environmental exposure in a confined bay beside the Northern Fleet&apos;s main bases; sanctions on transshipment; heavy vessel congestion</description>
					<styleUrl>#default30</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>33.4,69.09999999999999,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Hurd&apos;s Bank STS &amp; Bunkering Anchorage (offshore Malta)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: No operator — a shallow bank in international waters east of Malta, used as an anchorage by tankers, bunker suppliers and floating storage
Role: The Mediterranean&apos;s open-sea marketplace — ship-to-ship transfers, bunkering and cargoes waiting for orders just outside territorial jurisdiction, in the middle of the Suez–Gibraltar lane
Recent: The sanctions era made it a watchword — Russian-linked product transfers, blending and storage-afloat episodes drew enforcement attention; the third specimen of this map&apos;s STS family with Port Said and North Larak
Strategic role: Where Mediterranean cargoes change hands, identities and destinations at anchor — the informal half of the pricing zone whose formal half is Augusta
Risks: Jurisdictional grey zone by design; collision and spill exposure among anchored tankers; escalating scrutiny of exactly these waters</description>
					<styleUrl>#default30</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>14.75,35.95,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>North Larak Anchorage – STS Transfer Zone</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: No formal operator — an anchorage off Larak Island inside the strait, used by the Iranian and shadow-fleet tanker system
Role: Iran&apos;s sanctions-era transfer zone — ship-to-ship operations, floating storage and cargo identity-laundering at anchor, the Gulf counterpart of the Port Said STS zone on this map
Recent: August 2026 — ~35 tanker and cargo hulls recorded, roughly 74% transmitting no AIS (&quot;dark&quot;), including sanctioned vessels, as Iran rerouted flows around the blockaded Kharg system; Caspian Russia-to-Iran shipments nearly tripled in parallel
Strategic role: Where blocked barrels change hands — the visible signature of an export system operating outside the formal market
Risks: Collisions and spills among dark, ageing tankers; enforcement action; the anchorage is itself inside the contested strait</description>
					<styleUrl>#default30</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>56.41484311300043,26.88008478563453,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Port Said Anchorage - STS Transfer Zone</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: No single operator — a designated anchorage managed by Egyptian port and canal authorities
Role: Offshore ship-to-ship transfer zone at the Suez Canal&apos;s northern entrance — no fixed infrastructure; tankers moor side by side with fenders and pump cargo across at anchor
Recent: One of the main STS hubs of the sanctions era — naphtha loaded at Russia&apos;s Ust-Luga transferred here (and at Al Hoceima, Morocco) before onward delivery to Asia, with monthly volumes above 200,000 t; scarce ice-class tankers shuttle short runs out of Russian ports and hand cargoes to ordinary tankers mid-Mediterranean
Strategic role: Where sanctioned barrels change identity — hundreds of ships waiting for canal convoys make two tankers alongside unremarkable
Risks: Cargo origin becomes hard to trace (the point for some users, a compliance nightmare for others); ageing shadow-fleet tankers with unclear insurance — a spill could go uncompensated; growing Western scrutiny of exactly these zones</description>
