	<Folder>
		<name>C. Corridors</name>
		<Folder>
			<name>Crude Pipelines</name>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Cartagena-Puertollano Pipeline</name>
				<description>Status: Operational since 2010s — repeatedly targeted by fuel theft
Route: Repsol Cartagena Refinery (Mediterranean coast) → Repsol Puertollano Refinery (inland, Ciudad Real); 357 km
Owner/Operator: Repsol
Role: SPAIN&apos;S MAIN CRUDE OIL PIPELINE — the only route by which crude reaches Puertollano, the country&apos;s sole landlocked refinery. Built specifically to let Repsol operate Cartagena and Puertollano &quot;as if they were one&quot; integrated refining system
Why it matters: Without this line, Puertollano would have no crude supply at all — every barrel it processes first passes through Cartagena&apos;s coastal import terminal
Recent: A 2025 fuel-theft attack ruptured the line, spilling oil across 5 km2 of public woodland and causing over €4 million in damage — a rare case of a European pipeline suffering the kind of theft-driven attacks more commonly seen in Mexico or Nigeria on this map
Risks: Sole crude artery for an entire refinery; demonstrated vulnerability to theft and sabotage even in a stable European context</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
				<LineString>
					<tessellate>1</tessellate>
					<coordinates>
						-0.9329669487181957,37.57708633066845,0 -2.2,38.1,0 -3.2,38.4,0 -4.042886085789354,38.67329612676018,0 
					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Bolívar Coastal Fields–Ulé Gathering Line</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Route: Bolívar Coastal Fields (Tía Juana/Lagunillas/Bachaquero area) → Ulé Tank Farm, Tía Juana; ~27.5 km short gathering line
Owner/Operator: PDVSA
Role: Carries crude from wells across the Bolívar Coastal Fields to the Ulé Tank Farm for storage and onward pipeline shipment
Why it matters: Completes the physical link between production and gathering — crude does not reach Ulé by any other route; this line is the missing connective segment between the field and the tank farm already on this map
Export route: From Ulé, continues via the Ule-Amuay Pipeline to the Paraguaná Refining Center
Risks: A century-old producing basin&apos;s entire gathering flow concentrated on this one short corridor</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
				<LineString>
					<tessellate>1</tessellate>
					<coordinates>
						-71.55,10.2,0 -71.38217103377605,10.28287641630288,0 
					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Bolívar Coastal Fields–La Salina Gathering Line (inferred)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational — INFERRED connection, not individually named/verified
Route: Bolívar Coastal Fields (Tía Juana/Lagunillas/Bachaquero, eastern Lake Maracaibo shore) → La Salina Terminal, Cabimas; ~26 km
Owner/Operator: PDVSA
Basis for this line: NOT a specifically-named confirmed pipeline. Drawn from two supporting facts: (1) La Salina and the Bolívar Coastal Fields sub-fields sit on the same short stretch of Lake Maracaibo&apos;s eastern shore — Cabimas is a neighbouring town to Tía Juana and Lagunillas, within the same historic producing coast; (2) PDVSA&apos;s own reporting describes its western terminals as operating &quot;in integrated fashion with refineries, storage tanks and pipelines&quot; connecting these production areas, without naming each line individually
Role: Represents La Salina&apos;s stated role handling &quot;a broad range of lake crudes&quot; — consistent with a multi-source terminal fed by the surrounding coastal fields, distinct from Bajo Grande&apos;s single-dedicated-grade (Boscán) model
Why marked inferred: Unlike the Ulé link or the Boscán-Bajo Grande feeder, no specific named pipeline documentation was found for this exact route — treat this line as geographically reasonable but not independently verified
Risks: Same ageing lake-basin infrastructure affecting the whole eastern shore system; La Salina itself runs at only ~25% utilisation per PDVSA&apos;s own reporting</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
				<LineString>
					<tessellate>1</tessellate>
					<coordinates>
						-71.55,10.2,0 -71.46357356427646,10.3698221881928,0 
					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Mesa 30–Jose Diluent Feed (inferred)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational — INFERRED route, not individually named/verified
Route: Mesa Field (Oficina, Anzoátegui) → Jose Tank Farms &amp; Merey Blending Complex; ~146 km
Owner/Operator: PDVSA
Basis for this line: NOT a specifically-named confirmed pipeline. Mesa 30&apos;s own role is explicitly documented as the light-crude diluent for Orinoco extra-heavy crude, and Merey 16 (the blend product) is made specifically at the Jose Tank Farms — this line represents that necessary physical connection, inferred from function rather than a named pipeline record
Why NOT the same corridor: The Faja-Jose Corridor&apos;s own southbound leg carries refined NAPHTHA back to the wellhead for field-level dilution — a different mechanism from Mesa 30, which is a light CRUDE blended directly into Merey at the Jose tank farms, travelling north like the Orinoco crude itself rather than south as field diluent
Why it matters: Every barrel of Mesa 30 diverted here is one NOT available for domestic refining — a direct trade-off between export blending and Venezuela&apos;s chronic domestic fuel shortages
Risks: Same underinvestment and infrastructure decay affecting the whole eastern Venezuelan system</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
				<LineString>
					<tessellate>1</tessellate>
					<coordinates>
						-64.25,8.9,0 -64.55,9.5,0 -64.851,10.071,0 
					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Galeota Subsea Gathering Line</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Route: Galeota Block (Trintes Field, offshore) → Galeota Point Terminal, south-east Trinidad; ~5.5 km subsea line
Owner/Operator: Trinity Exploration &amp; Production / Heritage Petroleum
Role: The short connecting link bringing Galeota Mix ashore from the offshore Trintes field, Echo development and Foxtrot/Golf appraisal areas to the loading terminal
Why the chain is this short: Trinidad closed its only refinery (Pointe-à-Pierre, 168,000 b/d) in 2018 and pivoted entirely to raw crude export — there is no domestic refining step in this chain at all. Field, pipeline, terminal, tanker: that is the complete sequence
Export route: From Galeota Point Terminal, Galeota Mix loads directly onto tankers, priced against WTI and Dated Brent
Risks: A small, maturing offshore production base; national output has fallen to ~59,000-63,000 b/d, about half the 2006 level</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
				<LineString>
					<tessellate>1</tessellate>
					<coordinates>
						-60.95,10.12,0 -60.99548893107414,10.14264578549114,0 
					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Boscán–Bajo Grande Feeder Line</name>
				<description>Status: Operational — intermittent, tied to Chevron licence status
Route: Boscán Field, Zulia → Bajo Grande Terminal, Zulia; short feeder pipeline within the Maracaibo Basin
Owner/Operator: Petroboscán — PDVSA 60%, Chevron 40%
Role: Carries Boscán&apos;s extremely viscous, high-vanadium heavy crude the short distance from the field to its dedicated loading terminal at Bajo Grande
Why Bajo Grande specifically: A small terminal purpose-built for Boscán and asphalt-grade crude — 96,000 b/d shipping capacity, 1.345 million barrels storage — distinct from the larger La Salina terminal nearby, which handles other lake crudes and products
Technical note: Boscán&apos;s viscosity requires active vapour generation to keep it flowing at the terminal; equipment shortfalls here have repeatedly cut loading rates
Risks: Export volumes swing directly with Chevron&apos;s US OFAC licence status — suspended, reinstated, and renegotiated multiple times since 2019</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
				<LineString>
					<tessellate>1</tessellate>
					<coordinates>
						-71.88,10.28,0 -71.63800082282039,10.5042092993093,0 -71.60784855156606,10.51121835065015,0 
					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Ule-Amuay Pipeline (corrected)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Route: Ulé Tank Farm, Tía Juana, Zulia → Amuay Refinery, Carirubana, Falcón; 230 km, 26-inch diameter
Owner/Operator: PDVSA
Capacity: 23.46 million tonnes/year (roughly 470,000 b/d equivalent)
Role: The direct link between Venezuela&apos;s oldest and largest producing basin (the Bolívar Coastal Fields) and the giant Paraguaná Refining Center built specifically to process its output
Why it starts here, precisely: Ulé is a specific named tank farm at Tía Juana — the pipeline&apos;s true, documented origin, not an arbitrary point in the lake
Distinction from Boscán&apos;s route: This pipeline carries Bolívar Coastal Fields crude only; Boscán exports separately via La Salina/Bajo Grande and ship-to-ship transfer off Amuay — the two systems do not share this line
Recent: Multiple ruptures in 2020-2021 cut state output, reflecting the same maintenance collapse affecting the fields and refineries at both ends
Risks: A single 230 km line for the historic heart of Venezuelan production; age and rupture history; throughput mirrors the decline at both the field and refinery ends</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
				<LineString>
					<tessellate>1</tessellate>
					<coordinates>
						-71.38132274756209,10.28321866096709,0 -70.19426125981104,11.74134784241591,0 
					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Caño Limón–Coveñas Pipeline (OCC)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational — since 1986
Route: Caño Limón field, Arauca (Vereda La Osa, on the Venezuela border) → Banadía → Ayacucho → Coveñas, Sucre; 774-780 km, through Arauca, Boyacá, Norte de Santander, Cesar, Magdalena, Sucre and Bolívar departments, 33 municipalities, 5 pumping stations
Owner/Operator: Cenit (Ecopetrol subsidiary) since 2011; built 1986 by OxyCol (Occidental)
Capacity: 220,000-251,000 b/d — Colombia&apos;s SECOND-BIGGEST pipeline by volume, after OCENSA
Diameter: 18 inches (Caño Limón-Banadía) widening to 24 inches (Ayacucho-Coveñas)
Why it&apos;s a separate system: This pipeline has NO connection to OCENSA or the Casanare/Meta cluster — it is a fully independent corridor from a different producing region, and the two systems only meet at the shared export terminal, Coveñas
History: Over 1.5 billion barrels transported since 1986; among the most frequently attacked pipelines in the world during the FARC/ELN conflict era — a 2015 attack shut it for 67 straight days, Colombia&apos;s longest-ever outage of the line, and contributed to the country missing its 1 million b/d production target that year
Risks: Chronic guerrilla/paramilitary attack exposure; long remote corridor through conflict-affected departments</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
				<LineString>
					<tessellate>1</tessellate>
					<coordinates>
						-71.16850289736583,6.932988762727823,0 -72.46264264019985,7.036245043796077,0 -73.2,7.900000000000001,0 -73.8,9,0 -74.40000000000001,9.800000000000001,0 -75.69457540737353,9.40270989853707,0 
					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Cusiana–Monterrey Header Link</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Route: Cusiana-Cupiagua Fields → Monterrey Station, Casanare; ~37 km — the two sit in the same Casanare piedmont oil cluster
Owner/Operator: Ecopetrol / Cenit
Role: The short physical link at the HEAD of the OCENSA system. Cusiana-Cupiagua is where OCENSA&apos;s own trunk line begins; Monterrey Station, immediately next door, is where the separate ODL pipeline (carrying Rubiales/Quifa/Pirirí crude from Meta) ties into that same trunk
Why it matters: Confirms that Cusiana-Cupiagua and Monterrey are not two unrelated points on the map — they&apos;re neighbouring parts of ONE origin complex. OCENSA effectively gathers two crude streams (its own Cusiana-Cupiagua feed and the ODL/Rubiales feed) within this single 37 km cluster before heading north as one combined trunk
Risks: A concentrated origin point — any disruption here affects both the OCENSA and ODL crude streams simultaneously</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
				<LineString>
					<tessellate>1</tessellate>
					<coordinates>
						-72.70804668882953,5.002367426092581,0 -72.91336324095874,4.896236005544475,0 
					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>ODL Pipeline (Rubiales–Monterrey)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational since 2009
Route: Rubiales pumping station, Meta → Monterrey station, Casanare; 235-260 km, 24-inch diameter
Owner/Operator: Oleoducto de los Llanos Orientales (ODL) — Cenit/Ecopetrol 65%, Frontera Energy (formerly Pacific Rubiales) 35%
Capacity: 305,000 b/d, up from an initial 60,000 b/d at start-up
Role: Carries heavy diluted crude from Rubiales, Quifa and Pirirí fields directly to Monterrey, where it ties into OCENSA for the run to Coveñas
Why it matters: Built specifically because Rubiales&apos; heavy crude could not reach the coast any other way — described at the time as Colombia&apos;s largest transport infrastructure project in a decade
Risks: Affected by regional protests (2021); a single line for the whole Rubiales-Quifa-Pirirí production area</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
				<LineString>
					<tessellate>1</tessellate>
					<coordinates>
						-71.38817474174832,3.766952880847208,0 -72.09999999999999,4.1,0 -72.91326708687754,4.896144539787892,0 
					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Apiay-Porvenir Pipeline (Castilla–national grid)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational — a chronic capacity bottleneck
Route: Castilla/Chichimene fields, Meta → Apiay gathering station → Porvenir → onward to Monterrey/Vasconia, joining the national grid toward OCENSA
Owner/Operator: Ecopetrol
Role: The feeder line for Castilla Blend, Colombia&apos;s other major heavy Llanos stream (alongside Rubiales) — crude is diluted with naphtha at Apiay before moving
Why it matters: Industry literature identifies this as the MAIN BOTTLENECK in Ecopetrol&apos;s entire Llanos crude-evacuation system — insufficient dilution capacity has repeatedly capped how much Castilla crude can actually be moved
Risks: Chronic underinvestment relative to Castilla&apos;s production growth; viscosity/dilution engineering constraints limit throughput more than raw pipe capacity does</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
				<LineString>
					<tessellate>1</tessellate>
					<coordinates>
						-73.63939019334542,3.863464987911724,0 -73.56694245869484,4.081848225319145,0 -73.2,4.5,0 -72.91370346513688,4.896380097648428,0 
					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Oleoducto Norperuano (ONP) — Jungle to Bayóvar</name>
				<description>Status: Operational — but frequently shut down (sabotage, spills, community conflicts)
Route: San José de Saramuro, Loreto (Amazon jungle, on the Marañón River) → Station 5 → across the Andes at 2,390 m → the Sechura desert → Bayóvar Terminal, Piura; 854 km main line (1,106 km including the Ramal Norte branch from Andoas)
Owner/Operator: Petroperu
Role: PERU&apos;S LARGEST PIPELINE and, at the time of construction, called the most complex engineering project of its kind after the Alaska pipeline — the only route linking Amazon jungle crude (Blocks 192, 8, 67 and others) to the Pacific coast and the national refining system
Capacity: Historically moved over 100,000 b/d in its 1979-81 peak; far lower today as jungle production has declined
Chronic disruption: Repeatedly shut down for months at a time — sabotage and illegal tapping (11+ attacks recorded in one 18-month stretch), oil spills, and indigenous-community disputes over access for repairs (one 2022-23 shutdown of the Ramal Norte branch lasted over 85 days). Bayóvar&apos;s onshore tank stock exists specifically to buffer these outages
Why it matters: The physical link that makes Peru&apos;s own crude production relevant at all — without it, jungle oil could not reach any refinery or export point on the coast
Risks: Chronic sabotage and community-access disruption; a single pipeline for the entire country&apos;s domestic crude output</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
