			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Oldelval Pipeline (Vaca Muerta–Puerto Rosales)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational — repeatedly expanded (Duplicar 2024, Duplicar+ March 2025, Duplicar Norte due 2026-27)
Route: Neuquén Basin fields (Puesto Hernández, Medanito, Plaza Huincul, Challacó, Sierras Blancas) → Allen pumping station, Río Negro → Puerto Rosales, near Bahía Blanca; the Allen-Rosales trunk alone is 513 km
Owner/Operator: Oldelval — YPF 37%, ExxonMobil 21%, Chevron 14%, Pluspetrol, PAE, Pampa Energía, Tecpetrol
Capacity: Argentina&apos;s LARGEST crude pipeline system, 1,700 km of pipe; expanded to ~530,000-540,000 b/d by 2026 after the Duplicar+ project ($1.4 billion)
Role: The backbone artery of the Vaca Muerta shale boom — carries nearly all Neuquén Basin oil to Argentina&apos;s main export gateway at Puerto Rosales
Why it matters: Vaca Muerta&apos;s production has grown faster than this pipeline could expand — Oldelval has run at or near capacity for years, triggering successive expansion projects just to keep pace with drilling
Risks: Chronic capacity constraints; production growth consistently outrunning pipeline buildout</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
				<LineString>
					<tessellate>1</tessellate>
					<coordinates>
						-68.2,-38,0 -67.67323697253084,-38.93204872509881,0 -62.04974213422371,-38.92464805022301,0 
					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>VMOS Pipeline (Vaca Muerta Oil Sur, Allen–Punta Colorada)</name>
				<description>Status: Planned — targeting 2026 startup
Route: Allen, Río Negro (connecting to Oldelval and to Loma Campana via an already-operating 130 km feeder) → Punta Colorada deepwater port, Golfo San Matías, Río Negro; ~440 km
Owner/Operator: YPF-led consortium of seven partners — described as the largest private investment in Argentine history (~$2.5+ billion for this leg alone)
Capacity: Targeting 180,000 b/d by H2 2026, rising toward much higher volumes as the basin grows
Role: A NEW export route built because Puerto Rosales cannot berth the largest tankers efficiently — Punta Colorada is designed as a genuine deepwater terminal able to load VLCCs directly, unlike the shallower Bahía Blanca approach
Why it matters: Represents Argentina&apos;s bet that Vaca Muerta will need a second, larger export gateway beyond Oldelval/Puerto Rosales — diversifying away from a single export corridor
Risks: Major construction project still ramping up; cost overrun and schedule risk on a first-of-its-kind Argentine deepwater oil terminal</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
