						-67.67315468027273,-38.93349015095676,0 -65.02846323501976,-41.69516415718007,0 
					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>OCP Pipeline (Oleoducto de Crudos Pesados)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Route: Oriente Basin → parallel to SOTE for most of its length → Balao and the Punta Gorda terminal; ~480 km
Owner/Operator: OCP Ecuador consortium, a private group originally led by EnCana with Eni, Occidental, Repsol, Perenco and Perez Companc
Capacity: 450,000 b/d design, but throughput has run around 170,000 b/d for years
Cost: $1.4 billion, commissioned September 2003
Role: Carries the NAPO grade — and it was built precisely to pull heavy crude OUT of SOTE so that Oriente could recover its former 27-28° API quality
Strategic role: A pipeline built as a blend-protection measure. Ecuador chose to create a second, lower-value grade rather than let heavy barrels keep degrading its main one
Risks: Chronically underused as foreign producers&apos; expansion plans never materialised; several original shareholders have left Ecuador</description>
