						-92.13879801573002,19.06422696673802,0 -92.55,19.1,0 -92.84999999999998,18.75,0 -93.18000000000001,18.43,0 
					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Nuevo Teapa–Salina Cruz Pipeline (Trans-Isthmus)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational since 1979 (two parallel lines)
Route: Nuevo Teapa pumping station, Veracruz (Gulf coast, near Minatitlan) → Salina Cruz refinery, Oaxaca (Pacific coast); ~267 km, crossing the Isthmus of Tehuantepec
Owner/Operator: Pemex Logistica
Capacity: Two parallel lines, 240,000 b/d (30-inch) + 500,000 b/d (48-inch) — Mexico&apos;s key Gulf-to-Pacific crude corridor
Role: THE route that lets Gulf-side crude (Isthmus light and Maya heavy) reach Mexico&apos;s only Pacific refinery — without it, Salina Cruz would have no crude supply, since no oilfields sit near the Pacific coast
Why it matters for exports: Salina Cruz also exports Isthmus and Maya crude directly to the US West Coast and, at times, Asia — so this landline is what turns Gulf-side production into a Pacific export stream, avoiding the long sea route around the Yucatan and Panama
Risks: A single overland corridor for all Pacific-side supply; the origin area overlaps a dense pipeline network prone to leaks and illegal tapping</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
				<LineString>
					<tessellate>1</tessellate>
					<coordinates>
						-94.55,17.98,0 -94.84999999999999,17.3,0 -95.04999999999998,16.75,0 -95.1739,16.2116,0 
					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Nuevo Teapa–Tula Pipeline</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Route: Nuevo Teapa pumping station, Veracruz → Tula refinery, Hidalgo; overland crude trunk line supplying central Mexico
Owner/Operator: Pemex Logistica
Role: Carries Gulf-gathered crude inland to Tula, Mexico&apos;s largest-capacity refinery (340,000 b/d) and the main fuel supplier to the Mexico City metropolitan area
Why it matters: Represents the DOMESTIC-consumption side of Mexico&apos;s crude flow — this barrel is refined for Mexico City rather than exported, the counterpart to the Salina Cruz export/Pacific-refining route
Risks: A long inland corridor historically targeted by illegal tapping (&quot;huachicoleo&quot;), a chronic Pemex security and loss issue</description>
