				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Cantarell (Akal-C) – Cayo Arcas Subsea Pipeline</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Route: Akal-C platform, Cantarell Complex (Bay of Campeche) → Cayo Arcas SPM terminal, offshore Campeche; subsea pipeline
Owner/Operator: Pemex
Role: The SECOND branch of Cantarell/KMZ&apos;s offshore gathering system — separate from the Akal-C to Dos Bocas line already on this map. Cantarell crude splits between two destinations: some flows onshore to Dos Bocas for storage/refining, the rest flows to Cayo Arcas for direct tanker loading
Why it matters for exports: This is the pipeline that makes Cayo Arcas function — Mexico&apos;s single largest crude export point (12.4 million tonnes in six months in one recent period), moving Maya and Isthmus crude onto VLCCs up to 290,000 DWT that could never dock at an onshore Mexican port
Terminal note: Cayo Arcas itself is pure open water — two Single Point Mooring buoys plus an FSO (Ta&apos;Kuntah, 357,600-tonne capacity) that buffers crude during hurricane shutdowns; no storage tanks, no land, nothing visible above the sea but the buoys
Risks: Subsea pipeline integrity; the terminal closes entirely during storms (as in 2014, 2017), backing up flow along this line</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
