				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Chain leg 3 — Enbridge Line 9 (Sarnia–Montreal, via Toronto)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational — reversed to eastbound flow in stages, completed 2015
Chain position: LEG 3 of 3 — across southern Ontario to the Quebec refineries (THE FINAL LEG)
Route: Sarnia → London → Hamilton (North Westover Station) → Oakville / Mississauga → TORONTO (the line crosses the north of the city, roughly along Finch Avenue) → Oshawa → Belleville → Kingston → Cornwall → crosses the Ottawa River near Hawkesbury → Mirabel / Terrebonne → Montreal Terminal; 832 km, ~300,000 b/d
Owner/Operator: Enbridge
YES, it passes through Toronto: Line 9 runs across the northern edge of the city (Finch Avenue corridor); other cities on the route include London, Hamilton, Mississauga, Kingston and Cornwall
Sub-sections: Line 9A (Sarnia → North Westover, near Hamilton) + Line 9B (North Westover → Montreal)
Role: The final delivery leg — feeds Suncor&apos;s Montreal refinery (137,000 b/d) directly, and Valero&apos;s Jean-Gaulin refinery at Lévis (235,000 b/d) via marine vessel onward up the St. Lawrence from Montreal
IMPORTANT: Line 9 ENDS at Montreal. It is an import/refinery-supply line, NOT an export line — it does not continue to any Atlantic export port
Risks: Runs through Canada&apos;s most densely populated corridor; spill-response scrutiny</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
				<LineString>
					<tessellate>1</tessellate>
					<coordinates>
						-82.42,42.97,0 -81.23,43,0 -79.87,43.26,0 -79.55,43.65,0 -79.42,43.78,0 -78.90000000000001,43.9,0 -77.38,44.16,0 -76.48999999999999,44.23,0 -75.52,44.72,0 -74.62,45.42,0 -73.94,45.52,0 -73.524,45.636,0 
					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>NWO Pipeline (Wilhelmshaven–Gelsenkirchen)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Route: Wilhelmshaven deep-water crude terminal (North Sea) → the Ruhr-Rhine refineries, including Gelsenkirchen-Scholven and -Horst; the Nord-West Oelleitung (NWO) crude oil pipeline
Owner/Operator: Nord-West Oelleitung GmbH (NWO) — co-owned by Ruhr Oel, BP, Shell and Holborn Europa
Capacity: ~16.3 million t/yr (~106 million bbl/yr) onshore crude pipeline
Role: Carries imported crude from Wilhelmshaven — Germany&apos;s only deep-water oil port, able to take VLCCs — inland to the Rhine-Ruhr refineries; also supplies BP&apos;s Lingen refinery and Shell&apos;s Wesseling plant
Why it matters (2026 low-Rhine story): This pipeline is the reason Gelsenkirchen is insulated from the Rhine low-water crisis — it delivers crude to the Scholven tank farm by pipe rather than by river barge, unlike refineries that depend on the Rhine for crude or feedstock movement
Connectivity: From the NWO Wilhelmshaven tanker pier and 11-million-bbl tank farm, with salt-cavern storage at Wilhelmshaven and Wittmund held by Germany&apos;s EBV stockpiling agency
Risks: A single strategic corridor supplying much of western Germany&apos;s refining; the deep-water intake at Wilhelmshaven is the country&apos;s key crude entry point</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
				<LineString>
					<tessellate>1</tessellate>
					<coordinates>
