			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Western Desert–El-Hamra Crude Gathering System</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Route: Western Desert oil fields → El-Hamra terminal on the Mediterranean coast (~120 km west of Alexandria)
Owner/Operator: Western Desert Operating Petroleum Company (WEPCO), gathering crude from the region&apos;s producers — Khalda Petroleum (EGPC/Apache), Bapetco (EGPC/Capricorn/Cheiron), GPC and others
Role: The trunk gathering network that carries light sweet Western Desert Blend north from the scattered onshore fields to the coast for export — the pipeline half of the chain whose field and terminal are already on this map
Note: This is a REPRESENTATIVE trunk line — the real system is a branching gathering network from many fields converging on El-Hamra, not a single pipeline; the drawn route approximates the overall south-to-coast flow rather than an exact alignment
Capacity: Feeds a terminal now able to ship up to 1 million b/d
Risks: Multiple small fields feeding one export chain; ageing Western Desert infrastructure</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
				<LineString>
					<tessellate>1</tessellate>
					<coordinates>
						27.5,30,0 27.9,30.3,0 28.35,30.55,0 28.7,30.75,0 28.83091375286118,30.92848976405575,0 
					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Niger-Benin Crude Pipeline (Agadem-Sème)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational since May 2024
Route: Agadem Rift Basin, south-east Niger → Sème (Seme) terminal, Benin&apos;s Atlantic coast; ~1,950 km, of which ~1,275 km in Niger and ~675 km in Benin, with nine pump stations
Owner/Operator: West African Oil Pipeline (Niger &amp; Benin) Company, a CNPC subsidiary
Capacity: 90,000 b/d at start-up, ramping toward 110,000 b/d — a ~$7 billion Chinese-built megaproject
Role: The ONLY export route for landlocked Niger&apos;s crude — it turned a 20,000 b/d domestic-only producer into an exporter overnight, one of the longest crude pipelines in Africa
Why it matters: Like South Sudan and Chad, Niger&apos;s entire oil-export economy depends on one pipeline crossing a neighbour to reach the sea — and it launched amid a bitter Niger-Benin border feud after the 2023 coup, resolved only with Chinese mediation
Risks: Cross-border political disputes (border closed after the coup); militant and anti-government attacks; a single line for the whole export stream</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
				<LineString>
					<tessellate>1</tessellate>
					<coordinates>
						13.1,16.3,0 11,14,0 8,12,0 5,10,0 3.5,8,0 2.63,6.36,0 
					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Chad–Cameroon Pipeline (Doba–Kribi)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational since 2003
Route: Doba Basin fields, southern Chad → Kome-Kribi FSO, offshore Cameroon; 1,070 km, 30-inch buried line, four pump stations
Owner/Operator: COTCO (Cameroon Oil Transportation Company) / Esso-led consortium; the Chad section by TOTCO
Capacity: Design 225,000-250,000 b/d; has run 100,000-180,000 b/d
Role: The ONLY export route for landlocked Chad&apos;s crude — a $2.2-4.2 billion World Bank-backed megaproject that turned Chad into an oil exporter in 2003, a year ahead of schedule
Fee structure: Chad receives royalties metered at Kribi after a ~$10/barrel pipeline deduction — the transit country and pipeline take a large slice before Chad is paid
Why it matters: Like South Sudan, Chad&apos;s entire oil economy hinges on one pipeline crossing a neighbour to reach the sea
Risks: Single point of failure for 96% of national exports; historic World Bank disputes over revenue use; regional security</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
				<LineString>
					<tessellate>1</tessellate>
					<coordinates>
						16.85,8.300000000000001,0 15.5,8,0 14,7.5,0 13,6.499999999999999,0 11.5,5,0 10.2,3.5,0 9.800000000000001,2.9,0 
					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Petrodar Pipeline (Palogue–Bashayer, Dar Blend)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational but repeatedly disrupted by Sudan&apos;s civil war
Route: Upper Nile fields (South Sudan) → Bashayer terminal, Port Sudan, on the Red Sea; ~1,380 km, carrying Dar Blend
Owner/Operator: Petrodar / DPOC (Chinese-led)
Role: The export lifeline for Dar Blend — but it runs through the Khartoum refinery area, so fighting there since February 2024 has repeatedly cut ~100,000 b/d
Why it matters: South Sudan is landlocked and depends on oil for over 90% of government revenue, yet every barrel must cross Sudan to reach the sea — a textbook case of a producer held hostage by transit-country politics
Risks: Active war zone; ruptures, blockages and the bombed Khartoum refinery on the route</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
				<LineString>
					<tessellate>1</tessellate>
					<coordinates>
						32.5,10.5,0 32.8,13,0 32.55,15.5,0 33.5,17.5,0 37.29216810289423,19.38176859679605,0 
					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Greater Nile Oil Pipeline (Unity–Bashayer, Nile Blend)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Route: Unity State fields (South Sudan) → Bashayer terminal, Port Sudan, Red Sea; ~1,600 km, carrying Nile Blend
Owner/Operator: GNPOC / Petrodar systems (Chinese-led)
Role: The older of the two export lines and the one that kept South Sudan&apos;s exports alive — because it takes a different path from Petrodar, Nile Blend continued flowing when Dar Blend was shut by fighting
Why it matters: The existence of two separate pipelines is exactly why one grade survived and the other didn&apos;t — routing, not geology, decided which barrels reached market
Risks: Transit through Sudan; ageing infrastructure; conflict exposure</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
