					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Trans-Alaska Pipeline System (TAPS)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational since 1977
Route: Prudhoe Bay (Alaska North Slope) → Valdez Marine Terminal, on Prince William Sound — 800 miles / 1,288 km, 48-inch diameter, crossing three mountain ranges including the 4,700 ft Atigun Pass
Owner: Alyeska Pipeline Service Company — Hilcorp/Harvest 49%, ConocoPhillips 30%, ExxonMobil 21%
Capacity: 2.1 million b/d design; now averages far less (~500,000 b/d) as North Slope output has declined
Role: The ONLY route to market for Alaskan North Slope (ANS) crude — without it, there is no ANS grade and no Valdez loading. Feeds the ANS assessment already mapped at Valdez
Engineering: Runs half above ground on 78,000 supports to protect permafrost, with a zig-zag design that lets it flex in earthquakes; crude takes about 8.6 days to travel the full length
History: Built 1974-77 after the 1973 oil crisis made Prudhoe Bay economic; has carried over 17 billion barrels. Prudhoe Bay, discovered 1967, was the largest oil field ever found in North America (~9.6 billion barrels)
Why it matters: Physically isolates ANS as a West Coast grade priced off Brent — the pipeline that makes the entire US West Coast a separate crude market
Risks: Declining throughput approaching minimum operating levels; permafrost thaw; seismic exposure; a single line for an entire state&apos;s production</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
