			<Placemark>
				<name>Cactus II Pipeline (Permian–Corpus Christi)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational since 2019
Route: Plains&apos; Midland terminal → Wink South → McCamey → Corpus Christi / Ingleside, Texas; roughly 700+ km SOUTH to the Gulf coast — it does NOT pass through Cushing, which lies in the opposite direction (north, in Oklahoma)
Owner/Operator: Plains All American 70%, Enbridge 30%
Capacity: 670,000 b/d
Role: One of four dedicated Permian-to-Corpus pipelines (with Cactus I, Gray Oak and EPIC/Cactus III) that together move about 2.8 million b/d straight from the oilfields to the export coast, bypassing the inland hubs entirely
Why it matters: Corpus Christi became the top US crude export gateway precisely because of these direct lines — crude reaches tanker terminals (Ingleside, South Texas Gateway) without ever touching Cushing. Trafigura alone contracted 300,000 b/d on this line to feed its Corpus export terminal
Pricing link: Line-fill of Cactus II and EPIC in 2019 pulled so hard on Midland barrels that it moved the WTI Midland differential — the same terminal-basis sensitivity noted on the Enterprise and Plains Midland terminal entries
Risks: Permian takeaway is periodically maxed out; the four Corpus lines have repeatedly run at or above combined nameplate capacity</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
				<LineString>
					<tessellate>1</tessellate>
					<coordinates>
						-102.0367755391307,31.92636349361359,0 -102.22,31.2,0 -102.23,31.13,0 -101.3,30.2,0 -99.90000000000001,29.3,0 -98.09999999999999,28.35,0 -97.43987782336465,27.81108948855734,0 
					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Wink to Webster Pipeline (Permian–Houston–Beaumont)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational since October 2020
Route: Wink AND Midland (Permian) → Houston-area delivery points (Webster, Baytown, ECHO terminal) → connectivity onward to Texas City and Beaumont/Port Arthur; ~1,050 km / 650 miles, 36-inch diameter. Runs EAST-southeast to the Gulf refining coast — it does NOT pass through Cushing, which lies to the north in Oklahoma
Owner/Operator: Wink to Webster Pipeline LLC — a joint venture of ExxonMobil (operator), Plains All American, MPLX, Delek US, Lotus Midstream and Rattler Midstream
Capacity: Up to 1.5 million b/d of crude and condensate — the LARGEST crude pipeline by capacity leaving the Permian
Role: Feeds the Houston and Beaumont refining complex directly from the oilfields — ExxonMobil routes its own Permian production to its Baytown (560,000 b/d) and Beaumont (362,000 b/d) refineries on this line, controlling the chain from wellhead to refinery gate
Destinations on this map: Connects the Midland/Delaware fields to the Houston Ship Channel refineries, Texas City, and the Beaumont/Port Arthur cluster — the US Gulf Coast refining heartland
Why it matters: Alongside the Corpus-bound lines (Cactus II, Gray Oak, EPIC), this completes the two-directional Permian export picture — south to Corpus for tanker export, east to Houston/Beaumont for refining and Ship Channel docks
Risks: Permian takeaway periodically maxed out; quality segregation needed at origin to keep Midland-grade WTI unblended</description>
