					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Kenkiyak–Atyrau Pipeline (bidirectional)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Route: Kenkiyak pumping station, Aktobe region → T. Kassymov pumping station, Atyrau region; 448-455 km, 610 mm
Owner/Operator: MunayTas North-West Pipeline Company — KazTransOil 51%, CNPC 49%
Capacity: Commissioned 2003 at 6 million t/yr, designed to rise to 12 million t/yr
Key feature: Runs in REVERSE-AVERSE CYCLIC MODE — it can flow in either direction. In &quot;averse&quot; mode crude moves west to Atyrau for export via CPC to Novorossiysk or via Uzen–Atyrau–Samara into Transneft. In &quot;reverse&quot; mode the same crude moves east toward the Kazakhstan–China pipeline
Strategic role: Physically the switch by which Kazakhstan chooses Russia or China as its outlet. With CPC under drone attack through 2025-26, this bidirectionality is the country&apos;s most practical hedge
Risks: Modest capacity relative to CPC&apos;s 55 million t/yr; reversal is not instantaneous and requires line preparation
Alignment: schematic — endpoints and route are documented, the drawn corridor is indicative</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
				<LineString>
					<tessellate>1</tessellate>
					<coordinates>
						56.5,47.5,0 54.49999999999999,47.4,0 52.8,47.2,0 51.9,47.1,0 
					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Kenkiyak–Kumkol Pipeline</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Route: Kenkiyak, Aktobe region → Kumkol, Kyzylorda region; 794.2 km, through Aktobe, Kyzylorda, Karaganda, East Kazakhstan and Almaty regions
Owner/Operator: Kazakhstan-China Pipeline LLP — KazTransOil 50%, CNPC 50%
Capacity: 10 million t/yr at launch in 2009
Throughput: 52 million tonnes moved in its first decade — 24 million for export, 28 million to the domestic market
Role: THE integrating pipeline. It joined Kazakhstan&apos;s previously separate western and eastern systems into one national network
Why it matters: Two consequences followed. West Kazakh crude gained access to the Shymkent and Pavlodar refineries for the first time, and Kazakhstan gained a complete pipeline corridor to China that avoids Russia entirely
Alignment: schematic — endpoints and route are documented, the drawn corridor is indicative</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
				<LineString>
					<tessellate>1</tessellate>
					<coordinates>
						56.5,47.5,0 59.5,46.8,0 62.5,46.20000000000001,0 65.68000000000001,45.87,0 
					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Omsk–Pavlodar–Shymkent Pipeline</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Route: Omsk, Russia → Pavlodar → Atasu → Shymkent, southern Kazakhstan
Owner/Operator: KazTransOil (Kazakh section), connecting to the Russian Transneft network
Role: The Soviet-era line that supplies RUSSIAN crude to Kazakhstan&apos;s northern and southern refineries — the reason Pavlodar has always run on imported oil despite Kazakhstan being a major exporter
Onward routes: Also delivers Russian transit crude into the Atasu junction, where it joins the Atasu–Alashankou line to China
Strategic role: Explains Kazakhstan&apos;s export paradox — the country ships over 70 million t/yr abroad from the west while importing Russian crude in the north, because the Soviet network was built to move oil out of the region, not around it
Risks: Dependence on Russian crude supply and on Russian export policy for refineries serving Kazakh domestic demand
Alignment: schematic — endpoints and route are documented, the drawn corridor is indicative</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
