						73.37,54.99,0 74.2,53.5,0 76.97,52.28,0 73.8,49.8,0 70.5,46.50000000000001,0 69.65808372377884,42.25963641845861,0 
					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Uzen–Atyrau Pipeline (western collection leg)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Route: Uzen head pumping station, Mangystau region → Atyrau; the first leg of the 1,238 km Kazakh section of the Uzen–Atyrau–Samara system, commissioned 1978
Owner/Operator: KazTransOil
Role: Collects Mangystau production — chiefly Uzen, plus Zhetybai and Buzachi-area crude — and carries it north to Atyrau, where it can enter CPC, the Samara line, or the Atyrau refinery
Technical note: Operates in hot-pumping mode. Mangyshlak crude has a pour point up to +30°C and Buzachi crude is highly viscous with a pour point as low as −21°C, so continuous heating is required along the whole route. KazTransOil ran a 2021-26 programme optimising crude intake along the Zhetybai–Uzen–Atyrau section to manage these mixed properties
Strategic role: The origin leg of KEBCO — the grade begins its journey here
Alignment: schematic — endpoints and route are documented, the drawn corridor is indicative</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
				<LineString>
					<tessellate>1</tessellate>
					<coordinates>
						52.85,43.3,0 52.3,44.5,0 51.99999999999999,45.8,0 51.9,47.1,0 
					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Almetyevsk–Nizhny Novgorod–Yaroslavl Trunk</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Route: Kaleykino pumping station near Almetyevsk, Tatarstan → Starolikeevo near Nizhny Novgorod → Yaroslavl; the Almetyevsk–Gorky system (580 km, three parallel lines) continuing as the Gorky–Yaroslavl trunk
Owner/Operator: Transneft Prikamye and Transneft Upper Volga
Capacity: Almetyevsk–Gorky rated at 49.7 million t/yr across its three lines
Role: Carries Romashkino and Volga-Urals crude north-west to Yaroslavl, the head of the Baltic Pipeline System
Why it matters: This is the SOUTHERN feed for BPS-1. Without it, Primorsk could only be supplied by Timan-Pechora crude through Ukhta — far too little to fill a 76.5 million t/yr system. Most Urals loaded at Primorsk arrives this way
Alignment: schematic — endpoints and route cities are documented, the drawn corridor is indicative</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
				<LineString>
					<tessellate>1</tessellate>
					<coordinates>
						52.3,54.9,0 48,55.6,0 43.99,56.32999999999999,0 41.5,57.1,0 39.83,57.6,0 
					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Atyrau–Samara Pipeline</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Route: Atyrau, western Kazakhstan → Samara, Russia, where it joins Transneft&apos;s trunk network
Owner/Operator: KazTransOil (Kazakh section), connecting into Transneft
Capacity: ~17 million t/yr maximum; 8.8 million t shipped in 2024, running at roughly 70% of capacity
Strategic role: Kazakhstan&apos;s second-largest export route and the largest unused alternative to CPC — but it carries crude INTO the Russian system, where Kazakh barrels are commingled and sold as Urals rather than as a Kazakh grade
Recent: Volumes increased by 232,000 t above plan during the 2025 CPC disruption
Risks: Trades one form of Russian dependence for another; Kazakh crude loses its identity and price premium once blended into Urals</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
				<LineString>
					<tessellate>1</tessellate>
					<coordinates>
						51.9,47.1,0 51.3,48.99999999999999,0 50.60000000000001,51.2,0 50.15,53.2,0 
					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>ESPO Pipeline (Taishet–Kozmino)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Route: Taishet (Irkutsk region) → Kazachinskoye → Skovorodino (Amur region) → Khabarovsk → Kozmino on the Pacific; 4,188 km
Owner/Operator: Transneft
Capacity: Phase 1 Taishet–Skovorodino 2,757 km, since uprated toward 80 million t/yr; Phase 2 Skovorodino–Kozmino 2,046 km, opened December 2012; system target ~1.6 million b/d including the China spur
Crude grade: Creates ESPO Blend | API: ~34-35° | Sulfur: ~0.58-0.65% (light sour)
Benchmark: Priced against Dubai for Asian buyers, at a premium to Urals
Feeds: East Siberian fields (Vankor via Purpe, Verkhnechonskoye, Talakan) tie in directly; West Siberian crude arrives at the Taishet head via the Omsk–Taishet line
Branches: The China spur leaves the trunk at Skovorodino — the main line itself does NOT enter China; a further branch supplies the Komsomolsk refinery
Strategic role: The line that gave Russia a second export identity. Transneft deliberately routes low-sulfur East Siberian barrels here to hold ESPO&apos;s specification, which is the direct cause of Urals quality degrading in Europe
Risks: Single very long corridor across permafrost; Asian sales concentrated among few buyers; sanctions exposure</description>
