					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Kazakhstan–China Pipeline (Atasu–Alashankou)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Route: Atasu (Karaganda region) → Alashankou metering station, Xinjiang; 962 km
Owner/Operator: Kazakhstan-China Pipeline LLP — a joint venture of KazTransOil and CNPC
Capacity: 10 million t/yr (~200,000 b/d), with long-standing plans to double to 20 million t/yr; connects at Alashankou to the Alashankou–Dushanzi line feeding Chinese refineries
Throughput: Badly underused — 1.2 million t in 2024, planned at 1.0 million t for 2025, averaging only 85,000-86,000 t/month
Recent: In December 2025, Kashagan crude was sent east through this line for the FIRST time, after a drone strike damaged CPC&apos;s Black Sea moorings — CNPC taking ~30,000 t and Inpex ~20,000 t
Strategic role: Kazakhstan&apos;s only export corridor that avoids both Russia and the Black Sea; the eastern escape valve, tested live during the 2025-26 CPC crisis
Risks: Capacity far exceeds committed volumes; reaches only one buyer market; cannot absorb more than a fraction of CPC&apos;s 55 million t/yr</description>
