			<Placemark>
				<name>Fishkhabur-Ceyhan pipeline (Turkish leg)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Route: Fishkhabur border crossing → Silopi → Mardin → Şanlıurfa → Kahramanmaraş → Ceyhan tank farm and Yumurtalık marine terminal; ~625 km, five pumping stations; in service since 1976
Owner/Operator: BOTAŞ, the Turkish state pipeline company
Capacity: Nameplate up to 1.6 million b/d; currently running at roughly 15%, constrained entirely by upstream supply rather than the pipe
Recent: Shut March 2023 when the ICC ordered Türkiye to pay Iraq ~$1.5bn — the only major line closed by court order rather than conflict; restarted September 2025, resumed March 2026 after a pause. Türkiye terminated the 1973 agreement on 27 July 2026 with a twelve-month wind-down while a replacement framework is negotiated; flows expected to continue through expiry
Strategic role: Iraq&apos;s Hormuz hedge — its only Mediterranean outlet, sharing Ceyhan with BTC and stacking two countries&apos; contingency plans on one coastline
Risks: Treaty vacuum after July 2026 and tariff renegotiation from Turkish strength; southeastern Anatolia security; seismic exposure (the 2023 Kahramanmaraş quake suspended loadings); terminal concentration</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
				<LineString>
					<tessellate>1</tessellate>
					<coordinates>
						42.37783903410218,37.06789453394255,0 42.52946701575179,37.26087305606185,0 42.45162839955458,37.5212215767894,0 41.36021144437015,37.42149292849158,0 41.12745837019329,37.89894263782626,0 40.18873843174802,37.94393547133275,0 36.49397222636578,37.44024100413037,0 35.92589768108207,36.89758049013267,0 
					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark id="ADDMVL00000000000001">
				<name>Rostock–Schwedt Pipeline (MVL)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Route: Rostock oil port (Baltic Sea) → PCK Schwedt refinery, ~200 km
Owner/Operator: PCK Raffinerie GmbH
Capacity: Limited — historically able to load the refinery at roughly half its nameplate; a ~€400m upgrade has been pursued to raise throughput
Recent: Became Schwedt&apos;s lifeline after Germany ended Russian pipeline crude imports in January 2023
Strategic role: The substitute artery for the Druzhba supply that fed Schwedt for five decades — seaborne barrels landed at Rostock reach the Berlin fuel market through this line
Risks: Capacity bottleneck versus refinery nameplate; dependence on Baltic tanker deliveries and port logistics</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
				<LineString>
