					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark id="ADDBPS00000000000001">
				<name>Baltic Pipeline System (BPS-1)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Route: Timan-Pechora / West Siberia / Urals-Volga networks → Yaroslavl → Kirishi (KINEF refinery) → Primorsk oil terminal, Gulf of Finland; commissioned December 2001
Owner/Operator: Transneft
Capacity: Up to ~75 Mt/yr (~1.5 million b/d system); Primorsk loads ~1 million b/d of Urals plus ~300,000 b/d of diesel
Handles: Urals — Russia&apos;s flagship export grade, since 2022 shipped largely via the &quot;shadow fleet&quot; under the G7 price cap
Recent: Ukrainian long-range drones forced the first loading suspension at Primorsk in September 2025, with renewed strikes in March and May 2026; at the peak, roughly 40% of Russia&apos;s oil export capacity was assessed offline
Strategic role: Built to bypass the Baltic transit states and concentrate exports at a Russian-controlled port — the single most important outlet for seaborne Urals
Risks: Primary target of the Ukrainian deep-strike campaign; sanctions and price-cap enforcement; Gulf of Finland winter ice; single-corridor concentration</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
				<LineString>
					<tessellate>1</tessellate>
					<coordinates>
						39.87,57.63,0 38.6,58.35,0 37.9,59.10000000000001,0 36,59.35000000000001,0 34,59.45,0 32.05590868165775,59.48546610128013,0 30.9,59.85,0 29.9,60.1,0 28.72205088671118,60.33991086572199,0 
					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark id="ADDFPS00000000000001">
				<name>Forties Pipeline System (FPS)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Route: Forties Unity platform (central North Sea) → Cruden Bay landfall (169 km offshore) → Kinneil, Grangemouth (209 km onshore) → Dalmeny tank farm → Hound Point export jetty
Owner/Operator: INEOS (acquired from BP in 2017)
Capacity: Nominal ~575,000–610,000 b/d; carries liquids from ~85 UK and Norwegian fields — roughly 40% of UK North Sea oil
Handles: Stabilised Forties Blend delivered at Hound Point (a Dated Brent benchmark grade); gas liquids to Grangemouth
Strategic role: The upstream leg of the Forties chain on this map — wellhead to Hound Point loading to Rotterdam; a single-system chokepoint for a large share of UK production
Risks: Single-system concentration — outages at Kinneil or on the main line ripple directly into the Dated Brent benchmark; ageing infrastructure; declining North Sea throughput</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
				<LineString>
					<tessellate>1</tessellate>
					<coordinates>
						0.9,57.73,0 -1.855,57.418,0 -2.27,57.03,0 -2.66,56.73,0 -3.32,56.44,0 -3.7,56.02,0 -3.38,55.99,0 -3.359,56.005,0 
					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>East–West Crude Oil Pipeline - Petroline</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Route: Abqaiq processing complex (Eastern Province) → ~1,200 km west across the peninsula → Yanbu terminals and refining hub on the Red Sea; parallel 48-inch and 56-inch lines
Owner/Operator: Saudi Aramco
Capacity: ~5 million b/d nameplate (one line spent years in gas service before reconversion); reported plans for +2 million b/d; built in the early-1980s &quot;Tanker War&quot; explicitly as Hormuz insurance
Recent: Since the March 2026 Hormuz closure, the kingdom&apos;s primary export artery — Saudi Arabia held 57% of its 2025 export rate in May (UAE 82%, Iraq 8%), lifting Suez flows ~30%; after the July Houthi threat to Saudi ports, Yanbu barrels route north into SUMED — 1.8 million b/d in late July, 46% of Yanbu exports
Strategic role: The reason Saudi Arabia has options and Iraq has none — first link of the only chain (Petroline → Yanbu → SUMED → Sidi Kerir → Cape) avoiding both chokepoints
Risks: 5 million b/d ceiling versus far larger Gulf-coast production; desert pumping stations already struck by drones (2019); Yanbu now inside the Houthi threat perimeter; downstream links Aramco does not control</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
				<LineString>
					<tessellate>1</tessellate>
					<coordinates>
						38.24954936159315,23.97143160378243,0 38.50472567373777,24.15734388750042,0 49.66990672911108,25.92952433707718,0 
					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark id="0A8D0C235F3EB7ABB22C">
				<name>Adria pipeline</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Route: Omišalj terminal (Krk, Croatia) → Sisak, where it splits: north via Gola to MOL&apos;s Duna refinery (Százhalombatta), connecting there to the southern Druzhba and on to Slovnaft; east toward Serbia (NIS refineries); spurs to Slovenia, Bosnia and Rijeka
Owner/Operator: JANAF (Croatian state-controlled); commissioned ~1980 as the Yugoslav import artery
Capacity: The number at the heart of the fight — Croatia claims 11–15 Mt/yr toward Hungary; no more than ~2.2 Mt/yr has ever moved; Slovak section max ~3.68 Mt/yr
Recent: The 2026 Druzhba outage turned the dispute white-hot — MOL filed an EC complaint (4 March) alleging abusive tariffs; ~10-month independent capacity tests began 11 March (40,000 t/day by autumn, clarity expected early 2027), against the end-2027 cutoff of Russian supplies; a permanent expansion was decided
Strategic role: The designated non-Russian route for Hungary, Slovakia and Serbia — nominal on the map, unproven in the physics; the bottleneck is tariffs, contracts and trust as much as steel
Risks: Unproven sustained throughput; monopoly-pricing dispute in Brussels&apos; hands; Urals-configured refineries on the demand side; single terminal at Omišalj; sanctions-interpretation freezes</description>
				<LookAt>
					<longitude>18.8718405690427</longitude>
					<latitude>47.27185982556875</latitude>
