			<Placemark>
				<name>KMK pipeline</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Route: Karachi (Bin Qasim/Karachi coast) → Mahmoodkot, near Multan, Punjab; 870 km
Owner/Operator: Pak-Arab Pipeline Company (PAPCO) — Pak-Arab Refinery Ltd (PARCO) 51%, Shell Pakistan 26%, Pakistan State Oil 12%, TotalEnergies (ex-Caltex) 11%
Capacity: Commissioned 1981 at 2.9 million t/yr, upgraded to ~6 million t/yr (~120,000 b/d)
Role: Pakistan&apos;s main crude artery — feeds PARCO&apos;s Mid-Country Refinery at Mahmoodkot from crude landed at Karachi
Connectivity: The crude counterpart to PAPCO&apos;s own White Oil Pipeline, which runs the same corridor carrying refined product
Risks: Single-line dependence for Mid-Country Refinery&apos;s crude supply; over 40 years in service; security along the Sindh-Punjab corridor</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
				<LineString>
					<tessellate>1</tessellate>
					<coordinates>
						66.98145714315959,24.815916462872,0 67.14734743286908,24.81963772205058,0 67.73295327411115,26.47880449382054,0 68.83708338390119,28.03609185838163,0 70.40505932917557,29.21893150688989,0 70.93583539950947,30.16690227205832,0 
					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Samah/Dahra-Es Sider pipeline</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Route: Samah and Dahra fields (Sirte Basin desert) → 32-inch line north → Es Sider terminal, Libya&apos;s largest oil port (~350,000 b/d export capacity); loads the flagship Es Sider grade
Owner/Operator: Waha Oil Company — Libyan NOC 59.2%, ConocoPhillips 16.3%, TotalEnergies 16.3%, Hess 8.2% — one of the last places US majors lift Libyan equity crude
Capacity: The Waha fields can pump ~300,000 b/d (recently ~285,000); the line, not the fields, sets the ceiling — shutdowns have cut national output by ~200,000 b/d at a stroke
Recent: NOC describes the line as dilapidated — leaks, no maintenance budget, blockades (2020, 2022), emergency repair shutdowns (2021, 2022); in 2026 every outage tightens the Med light sweet pool Europe leans on
Strategic role: An onshore chokepoint — one corroded 32-inch pipe in the desert moves a fifth of a country&apos;s production
Risks: Mechanical failure as the base case; militia and PFG action at both ends; force-majeure frequency; underinvestment locked in by fiscal chaos; the Gulf of Sirte cluster can be blockaded as a unit</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
				<LineString>
					<tessellate>1</tessellate>
					<coordinates>
						19.92222383965017,28.2988436855565,0 18.35009919981376,30.63009210825657,0 18.35474831896591,30.69110911193379,0 
					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Baltic Pipeline System-2 (BPS-2)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Route: Unecha (Bryansk Oblast, Druzhba junction) → Smolensk, Tver and Novgorod oblasts → Ust-Luga, Leningrad Oblast; ~998 km trunk (simplified route — actual path follows a less direct corridor)
Owner/Operator: Transneft; Rosneftbunker holds a related terminal interest at Ust-Luga
Capacity: Built in two phases (2012, 2013) to ~38 million t/yr delivered to Ust-Luga, plus a further ~12 million t/yr diverted to the Kirishi refinery via the branch line
Recent: Built explicitly to bypass Belarus, Poland and Ukraine after the 2007 Russia-Belarus transit tariff dispute; constructed 2009-2013 at a cost of roughly $4 billion
Strategic role: Russia&apos;s second Baltic export route alongside BPS-1/Primorsk — together the two systems let Russia route the same crude either overland via Druzhba or seaborne via the Baltic, shifting flows to manage transit-country leverage
Risks: Drone-attack exposure at the Ust-Luga terminus (2026 strikes); routing decisions subject to geopolitics rather than pure economics; shares BPS-1&apos;s exposure profile</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
				<LineString>
					<coordinates>
						32.68,52.85,0 32.85,53.95,0 32.3,55.5,0 32.03,56.65000000000001,0 30.8,57.9,0 29.6,58.89999999999999,0 28.38,59.66,0 
					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>BPS-2 Kirishi Spur</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Route: Branches from the BPS-2 mainline near NPS-7 (Leningrad Oblast) to the Kirishi refinery (KINEF); ~172 km
Owner/Operator: Transneft
Role: Diverts part of BPS-2&apos;s crude inland to feed Kirishi refinery rather than continuing to Ust-Luga for direct export — ties Russia&apos;s largest north-western refinery into the same trunk system as the export terminal
Capacity: Allocated roughly 12 million t/yr of BPS-2&apos;s total throughput
Strategic role: Shows BPS-2 is not purely an export pipeline — it also functions as a domestic supply line, letting Transneft balance flows between refining (Kirishi) and export (Ust-Luga) from the same source
Risks: Shares BPS-2&apos;s exposure to attack and geopolitical routing decisions; a single spur whose failure would cut this supply route to Kirishi, though Kirishi also receives crude via the older Sever/BPS-1 corridor</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
				<LineString>
					<coordinates>
						29.6,58.89999999999999,0 32.056,59.485,0 
					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Ukhta–Yaroslavl Trunk Oil Pipeline</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Route: Ukhta (Komi Republic) → Yaroslavl, roughly 900 km (simplified here as a direct line — the real route is longer and less straight)
Owner/Operator: Transneft
Role: Carries Timan-Pechora crude, gathered via the upstream Usa-Ukhta line, into the Yaroslavl refinery and the head of BPS-1
Recent: LUKOIL noted in 2012 that this line and the upstream Usa-Ukhta segment were running at full capacity, prompting new interfield pipelines to relieve pressure on both
Strategic role: Without this line, the Baltic Pipeline System has no visible upstream source — this is what actually feeds it
Risks: Ageing Soviet-era infrastructure; sub-zero winter operating conditions; capacity constraints already flagged by operators over a decade ago</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
				<LineString>
					<coordinates>
						53.7226,63.5754,0 39.7851,57.55749999999999,0 
					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Faja–Jose Crude &amp; Diluent Corridor</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Type: The country&apos;s most important pipeline corridor — parallel lines carrying diluted extra-heavy crude ~200 km north from the Faja to the Jose complex, with naphtha diluent returning south
Operator: PDVSA and JV systems (dedicated PetroPiar/PetroCedeño lines among them)
Capacity: Sized for the upgraders&apos; intake; utilisation follows Faja output
Crude grades: Diluted extra-heavy (DCO) northbound; naphtha southbound
Role &amp; connections: The physical spine of the entire export model — no corridor, no Merey; every incremental Faja barrel under the new framework must pass here or justify new lines
Source: Industry data; corridor drawn schematically — refine on imagery</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
				<LineString>
