					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>TC Marketlink Pipeline (Cushing–Nederland)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Route: Cushing → southeast Texas → the Nederland terminal in the Port Arthur–Beaumont refining complex
Owner/Operator: TC Energy (the Gulf Coast leg of the Keystone system)
Capacity: ~700,000+ b/d
Handles: Cushing barrels — including Canadian heavy delivered by Keystone — to the largest US refining concentration
Strategic role: The second southbound valve, pairing with Seaway to connect the paper hub to Gulf physical demand; the reason Canadian crude priced at Hardisty can end up in a Port Arthur coker
Risks: Shared-system exposure with Keystone upstream; tariff competition with Seaway; Gulf Coast demand cycles</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
				<LineString>
					<tessellate>1</tessellate>
					<coordinates>
						-96.76999999999998,35.95,0 -95.90000000000001,34.5,0 -95.19999999999999,33,0 -94.59999999999999,31.5,0 -94.09999999999999,30.2,0 -93.9632313560444,29.89642404493658,0 
					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Keystone Cushing Extension (Steele City–Cushing)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Route: Steele City, Nebraska (junction of the Keystone mainline from Hardisty, Alberta) → Kansas → Cushing
Owner/Operator: TC Energy
Capacity: Part of the ~600,000+ b/d Keystone system&apos;s southern flows
Handles: Canadian heavy and synthetic crude into the hub&apos;s tanks — the northern feed of the WTI delivery point
Recent: The December 2022 Mill Creek rupture in Kansas — the largest US onshore spill in a decade — happened on this segment, cutting deliveries and briefly moving the WTI market
Strategic role: The pipe that makes Cushing a continental hub rather than a Texan one — Alberta barrels priced against Oklahoma paper
Risks: Spill history and regulatory pressure; upstream dependence on one mainline; heavy-light quality management in shared tanks</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
				<LineString>
					<tessellate>1</tessellate>
					<coordinates>
						-97.01999999999998,40.05,0 -97.2,38.8,0 -97,37.5,0 -96.90000000000001,36.6,0 -96.76999999999998,35.95,0 
					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Basin Pipeline (Permian–Wichita Falls–Cushing)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Route: Jal/Wink area in the Permian Basin → Midland → Wichita Falls → Cushing
Owner/Operator: Plains All American
Capacity: ~500,000+ b/d
Handles: West Texas Intermediate in the literal sense — Permian barrels moving to the delivery point that named them
Recent: The shale era inverted its importance: most Permian growth now flows straight to Corpus Christi and Houston, making Basin the optional route and the Cushing–Midland spread the signal of which path pays
Strategic role: The historic artery between the basin and the benchmark — WTI&apos;s own supply line
Risks: Structural competition from Permian-to-Gulf pipelines; utilisation swings; ageing infrastructure</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
				<LineString>
					<tessellate>1</tessellate>
					<coordinates>
						-102.02564382691,31.93437343996289,0 -100.5,32.8,0 -98.50000000000001,33.9,0 -97.59999999999999,35,0 -96.76999999999998,35.95,0 
					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Keystone Mainline (Hardisty–Steele City)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Route: Hardisty, Alberta → east past Regina → southeast across Manitoba → entering the US in eastern North Dakota → south through the Dakotas and Nebraska → Steele City junction, where the Cushing Extension continues south and an eastern leg runs to Wood River/Patoka, Illinois
Owner/Operator: TC Energy
Capacity: ~590,000+ b/d of Canadian heavy and synthetic crude
Handles: Western Canadian Select and synthetic grades from the Hardisty blending hub toward US Midwest refineries and the Cushing system
Recent: April 2025 — rupture at Fort Ransom, North Dakota (~3,500 barrels), another entry in the system&apos;s spill record after the 2022 Kansas rupture; the Keystone XL shortcut (Hardisty–Steele City direct) was cancelled in 2021, leaving this the system&apos;s only northern leg
Strategic role: Canada&apos;s main southbound crude artery before TMX — the pipe that ties Alberta&apos;s discount barrel to Oklahoma&apos;s benchmark
Risks: Spill history and regulatory scrutiny; single-mainline dependence for the whole system; heavy-crude demand cycles at the far end</description>
				<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
				<LineString>
					<tessellate>1</tessellate>
					<coordinates>
						-111.269128572296,52.64111694635146,0 -108.2669843044553,51.45903134600698,0 -104.6,50.3,0 -101.5,49.6,0 -97.8,48.99999999999999,0 -97.5,46.8,0 -97.3,44.5,0 -97.09999999999999,42.5,0 -97.01999999999998,40.05,0 
					</coordinates>
