		<Folder>
			<name>Crude-by-Rail Corridors</name>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Crude-by-Rail Corridor (Hardisty–Sorel-Tracy)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational (spread-driven)
Route: Hardisty Rail Terminal, Alberta → across the Prairies (Regina, Winnipeg) → northern Ontario (Thunder Bay, Sudbury) → Montreal → Kildair terminal, Sorel-Tracy, Quebec; ~3,600 km on the Canadian National (CN) and Canadian Pacific (CP) main lines
Cargo: Western Canadian Select and diluted bitumen in unit trains (up to 120 railcars), railed to tidewater on the St. Lawrence for export to Europe
Why it exists: Canada has NO crude-export pipeline to its east coast (Energy East was cancelled in 2017), so oil-sands crude bound for Europe travels this rail corridor to reach a St. Lawrence tanker terminal — the physical reason the eastern export route is rail, not pipe
Landmark: The 2014 Suncor cargo — ~700,000 bbl of WCS railed to Kildair and shipped on the Aframax Minerva Gloria to a Sardinian refinery — moved along this corridor
Economics: All-in rail cost from Alberta runs in the mid-to-high teens $/bbl; the route only opens when the WCS-Brent discount is wide enough to pay for it
Risks: Cost and railcar availability; winter weather across the Prairies and Shield; a marginal, arbitrage-dependent flow</description>
				<styleUrl>#failed8</styleUrl>
