		</Folder>
		<Folder>
			<name>Product Pipelines</name>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Muscat–Sohar Product Pipeline (MSPP)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Route: Mina al-Fahal refinery → the Al Jifnain terminal (the distribution hub serving Muscat and its airport) → along the Batinah coast → Sohar refinery and port; ~290 km
Owner/Operator: OQ (with the Al Jifnain terminal as the central distribution node)
Handles: Gasoline, diesel and jet fuel between the country&apos;s two northern refineries; jet supply toward Muscat International Airport
Strategic role: Connects Oman&apos;s two refining centres into one products system and cut thousands of tanker-truck movements off the coastal highway
Risks: Single products corridor for the capital region; coastal infrastructure exposure</description>
				<styleUrl>#m_ylw-pushpin6</styleUrl>
				<LineString>
					<tessellate>1</tessellate>
					<coordinates>
						58.5,23.63,0 58.1,23.55,0 57.89,23.68,0 57.44,23.85,0 57.25430385774065,23.89857247634136,0 57.10593266527156,23.96168935432249,0 56.85319846251176,24.1886756168036,0 56.62411109356334,24.46072202805168,0 
					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>MTT-WOP (Machike–Thallian–Taru Jabba White Oil Pipeline)</name>
				<description>Status: Planned
Route: Machike (near Lahore) → Thallian → Taru Jabba (near Peshawar), alongside the motorway corridor; reported length has varied between announcements, roughly 435-477 km
Owner/Operator: Consortium led by Pakistan&apos;s Frontier Works Organisation (FWO), with Pakistan State Oil (PSO), PARCO/PAPCO and Inter-State Gas Systems (ISGS); Azerbaijan&apos;s SOCAR has taken a stake
Capacity: Initial 6-7 million t/yr, expandable to 10 million t/yr
Role: Extends Pakistan&apos;s white-oil backbone north from Machike to Peshawar, with connectivity options to the Attock refinery, Chakpirana and Faqirabad — completing a continuous Karachi-to-Peshawar products corridor
Recent: Consortium agreement signed September 2024 after a February 2024 MoU, backed by Pakistan&apos;s Special Investment Facilitation Council (SIFC); construction not yet confirmed complete as of the most recent reporting
Risks: Project-execution and financing risk typical of large state-backed infrastructure; the Karachi-Peshawar corridor still depends on the existing WOP/MFM network until this is commissioned</description>
				<styleUrl>#m_ylw-pushpin6</styleUrl>
				<LineString>
					<tessellate>1</tessellate>
					<coordinates>
						73.98999999999999,31.65,0 73.65000000000001,31.85,0 73.27,31.9,0 73,32.2,0 72.84999999999999,32.5,0 72.7,32.78,0 72.67,32.93,0 72.75,33.15,0 72.87,33.4,0 72.64,33.57,0 72.5,33.8,0 72.25,33.87,0 71.98,34.01,0 71.76000000000001,33.99,0 
					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>White Oil Pipeline (WOP)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Route: Karachi (Bin Qasim/Fauji Oil Terminal) → Mahmoodkot, near Multan; 786-817 km, 26-inch line
Owner/Operator: Pak-Arab Pipeline Company (PAPCO) — PARCO 51%, Shell Pakistan 26%, Pakistan State Oil 12%, TotalEnergies (ex-Caltex) 11%
Capacity: Commissioned mid-2000s at an initial ~5 million t/yr, expandable to ~12 million t/yr (a ~$480 million project)
Role: Pakistan&apos;s main refined-product artery — moves diesel, and increasingly gasoline, from Karachi&apos;s coastal refineries and import terminals to the interior, feeding onward into the MFM and (once complete) MTT-WOP networks; accounts for a large share of national diesel consumption
Risks: Single-corridor dependence for interior fuel supply; single-to-multi-product conversion complexity; security along the Sindh-Punjab route</description>
				<styleUrl>#m_ylw-pushpin6</styleUrl>
				<LineString>
					<tessellate>1</tessellate>
					<coordinates>
						67.348,24.78,0 67.65000000000001,24.92,0 68.28,25.43,0 68.05,25.9,0 67.87,26.35,0 67.78,26.73,0 67.95,27.15,0 68.20999999999999,27.56,0 68.64,27.96,0 69.09999999999999,28.3,0 69.58,28.65,0 70.09999999999999,28.95,0 70.33,29.1,0 70.44,29.29,0 70.63,30.05,0 70.937,30.169,0 
					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>KPLP (Korangi–Port Qasim Link Pipeline)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Route: Korangi (Karachi) to Port Qasim, Pakistan — a short link pipeline bypassing road transport between the city&apos;s refining/storage cluster and the Port Qasim terminal complex
Owner/Operator: Pak-Arab Pipeline Company (PAPCO), part of the PARCO pipeline network
Role: Connects oil-carrying vessels and Port Qasim&apos;s terminal facilities directly to the Korangi tank farm, avoiding the more congested Keamari Port route
Strategic role: A short but structurally important link that eases bottlenecks and cuts logistics costs across Karachi&apos;s crowded oil-handling zone
Risks: Concentrated within Karachi&apos;s dense industrial/port zone — exposed to the same urban congestion and security issues it was built to route around</description>
				<styleUrl>#m_ylw-pushpin6</styleUrl>
				<LineString>
					<tessellate>1</tessellate>
					<coordinates>
						67.16,24.82,0 67.23999999999999,24.805,0 67.3,24.79,0 67.348,24.78,0 
					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>MFM (Mahmoodkot–Faisalabad–Machike Pipeline)</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Route: Mahmoodkot (near Multan) → Faisalabad → Machike, near Lahore, Punjab; 362 km
Owner/Operator: PARCO / Pak-Arab Pipeline Company (PAPCO)
Capacity: Commissioned 1997, designed for ~3.7 million t/yr
Role: Extends Pakistan&apos;s white-oil backbone from the Mahmoodkot hub (fed by the KMK crude line and WOP products line) onward to Faisalabad and Machike — the current northern terminus of the continuous products corridor from Karachi, until the MTT-WOP extension to Peshawar is completed
Risks: Single-line dependence for products reaching central Punjab; nearly 30 years in service</description>
