					</coordinates>
				</LineString>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark id="089231CDAD3ED2D9E710">
				<name>Mozambique–South Africa Petroleum Products Pipeline</name>
				<description>Status: Long delayed / unconfirmed — shown here as planned infrastructure; some trackers list the project as cancelled and there is no confirmation it was ever built
Route (as proposed): Matola fuel terminal (Maputo, Mozambique) → Nelspruit, Mpumalanga → Kendal, Mpumalanga, South Africa; a ~450-500 km multiproduct petrol/diesel line
Owner/Operator: Petroline consortium — Mozambique&apos;s state fuel company Petromoc (40%), Gigajoule International, Companhia de Desenvolvimento de Petroleo de Moçambique and the WOESA Consortium, with UK&apos;s Petrofac having taken a stake at one point
Capacity (as proposed): ~3.5-6 million m³/yr (~100,000 b/d)
Recent: Announced 2007 for a 2009-2011 start; construction was repeatedly delayed by a tariff dispute between Petroline and South Africa&apos;s state-owned Transnet, then postponed in 2011; no independent confirmation of completed construction has surfaced since
Strategic role (as proposed): Intended to shift Gauteng&apos;s fuel imports away from congested Durban port routes via the shorter Maputo corridor
Risks: Regulatory/tariff dispute with Transnet; the project may never have been built despite years of announcements</description>
