				<Placemark>
					<name>Danube River</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Route: Black Forest, Germany → Vienna → Bratislava → Budapest → Belgrade → the Iron Gate → Romanian/Bulgarian border → Danube Delta and the Black Sea via Sulina; ~2,850 km, Europe&apos;s second-longest river, linked to the Rhine by the Rhine-Main-Danube Canal
Handles: Refined product and crude/feedstock barge traffic serving refineries and terminals along its banks — Petrobrazi, Petromidia and Vega (Romania), Pančevo (Serbia), MOL Danube (Hungary) and Slovnaft Bratislava (Slovakia) all sit on or near the river
Connectivity: The Rhine-Main-Danube Canal links it to the Rhine barge system and the ARA hub; Ukrainian grain and product exports have increasingly moved via the Danube ports of Izmail and Reni since Black Sea shipping became contested after 2022
Strategic role: South-eastern Europe&apos;s equivalent of the Rhine corridor — the barge backbone connecting several of this map&apos;s refineries to the Black Sea and, via the canal, to north-west Europe
Risks: Seasonal low water and winter ice; a shallower, less continuously dredged system than the Rhine; heightened strategic importance (and security risk) since 2022 as a Black Sea shipping alternative</description>
