				<name>Physical Price Assessments</name>
				<Placemark>
					<name>WCS Hardisty (Physical Assessment)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Type: Physical price assessment — Canada&apos;s benchmark heavy sour crude differential
Function: Prices Western Canadian Select at the Hardisty blending hub — the reference point for the value of Canada&apos;s oil-sands export barrel
Grade: WCS, ~20.9 API, ~3.5% sulfur (heavy sour), blended at Hardisty from bitumen, in-situ crude and diluent
Mechanism: Assessed daily by Platts/Argus from real physical bids, offers and trades, quoted as a discount to the WTI Calendar Month Average (WTI CMA) — the average of WTI futures settlements over a delivery month, used because physical cargoes price for month-long delivery rather than a single day
Why the discount moves: Widens when heavy-crude refinery demand falls (planned maintenance/turnarounds), when pipeline capacity out of Alberta is maxed out (surplus crude trapped at the hub), or when oil-sands production rises faster than takeaway capacity — all three hit at once in September 2026, pushing the discount to a two-year low near -$21/b
Location differential — the same crude, three prices: WCS Hardisty (-$21.35 to WTI CMA, Sept 2026) is priced well below WCS Cushing (-$13/b) and WCS Nederland (-$12/b) — the gap is roughly the cost and risk of moving the barrel from the landlocked hub to the US refining markets
Why here: Hardisty is the origin of the Enbridge Mainline and the pricing point where trapped Canadian barrels are most exposed to oversupply — the discount is effectively a live gauge of Canada&apos;s pipeline-capacity constraint
Risks: Extreme sensitivity to Trans Mountain/Mainline apportionment and refinery-maintenance schedules; a discount that can swing over $10/b in weeks</description>
