				<Placemark>
					<name>Los Angeles CARBOB Assessment</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Type: Physical price assessment — the US West Coast gasoline benchmark
Function: Prices California&apos;s unique boutique gasoline blendstock in the Los Angeles market — the demand-side product benchmark that complements the ANS crude assessment on the crude side
What CARBOB is: California Reformulated Blendstock for Oxygenate Blending — the gasoline component delivered before ethanol is splash-blended in, meeting California Air Resources Board (CARB) specifications that essentially cannot be sold in any other state
Mechanism: Assessed daily by Platts, Argus and OPIS for the LA (West Line) market, quoted with seasonal RVP (Reid Vapour Pressure) adjustments tied to the Kinder Morgan pipeline system — 5.99 RVP in summer, higher in winter. A separate San Francisco (North Line) assessment exists for Northern California
Why here: The Los Angeles basin — the largest California fuel market, ringed by the refineries and storage terminals already on this map
Why it matters: CARBOB is the pricing embodiment of California&apos;s isolation. Because the fuel spec is unique to the state and the West Coast has no pipeline link to Gulf Coast refineries, LA CARBOB can spike violently when a single local refinery trips — the same island dynamic that explains why the region has no mega-refinery. It is the product-market counterpart to ANS: the West Coast prices its crude off Brent and its gasoline off a spec nobody else uses
Relationship to other benchmarks: The LA 3-2-1 crack spread is built from ANS crude, LA CARBOB gasoline and CARB diesel — the standard measure of West Coast refining profitability
Risks: Extreme price volatility on refinery outages; structural gasoline-demand decline; a shrinking refinery base that thins the physical market behind the assessment</description>
