				<Placemark>
					<name>US Gulf Medium-Sour Benchmark (Mars / ASCI)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Type: Physical price assessment — the US Gulf Coast MEDIUM-SOUR benchmark (grade + index combined)
Function: Prices US Gulf of Mexico medium sour crude, and — through ASCI — sets how Gulf OPEC producers price their crude sold to the US
This entry combines two measures of ONE market:
— MARS: the physical medium-sour grade itself (29.6 API, 1.81% sulfur), arriving on the Mars pipeline from the deepwater Mars-Ursa complex, basis Clovelly, Louisiana. A traded benchmark in its own right and the most liquid of the Gulf medium-sour grades
— ASCI (Argus Sour Crude Index): the index BUILT FROM Mars plus Poseidon and Southern Green Canyon trades, added to the NYMEX Month 1 WTI settlement. ~500,000 b/d of physical volume, ~30 participants
Why merged: Mars is the anchor component of ASCI — they are the same medium-sour market measured two ways (a single grade, and the index that grade dominates), so they share one assessment circle rather than two overlapping ones
Why it matters: This is how Saudi Arabia, Iraq and Kuwait set their Official Selling Prices (OSPs) for crude exported to the US — their US-bound barrels are benchmarked against ASCI, an American index
Why here: Clovelly, Louisiana — the LOOP onshore delivery and storage hub for Mars pipeline crude
Risks: Declining Gulf of Mexico medium-sour output shrinking the physical base; competition from light-sweet AGS/WTI Houston markers</description>
