				<Placemark>
					<name>WTI Houston (MEH) Assessment</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Type: Physical price assessment — the US Gulf Coast LIGHT-SWEET export benchmark
Function: Prices Midland-quality WTI once it reaches the Houston coast, at the point where it either feeds the 4.4 million b/d local refinery network or leaves by tanker — the &quot;export-parity&quot; light sweet marker
Grade: 40-44 API, under 0.2% sulfur (light sweet), basis Magellan East Houston (MEH)
Mechanism: Assessed by Argus (Argus Houston WTI / AGS Marker) and Platts (WTI MEH) as a volume-weighted average of physical trades at the MEH terminal; published both outright and as a spread to Cushing WTI, so hedgers can convert between the coast and the NYMEX delivery point. Around 500,000 b/d of physical trade underpins it
Why here: Magellan East Houston terminal on the Houston Ship Channel, where Permian pipelines (BridgeTex, Longhorn, Wink-to-Webster) land unblended WTI in segregated tankage
Relationship to other benchmarks: The Cushing-to-Houston spread is pipeline economics made visible; WTI Houston removed the basis risk of pricing coastal barrels off the distant Cushing or Louisiana LLS markers
Risks: Hurricane/ship-channel disruption; competition among MEH, ECHO and other terminals for the pricing point</description>
