				<Placemark>
					<name>Alaskan North Slope (ANS) Assessment (Valdez)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Type: Physical price assessment — the US WEST COAST crude benchmark
Function: Prices Alaskan North Slope crude delivered to the US West Coast (PADD 5) — a market almost entirely separate from the Gulf Coast and Cushing systems, since the Rockies wall off West Coast refiners from mid-continent crude
Grade: ~31-32 API, ~1.0% sulfur (medium), the backbone of California and Pacific Northwest refining
Mechanism: Assessed by Platts and Argus, typically as a differential to Dated Brent or ICE Brent (not WTI), because West Coast refiners&apos; waterborne alternatives are Brent-priced imports — one of the few US grades that tracks Brent rather than WTI
Why here: The Valdez Marine Terminal, southern end of the Trans-Alaska Pipeline (TAPS) — already on this map — where ANS loads onto tankers for the US West Coast
Why it matters: Represents a genuinely separate US pricing region; ANS trades on Brent-linked economics while everything else on the US map trades off WTI, making it the clearest illustration of how isolated the West Coast market is
Risks: Declining North Slope output; TAPS minimum-throughput concerns; a shrinking, isolated West Coast refining base</description>
