				<Placemark>
					<name>WTI Midland (Physical Assessment)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Type: Physical price assessment — the upstream Permian light-sweet benchmark
Function: Prices West Texas light sweet crude at the wellhead-region terminals before it moves to the coast — the value that governs whether a Permian barrel is worth railing, piping or shipping onward
Grade: 40-44 API, under 0.2% sulfur (light sweet)
Mechanism: Assessed by Argus and Platts from physical spot trades at Midland-area terminals. WTI Midland is the &quot;upstream benchmark&quot; — its barrels feed onward to Houston, Corpus Christi and Cushing
Why it matters globally: Since 2023, WTI Midland delivered CIF Rotterdam is a deliverable component of Dated Brent — the first time a US grade entered the Brent basket. This is the physical link that ties the Permian directly to the European benchmark, and a big reason Permian export economics now move with the Brent-WTI spread
Why here: The Midland-area terminals in the heart of the Permian, upstream of the Gulf coast pricing points, already mapped on this map as the Midland and Delaware basins
Risks: Pipeline takeaway constraints from the Permian; quality-bank disputes as light Permian barrels dilute other streams</description>
					<styleUrl>#hub1010</styleUrl>
