				<Placemark>
					<name>Light Louisiana Sweet (LLS) Assessment (St. James)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational — a waning benchmark
Type: Physical price assessment — the traditional Louisiana light-sweet marker
Function: Prices light sweet crude delivered on the Louisiana Gulf Coast; historically the premium US Gulf light-sweet benchmark before the shale era reshaped the coast
Grade: 38.4 API, 0.39% sulfur (light sweet), basis St. James, Louisiana
Mechanism: Assessed by Argus and Platts from physical spot trades basis the St. James terminal hub — already on this map — where Gulf and inbound pipeline crude converge
Market position: Argus describes it as the &quot;waning light sweet benchmark&quot; — it has lost ground to WTI Houston (MEH) as the export era pushed price discovery toward the Houston Ship Channel, but it remains the reference for Louisiana refinery and export barrels
Why here: The St. James, Louisiana terminal complex, the physical delivery basis of the assessment
Risks: Eroding liquidity as WTI Houston takes share; dependent on a shrinking pool of light-sweet trade at St. James</description>
