				<Placemark>
					<name>Dated Brent (BFOET Physical Assessment)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Type: Regional physical price assessment — the world&apos;s most referenced crude benchmark, underpinning roughly two-thirds of internationally traded crude
Function: Sets the price for light sweet crude in the Atlantic Basin and serves as the reference differential for grades worldwide, from West African to Caspian streams
Mechanism: Assessed daily by S&amp;P Global Platts from real loading programmes of the BFOET basket — five North Sea crude streams: Brent, Forties, Oseberg, Ekofisk and Troll — plus, since 2023, WTI Midland delivered CIF Rotterdam. Whichever qualifying cargo is cheapest to deliver sets the assessment
What this circle encompasses: The North Sea fields and loading terminals underpinning the assessment — Brent/Ninian via Sullom Voe (Shetland), Forties via Hound Point/Dalmeny, Oseberg via Sture, Troll via Mongstad, and Ekofisk — all already mapped individually within this ring
Why widened: The original Brent field is nearly exhausted; the basket has been progressively broadened (Forties 2002, Oseberg 2007, Ekofisk 2007, Troll 2018, WTI Midland 2023) to keep enough physical liquidity behind the benchmark — the same structural fix later applied to Dubai
Risks: North Sea output decline; growing dependence on WTI Midland cargoes to maintain volume</description>
