			<Folder>
				<name>Financial Derivatives &amp; Futures</name>
				<Placemark>
					<name>NYMEX WTI Futures (CL), Cushing</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Type: Exchange-traded, physically-settled futures — the world&apos;s most-traded oil contract
Function: The global reference for crude price discovery, hedging and speculation — the &quot;WTI&quot; quoted on every financial ticker is this contract (symbol CL). Alongside ICE Brent, it anchors the entire oil derivatives market
Mechanism: Traded on NYMEX/CME; physically delivered at Cushing, Oklahoma, in 1,000-barrel lots. Deliverable grades are domestic light sweet streams meeting WTI quality. Most positions are closed before expiry, but the physical delivery obligation is what ties the screen price to real barrels at Cushing
Why here: Cushing is the contract&apos;s literal delivery point — the futures instrument and the physical storage hub coincide, which is why this circle overlaps the Cushing Major Energy Hub. This entry is the tradeable contract; the hub entry is the physical tank-farm crossroads
Historic note: In April 2020 the expiring May contract traded to MINUS $37.63/barrel — holders faced delivery into brim-full Cushing tanks with nowhere to put the oil, the first negative settlement in the contract&apos;s history and a vivid demonstration of what &quot;physically-settled&quot; really means
Risks: Landlocked Cushing basis can diverge from coastal export values; periodic questions about whether an inland delivery point still reflects a global export market</description>
