				<Placemark>
					<name>ICE Brent Crude Futures (London)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Type: Financial derivative — cash-settled futures contract (symbol B)
Function: The primary global instrument for hedging and speculating on crude prices — the &quot;Brent&quot; quoted on every financial news ticker is THIS contract, not a physical cargo. Over two-thirds of internationally traded crude prices against the Brent complex
Mechanism: Traded on ICE Futures Europe, the regulated exchange registered at 4 Bouverie Street, London EC4Y 8AX. Cash-settled against the ICE Brent Index (derived from the physical BFOE forward market); an expiring EFP delivery mechanism exists for those who take it that far, but the overwhelming majority of positions are cash-settled
Why here: 4 Bouverie Street, just off Fleet Street in the City of London, is the exchange&apos;s registered legal home — roughly 1,000 km from the Shetland and Norwegian terminals where the physical BFOET crude actually loads. This is the clearest illustration on the map of the split between a physical assessment (Dated Brent, in the North Sea) and the financial instrument built on top of it (this contract, in London)
Execution detail: Although the contract is legally a London venue, ICE&apos;s electronic matching engine — the servers that actually execute trades — sits in the United States (Chicago area), not London. So even &quot;London&quot; Brent is not physically traded in London: the legal venue and the execution hardware are on different continents
Role in the Brent-Dubai EFS: This is the BRENT LEG of the Brent-Dubai Exchange of Futures for Swaps. The EFS lets a trader convert an ICE Brent futures position into a Platts Dubai swap position (or vice versa) in one transaction — see the Inter-Benchmark Spreads entry for the mechanism
Risks: Basket dilution as North Sea output declines; competition from Murban and Shanghai INE for East-of-Suez pricing share</description>
