				<Placemark>
					<name>ICE Low Sulphur Gasoil Futures (ARA delivery)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Type: Exchange-traded futures — Europe&apos;s benchmark middle-distillate contract, physically deliverable
Function: The primary hedging and price-discovery instrument for diesel and heating oil in Europe — the middle-distillate equivalent of what Brent is for crude
Mechanism: Traded on ICE Futures Europe; physically delivered by barge into ICE-approved installations across the Amsterdam-Rotterdam-Antwerp (ARA) range. Formerly the &quot;Gasoil&quot; contract, respecified to Low Sulphur Gasoil (10ppm) to match modern European diesel specifications
Why here: This small circle marks the contract as a distinct instrument; it delivers into the wider ARA barge market shown by the larger ARA Major Energy Hub circle around it. The two circles are deliberately nested — the big ring is the hub, this smaller one is the specific tradeable contract
Relationship to other benchmarks: Traders use the gasoil crack (gasoil futures minus Brent) to hedge refining margins — one of the most-watched refining-economics spreads in the market
Risks: European diesel demand decline; competition from US ULSD (Heating Oil) futures; barge-logistics distortions during Rhine low-water events</description>
