				<Placemark>
					<name>Shanghai INE Crude Oil Futures (SC)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Type: Exchange-traded, physically-settled futures — RMB-denominated
Function: China&apos;s attempt at a third global crude benchmark alongside Brent and WTI, and a deliberate step toward yuan internationalization in commodity pricing
Mechanism: Launched March 2018 on the Shanghai International Energy Exchange (INE, a unit of the Shanghai Futures Exchange) — China&apos;s first futures contract open to foreign investors from inception. Accepted deliverable grades include a basket of Middle Eastern crudes (Oman, and historically Basra Light among others), reflecting China&apos;s actual import slate rather than domestic production
Why here: Shanghai hosts the exchange itself, but delivery is NOT concentrated at one point — it is spread across roughly 17 designated tank facilities at 11 different Chinese ports, from Hebei in the north to Hainan in the south. This circle marks the exchange&apos;s home, not a delivery point
Track record: Became the world&apos;s third most active crude futures market within months of launch; 3.4 billion barrels traded in a single month (August, one early year) versus about five times that for ICE Brent the same month. Foreign participation rose from ~7% (2018) to ~19-25% (2021) of trading volume
Risks: Liquidity still described by some traders as &quot;a Chinese market for the Chinese&quot; rather than a global one; a 2020 episode saw traders unable to find storage space to complete physical delivery, exposing capacity limits versus Cushing&apos;s much larger buffer; deliverable spot prices still influence the contract more than the reverse</description>
