				<Placemark>
					<name>Platts Dubai Swaps (Singapore OTC Market)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Type: Financial derivative (cash-settled swap) — no crude oil ever changes hands in this market
Function: Hedging and speculation on the Dubai benchmark
Mechanism: A cash-settled swap against the Platts daily Dubai assessment. S&amp;P Global&apos;s own data explicitly labels this series &quot;Dubai Swaps Spore,&quot; because it is assessed and published FROM Singapore, distinct from the Dubai physical price it tracks
Why here: This is Singapore&apos;s role as a paper/financial trading centre — separate from its role as a physical products hub (MOPS, at Jurong Island, mapped separately). The same city hosts two unrelated oil markets: real barrels at Jurong, pure price risk downtown
History: Adopted in the 1990s because Dubai was freely traded and not subject to the retroactive official-selling-price systems used by Gulf state producers
Risks: A purely financial market — its liquidity depends on the physical Dubai assessment remaining credible even as the underlying grade&apos;s own output keeps shrinking</description>
