			</Folder>
			<Folder>
				<name>Inter-Benchmark Spreads</name>
				<Placemark>
					<name>WTI–Brent Spread (Transatlantic Arbitrage)</name>
					<description>Status: Actively traded
Type: Inter-regional arbitrage spread — NOT a location-based benchmark
Function: The single most-watched crude spread in the world — comparing US (WTI) and international (Brent) crude values, and the signal that governs whether US crude is competitive to export
What this line means: No oil moves along it. It represents the PRICED RELATIONSHIP between NYMEX WTI futures (Cushing) and ICE Brent futures (London), the two contracts it links
Mechanism: Quoted simply as Brent minus WTI. When the gap is wide enough to cover freight from the US Gulf to Europe or Asia, US crude exports become profitable and cargoes flow east; when it narrows, that arbitrage window closes
How it moves: Before 2011 WTI usually traded at a small PREMIUM to Brent. The US shale boom flooded landlocked Cushing with crude it couldn&apos;t export (the export ban held until December 2015), pushing WTI to a steep DISCOUNT — at times over $25/b below Brent. Since the ban lifted and export pipelines to the Gulf were built, the spread has narrowed to roughly freight economics, typically $3-6/b
Why it matters: This spread turned the US from an isolated market into the world&apos;s swing crude exporter — it is the number that decides, every day, whether a Permian barrel is worth more in Houston or shipped to Rotterdam. It is the Atlantic-basin counterpart to the Brent-Dubai EFS that links Europe to Asia
Relationship to the map: One end sits at Cushing (WTI, NYMEX delivery), the other in London (ICE Brent) — the same two points that anchor the crude futures entries in the Financial Derivatives folder
Risks: None conventional — but a widening or collapsing WTI-Brent spread is itself a real-time signal of shifting US export economics and global supply balance</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin1</styleUrl>
					<LineString>
						<tessellate>1</tessellate>
						<coordinates>
							-96.76999999999998,35.982,0 -70,40,0 -40,48,0 -0.1022184065881737,51.5152163386639,0 
						</coordinates>
					</LineString>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Brent–Dubai EFS (Exchange of Futures for Swaps)</name>
					<description>Type: Inter-regional arbitrage spread — NOT a location-based benchmark
Function: Comparing Atlantic Basin and Middle East/Asia market conditions, identifying arbitrage opportunities
What this line means: No oil moves along it — it represents the priced relationship between ICE Brent futures (London) and Platts Dubai Swaps (Singapore)
Example: Recently assessed near $9.37/b
Risks: None conventional — a widening or collapsing EFS is itself a signal of shifting regional balance</description>
