				<Placemark>
					<name>Zenith Energy Los Angeles Terminals (Long Beach / Dominguez Hills / Alamitos)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Zenith Energy (acquired from Plains All American in 2019)
Role: Three interconnected independent storage terminals in Los Angeles County — Long Beach, Dominguez Hills and Alamitos — the main third-party crude and products storage system in the LA basin
Capacity: 8.2 million barrels combined, storing crude, petroleum products, chemicals and renewable fuels
Connectivity: About 50 miles of bi-directional pipeline linking the three terminals, plus direct connection to every major refinery in the region and to Line 2000, Line 63 and the M70 pipeline systems from Northern California, and to deepwater docks
Why here: Independent (fee-based, third-party) storage — rented tankage, distinct from the refinery-owned tanks; the same category as Vopak and BOSTCO
Strategic role: The commercial storage flexibility that lets traders and refiners balance supply in an isolated, import-dependent market; increasingly positioned for low-carbon/renewable fuels too
Risks: Serves a contracting refinery base; earthquake exposure in the LA basin</description>
