			<Folder>
				<name>SPR</name>
				<Placemark>
					<name>EBV Strategic Reserve — Wilhelmshaven / Wittmund Caverns (NWKG)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Erdölbevorratungsverband (EBV), Germany&apos;s national petroleum stockpiling agency, via its wholly-owned subsidiary Nord-West Kavernengesellschaft (NWKG)
Type: Strategic petroleum reserve — underground salt-cavern storage of crude oil and refined products
Role: Part of Germany&apos;s compulsory 90-day emergency oil reserve — the country&apos;s equivalent of a strategic petroleum reserve, but funded by law through mandatory contributions from every oil importer and producer rather than owned outright by the state treasury
System scale: The EBV holds ~20-22 million tonnes of crude-oil-equivalent (90 days of net imports), at least one-third as finished products, distributed across Germany so each refining region keeps at least 15 days&apos; supply on hand
This location: The NWKG salt caverns at Wilhelmshaven (Rüstringen) and nearby Wittmund (Friedeburg/Etzel) — the northern anchor of the reserve, connected to the NWO crude terminal and tank farm already on this map; any withdrawal or injection passes through the NWO tanks for measurement
How it is used: Releases must be authorized by the Federal Ministry for Economic Affairs by decree — e.g. the 2022 IEA coordinated release, and the September 2026 authorization of 30,000 m3 each of gasoline and diesel to ease the low-Rhine supply squeeze on inland refineries
Note: The EBV deliberately does not publish every reserve site for security reasons; this pin marks the main documented NWKG cavern cluster, not the reserve&apos;s full national footprint
Risks: Salt-cavern integrity; the reserve&apos;s release depends on federal authorization; concentration of northern stocks around the Wilhelmshaven hub</description>
					<styleUrl>#failed00</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>7.85,53.52,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Bayou Choctaw Strategic Petroleum Reserve</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: US Department of Energy
Role: One of the four sites of the US Strategic Petroleum Reserve — crude stored in salt-dome caverns ~20 km southwest of Baton Rouge, within pumping distance of the river refining row
Storage capacity: ~76 million barrels of the SPR&apos;s ~714 million authorised; the national reserve held ~370 million entering 2026 after the 2021–23 drawdowns, under a mandated refill programme buying below $79
Connectivity: Pipeline ties into the St. James hub and the Capline corridor — release barrels reach refineries in days
Recent: The 2022 release — the largest in SPR history — moved through sites like this one; the 2026 price spike reopened the release-vs-refill debate with the reserve at half strength
Strategic role: The emergency barrel next door to the refineries that would need it — geography chosen precisely for that
Risks: Cavern maintenance backlog; refill economics above the price cap; the policy whiplash of using reserves as market policy</description>
					<styleUrl>#failed00</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-91.30880945651951,30.31751225264369,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
