	<Folder>
		<name>E. Demand Centres</name>
		<Folder>
			<name>Petrochemical Plants</name>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Nouri Petrochemical Plant (Borzouyeh), Assaluyeh</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Owner: National Petrochemical Company 76.5%, Social Security Investment Company 17%, State Pension Fund Investment Company 6.5%
Capacity: 4.5 million t/yr of aromatics — one of the largest aromatics producers in the world, built as the &quot;Aromatic IV&quot; project at a cost of about $624 million
Feedstock: 100,000 b/d of GAS CONDENSATE, roughly 4.5 million t/yr, drawn from the South Pars gas refineries — the plant is physically connected to eleven of them, which is why it rarely has supply problems
Main products: Paraxylene, benzene, orthoxylene, raffinate, heavy aromatics, LPG and pentane across eleven process units; record output of 792,723 t of paraxylene and 476,342 t of benzene in a single year
Secondary role: Also one of six Iranian petrochemical plants that produce gasoline, at around 6 million litres a day, used to support national fuel supply
Markets: Roughly 60% exported, the rest sold domestically or passed on as feedstock to the Tondgouyan and Jam petrochemical plants
Strategic role: The downstream end of the South Pars condensate chain — the same liquid that feeds the Persian Gulf Star refinery is split here into aromatics for polyester, plastics and pharmaceuticals instead of into fuels
Risks: Sanctions restrict export markets and catalyst supply, though over 80% of catalysts and chemicals are now sourced domestically; paraxylene margins are exposed to Asian oversupply</description>
