					<Placemark>
						<name>Los Angeles International Airport</name>
						<description>Status: Operational
Role: The main gateway airport for the US West Coast and a major Pacific long-haul hub
Fuel relevance: Supplied by California&apos;s own refining capacity — a genuinely separate refining and pricing region from the US Gulf Coast, not otherwise represented on this map
Connectivity: Fed by local California refineries and marine imports via the Los Angeles/Long Beach port complex
Risks: California&apos;s shrinking refining capacity has raised import dependence for West Coast jet fuel</description>
						<styleUrl>#msn_icon?color=1976d2&amp;id=2000&amp;scale=440001</styleUrl>
						<Point>
							<coordinates>-118.4085,33.9416,0</coordinates>
						</Point>
					</Placemark>
					<Placemark>
						<name>Tokyo Haneda Airport</name>
						<description>Status: Operational
Role: Japan&apos;s primary domestic and increasingly international hub, alongside Narita
Fuel relevance: Represents North Asian jet-fuel demand, partly supplied by Japanese refineries already on this map (Idemitsu, ENEOS, Cosmo) and partly by imports, including the Sakhalin-linked crude chain
Connectivity: Tokyo Bay-area refineries and terminals feed the airport
Risks: Japan&apos;s refining capacity has consolidated in recent years, concentrating supply among fewer plants</description>
						<styleUrl>#msn_icon?color=1976d2&amp;id=2000&amp;scale=440001</styleUrl>
						<Point>
							<coordinates>139.7798,35.5494,0</coordinates>
						</Point>
					</Placemark>
					<Placemark>
						<name>Chicago O&apos;Hare International Airport</name>
						<description>Status: Operational
Role: A major US inland hub — United and American Airlines both operate large hubs here
Fuel relevance: Represents US Midwest jet-fuel demand, supplied via pipeline from the Chicago-area refineries and the Patoka/Wood River hub already on this map (BP Whiting, Phillips 66 Wood River)
Connectivity: Tied to the same Midwest pipeline network as Whiting and Wood River refineries
Risks: Landlocked, pipeline-dependent supply chain, distinct from coastal airports&apos; import options</description>
						<styleUrl>#msn_icon?color=1976d2&amp;id=2000&amp;scale=440001</styleUrl>
						<Point>
							<coordinates>-87.90730000000001,41.9742,0</coordinates>
						</Point>
					</Placemark>
					<Placemark>
						<name>Beijing Capital International Airport</name>
						<description>Status: Operational
Role: China&apos;s flagship northern hub, alongside the newer Daxing airport
Fuel relevance: Represents North China jet-fuel demand, tied to the Bohai Bay refining cluster already on this map (Dalian-area refineries and terminals)
Connectivity: Supplied via China&apos;s domestic pipeline and rail products network from Northeast China refineries
Risks: State-directed allocation of jet fuel supply; limited visibility into exact supply routes</description>
						<styleUrl>#msn_icon?color=1976d2&amp;id=2000&amp;scale=440001</styleUrl>
						<Point>
							<coordinates>116.6031,40.0799,0</coordinates>
						</Point>
					</Placemark>
					<Placemark>
						<name>Shanghai Pudong International Airport</name>
						<description>Status: Operational
Role: China&apos;s principal international gateway and a major cargo hub
Fuel relevance: Tied to the Zhejiang/Zhoushan refining and storage cluster already on this map — one of China&apos;s largest refining and crude-storage concentrations
Connectivity: Supplied via coastal shipping and pipeline from the Zhoushan-area terminals
Risks: Concentration of supply in one coastal refining cluster</description>
						<styleUrl>#msn_icon?color=1976d2&amp;id=2000&amp;scale=440001</styleUrl>
						<Point>
							<coordinates>121.8083,31.1443,0</coordinates>
						</Point>
					</Placemark>
					<Placemark>
						<name>Istanbul Airport</name>
						<description>Status: Operational
Role: Turkey&apos;s main hub and Turkish Airlines&apos; base — one of the fastest-growing long-haul transfer hubs globally
Fuel relevance: Sits at the crossroads of the Bosphorus chokepoint already on this map, through which a large share of Black Sea and Russian crude and product exports transit
Connectivity: Supplied via Turkish domestic refining (Tüpraş) and imports arriving through Bosphorus/Marmara terminals
Risks: Geopolitically sensitive location; regional conflict risk affecting Black Sea supply routes</description>
						<styleUrl>#msn_icon?color=1976d2&amp;id=2000&amp;scale=440001</styleUrl>
						<Point>
							<coordinates>28.7519,41.2753,0</coordinates>
						</Point>
					</Placemark>
					<Placemark>
						<name>Hong Kong International Airport</name>
						<description>Status: Operational
Role: One of the world&apos;s busiest cargo airports and a major long-haul passenger hub
Fuel relevance: Represents South China/regional demand, supplied largely by imports since Hong Kong has no domestic refining capacity
Connectivity: Fed by marine imports, including flows from the Singapore and Chinese refining hubs already on this map
Risks: Total import dependence for jet fuel, unlike most other major hubs on this map</description>
						<styleUrl>#msn_icon?color=1976d2&amp;id=2000&amp;scale=440001</styleUrl>
						<Point>
							<coordinates>113.9185,22.308,0</coordinates>
						</Point>
					</Placemark>
					<Placemark>
						<name>Doha Hamad International Airport</name>
						<description>Status: Operational
Role: Qatar&apos;s hub and Qatar Airways&apos; base — a major Gulf long-haul transfer point
Fuel relevance: Directly tied to Qatar&apos;s Ras Laffan industrial complex already on this map, one of the world&apos;s largest integrated gas-to-liquids and condensate processing hubs
Connectivity: Supplied via pipeline and truck from Ras Laffan and Mesaieed
Risks: Concentrated dependence on Qatari domestic supply infrastructure</description>
						<styleUrl>#msn_icon?color=1976d2&amp;id=2000&amp;scale=440001</styleUrl>
						<Point>
							<coordinates>51.60809999999999,25.2731,0</coordinates>
						</Point>
					</Placemark>
					<Placemark>
						<name>Indira Gandhi International Airport</name>
						<description>Status: Operational
Role: India&apos;s busiest airport and a fast-growing South Asian hub
Fuel relevance: Represents South Asian jet-fuel demand growth, supplied via pipeline from India&apos;s northern refining cluster
Connectivity: Fed by Indian Oil Corporation and other domestic refiners via cross-country products pipelines
Risks: Rapid demand growth outpacing some domestic pipeline capacity, forcing rail and road supplementation</description>
						<styleUrl>#msn_icon?color=1976d2&amp;id=2000&amp;scale=440001</styleUrl>
						<Point>
							<coordinates>77.09999999999999,28.5562,0</coordinates>
						</Point>
					</Placemark>
					<Placemark>
						<name>Dallas-Fort Worth International Airport</name>
						<description>Status: Operational
Role: A major US hub — American Airlines&apos; largest base — and a key inland Texas gateway
Fuel relevance: Represents US South-Central demand, supplied from the same Gulf Coast/Permian-linked refining and pipeline network as the Houston/Corpus Christi complex already on this map
Connectivity: Fed via Texas products pipelines from Gulf Coast refineries
Risks: Same Gulf Coast hurricane and refinery-outage exposure that affects the broader Texas system</description>
						<styleUrl>#msn_icon?color=1976d2&amp;id=2000&amp;scale=440001</styleUrl>
						<Point>
							<coordinates>-97.0403,32.8998,0</coordinates>
						</Point>
					</Placemark>
